var PASSAGE_BATCH={"chsl": [{"tid": "chsl-b1-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The morning walk", "text": "A morning walk is a fine way to begin the day. The air is cool, clean and fresh at that early hour. Birds sing in the trees along the quiet village road. The sun comes up slowly and colours the eastern sky. Many elderly people walk in the park near our colony. Some walk together in groups and talk in a friendly way. Others like to walk alone with their own quiet thoughts. A brisk walk makes the heart strong and the body active. It sends fresh blood to the brain and clears the mind. Doctors advise a daily walk to people of every age. It burns extra fat and keeps the body weight under control. People who walk daily seldom fall ill in the winter season. They sleep soundly at night and rise fresh every morning. A walk needs no money, no machine and no special dress. You only need a pair of shoes and a little willpower. The habit is simple to form and difficult to give up. Elderly walkers often carry a stick for balance and support.\nThere is much more to a walk than fitness of the body. Green lawns and bright flowers please the eyes and lift the spirit. Children play on the swings and their laughter fills the air. You may meet a neighbour and enjoy a short friendly chat. Such small pleasures make the whole day feel light and happy. A quiet walk also gives you time to think over problems. Many difficult questions become clear during a peaceful morning walk. Morning light also helps the body in many small ways. It sets the mood for work, study and family time. Begin with fifteen minutes and add a little every week. Within a month the habit will become a part of life. You will miss the walk on any day you skip it. So tie your shoes tomorrow and step out before breakfast. Your body and mind will thank you for this small effort."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Saving water at home", "text": "Water is a precious gift of nature and we must save it. Every drop that we waste is lost to us for ever. In many towns the taps run dry during the summer months. People stand in long queues with pots and buckets in hand. We can save a large amount of water inside our homes. The first step is to repair every tap that leaks daily. A leaking tap can waste a bucket of water overnight. We should keep the tap closed while we brush our teeth. A single mug of water is enough for that small job. A bucket bath uses far less water than a shower bath. Garden plants can drink the water left after washing vegetables. We should cover our buckets and tanks to keep water clean. Mosquitoes breed quickly in open water kept for many days. A covered tank also keeps dust and leaves out of it. Farmers in dry regions dig small ponds to catch rainwater. Such ponds fill the ground with moisture for nearby wells. In cities, tall buildings now gather rain from their roofs. This simple method gives free water for gardens and toilets.\nThe kitchen and the bathroom are the places to begin saving. Rain water can be gathered in tanks during the rainy season. It can clean the floors and water the garden for weeks. Children should learn the value of water from an early age. Teachers can explain it through stories, plays and simple examples. If every family saves a little, the whole town gains much. Wells and ponds will hold water for a longer period. Farmers will get enough water for their fields in time. Saving water is a duty that belongs to every citizen. Let us begin today instead of waiting for a dry summer. Small steps taken by many hands can bring a big change. Every bucket saved today is a bucket ready for tomorrow."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The joy of reading", "text": "Books are among the finest friends a child can ever have. A good book can carry you to distant lands and cities. You can meet kings, queens and brave soldiers of old times. You can sail across the seas and climb snowy mountains. All of this happens while you sit quietly in your room. Our school library has long rows of books on wooden shelves. There are stories, poems and books on animals, birds and stars. Every class visits the library twice a week for reading. We may choose any book that matches our age and taste. The librarian helps us find books that we will enjoy. She advises us to read a few pages every single day. Some students prefer stories about animals and their clever tricks. Others enjoy poems, riddles and tales of adventure and travel. A few senior students read about science and famous inventors. The library also keeps magazines and newspapers on a table. We must handle every book with clean hands and care. Pages should never be folded, torn or marked with ink. A book returned in good condition can serve many readers.\nReading books makes the mind sharper and quicker with every week. It adds many new words to the words we already know. It helps us write better answers and speak with confidence. A child who reads regularly performs well in class tests. Stories also teach us the difference between right and wrong. They show us how honest and kind people lived earlier. In free time, a book is a better companion than a phone. It does not strain the eyes the way a screen does. So let us make reading a daily habit from this year. One book every week is a fine target for a student. By the end of the year, you will have read plenty. Your friends will soon notice the change in you."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The bicycle", "text": "The bicycle is a simple and cheap means of travel for all. It has two wheels, a seat, a chain and two pedals. The rider moves it forward with the strength of his legs. It needs no petrol, no gas and no battery to run. So it adds no smoke or dust to the air around us. In towns and villages, bicycles are seen on every road daily. Children ride them to school with bags upon their backs. Men ride to office and women ride to the market. A bicycle can pass through narrow lanes where cars cannot enter. It is easy to park and simple to repair at home. A puncture can be mended at a small shop nearby. Most bicycles carry a bell to warn people on the road. A carrier at the back holds bags, books or vegetables. Some riders fit a small basket in front for shopping. Children learn to ride first with the help of parents. They practise in open grounds away from traffic and crowds. A helmet keeps the head safe in case of falls. Good brakes and a tight chain make every ride safer.\nCycling is also a healthy exercise for the whole body. It makes the legs strong and keeps the heart in shape. It burns extra fat and helps a person remain slim. Many cities are now building separate paths for cycle riders. More and more people prefer cycles for short daily journeys. This reduces traffic jams on crowded roads during office hours. It also saves the money spent on fuel every month. For a short trip, very few vehicles can beat the bicycle. It is gentle on nature and gentle on our pockets. Parents often gift a bicycle when a child grows older. The gift teaches balance, courage and care of things. A well kept bicycle serves a family for many years. Its parts are cheap and easy to replace when worn. Every home should keep at least one bicycle for daily use."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How vaccines protect us", "text": "Vaccines are among the most valuable gifts of modern science to human health. A vaccine trains the immune system to recognise a harmful germ before it can cause serious disease. It holds a weakened or inactive form of a virus or bacterium in small quantity. When this enters the body, the defence cells learn to identify the invader. They also prepare antibodies that stay ready in the blood for a future attack. If the real germ enters later, the body destroys it quickly, often before any symptoms appear.\nSmallpox, once a feared killer on every continent, was declared wiped out across the world in 1980. Polio has nearly vanished from India after years of pulse campaigns across the country. In India, the Universal Immunisation Programme carries vaccines free to every district hospital and village health centre. The Indian Medical Association repeats this advice in public guidance. The World Health Organization works with governments and volunteers to supply vaccines to the farthest regions of the world. Children receive their doses during their first years of life, following a fixed central schedule. Some parents fear side effects, although serious reactions are very rare in practice. Doctors point out that the diseases themselves are far more dangerous than any vaccine.\nMild fever or slight pain at the injection site often passes within a day or two. Keeping vaccination records helps schools and health volunteers track every schoolchild in a district. In recent years, scientists have produced effective vaccines at remarkable speed against new viruses throughout the world. Public trust, however, remains as important as the science behind the needle itself. When people understand the benefits clearly, coverage rises steadily and outbreaks fall throughout the country."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The Indian monsoon", "text": "The monsoon is easily the most awaited season in the yearly calendar of the country. It arrives when moist winds from the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal sweep inwards over the coast. The south-west monsoon usually reaches the Kerala coast around the first week of June annually. From the coast it advances northward, covering the entire country within about six weeks. Farmers watch the sky with hope because most farms in the country still depend on rainfall. A good monsoon fills reservoirs, recharges groundwater and promises a rich harvest of grain.\nThe season, however, is not always gentle, nor is its rain evenly spread geographically. Some districts receive far more rain than they need within a few days. Rivers swell beyond their banks and flood the low-lying villages situated along their course. Cities struggle with waterlogged roads, delayed trains and power cuts during heavy spells of rain. In contrast, parts of Rajasthan and the Deccan plateau may receive very little rainfall. The India Meteorological Department issues regular forecasts that help farmers and officials plan ahead properly.\nScientists also study ocean temperatures across the Pacific to predict the strength of the season. A weak monsoon raises food prices and strains the rural economy for many months afterwards. A strong monsoon brings relief, although it also demands better flood management from authorities. In 2023 the season ended with a deficiency that worried planners in several states. Agricultural universities now train village volunteers to read rain gauges and share their readings by phone. Such local knowledge, combined with satellite pictures, sharpens the official forecast for every district. The monsoon thus remains both a blessing and a serious challenge for the whole country."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Digital payments in daily life", "text": "Digital payments have quietly changed the way India buys, sells and saves money every day. A customer at a small tea stall can now pay the exact amount by scanning a printed code. The Unified Payments Interface, popularly known as UPI, moves money between bank accounts within seconds. It works at all hours, even on public holidays, and charges nothing for personal transfers. Street vendors, auto drivers and farmers in distant villages have adopted it with surprising speed. For many of them, an ordinary mobile phone has become both a shop counter and a cash box. In October 2024 alone, the system handled more than 1,000 crore transactions across the country. Shops display their payment codes at the counter, and customers have stopped asking for small change.\nThis change has brought clear gains for ordinary people and for the wider economy. Cash no longer needs to be carried in large amounts, which reduces the risk of theft. Every transaction leaves a digital record, helping small traders prove their income to banks. Loans that were once completely out of reach are now offered against these transaction records. The government also saves money because subsidies reach beneficiaries directly in their bank accounts.\nYet the new system carries risks that every user must understand with proper care. Fraudsters send fake messages and trick innocent people into revealing their secret PIN numbers. A single careless click can empty a savings account within minutes, cyber police units warn. Banks advise customers never to share one-time passwords with any caller on the phone. Older citizens, especially in rural areas, still need patient guidance and regular support from family. Digital payments will certainly keep growing, but trust and safety must grow alongside them."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Inside a honeybee colony", "text": "A honeybee colony works like a busy little city ruled by a single queen bee. The queen is the only bee in the hive that lays eggs for the colony. She may lay nearly 1,500 eggs on a warm day during the peak breeding season. Around her work thousands of female worker bees, and each worker has a fixed duty. Young workers clean the cells and feed the growing larvae inside the hive. Older workers fly out daily to collect nectar and pollen from flowers nearby.\nWorker bees communicate the location of good flowers through a curious dance on the comb. By moving in a figure of eight, a returning bee indicates both direction and distance accurately. Other bees watch this dance carefully and then fly straight to the rich patch. The Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch won worldwide fame for decoding this dance language of the bees. This exchange of information helps the colony gather food with remarkable efficiency throughout the day. Bees also play a far greater role in nature than simply making honey. As they move from flower to flower, they carry pollen grains on their hairy bodies.\nThis pollination allows many crops and wild plants to produce seeds and fruits properly. Scientists estimate that a substantial share of our food crops depends on such insect pollinators. Beekeeping societies in Kerala and Bihar now train rural women to keep hives and sell honey under local brand names. In recent years, bee numbers have fallen sharply because of pesticides, disease and habitat loss worldwide. Farmers and gardeners now plant more flowering plants and avoid harmful sprays during bloom. The Indian government also promotes beekeeping as a useful source of extra rural income. Protecting the honeybee, in the end, also protects our own food supply."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Groundwater depletion in northern India", "text": "Across the alluvial plains of Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh, groundwater extraction now runs far ahead of the recharge that monsoon rainfall and canal seepage provide. Measurements from NASA satellites showed the region losing groundwater equal to 4.0 centimetres each year between 2002 and 2008. These rank among the steepest declines on record. The Central Ground Water Board has classed most development blocks of Punjab as over-exploited, with agricultural extraction in several districts standing near 160 per cent of the annual recharge by 2022.\nThe driving force is the paddy and wheat rotation, held in place by free electricity, assured procurement and guaranteed prices that reward the thirstiest cropping pattern. A hectare of transplanted rice can consume 15 lakh litres of irrigation water before harvest, most of it pumped from borewells that farming households deepen by roughly a metre every season. As shallow wells fail, indebted households borrow at heavy interest to sink tube wells beyond 60 metres.\nDeeper aquifers increasingly carry fluoride, salinity and uranium above safe limits, so a crisis of quantity is steadily becoming a crisis of health across Bathinda, Mansa and Sangrur. Punjab answered in 2009 with a law that delays transplanting until the rains approach, while Haryana pays cultivators 7,000 rupees an acre to shift their cropping towards maize, pulses and oilseeds.\nDrip irrigation, direct seeding and groundwater mapping under the Atal Bhujal Yojana all seek to cut agricultural demand without cutting the livelihoods of small cultivators. Researchers at the International Water Management Institute warn that metering, procurement reform and crop diversification must advance together throughout twenty years before the decline can be reversed."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The Indian postal network through history", "text": "When the East India Company formalised its dak arrangements in 1774, nobody foresaw that the service would administer the largest postal network in the world. The Post Office Act of 1854 unified postage rates across the subcontinent and introduced the adhesive stamp, three years after the Scinde Dawk experiments in Sindh led the way. For a few pies a letter could then travel from Peshawar to Kanyakumari, and handwritten correspondence spread outward from courts and counting houses into the homes of ordinary households.\nRailways and steamships compressed delivery times steadily, while the telegraph, administered alongside the posts from 1854, separated intelligence from the correspondence of letters. By 1947 the post office had become an arm of the state itself, running savings banks from 1882, carrying money orders to migrant households and holding records in far districts. Postmasters at headquarters supervised the exchanges, and mail runners carried bags between villages throughout the princely states and British districts. The six-digit Postal Index Number of 1974 rationalised sorting throughout a network that counts above 154,000 offices, exceeding the branch strength of every scheduled commercial bank in India.\nSince the late 1990s, electronic mail, courier networks and mobile telephones have stripped away the letter traffic on which departmental finances rested for more than a century. India Post has responded through parcels, electronic commerce and doorstep banking, and its payments bank, launched in 2018, carries accounts to villages without a branch or cash machine.\nIts survival nevertheless rests on converting unmatched physical reach into digital relevance, without surrendering the obligation to serve each address throughout the Indian Union."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Urban flooding and the failure of drainage", "text": "The floods that paralysed Chennai in December 2015, Mumbai in August 2017 and Bengaluru in September 2022 were not accidents of weather but the outcome of planning choices accumulated over fifty years. Each city receives most of its annual rainfall inside a few monsoon weeks, and each once stored that surplus in marshlands, tanks and interconnected lakes across its low districts. In Chennai, the Pallikaranai marsh, measured at 5,500 hectares in the 1960s, had shrunk to 600 hectares by 2015, its margins lost to technology parks and housing towers.\nStorm water drains in many wards were designed for rainfall of 25 millimetres an hour, under half what a cloudburst now delivers onto the same streets. Responsibility is fragmented in a manner protecting every agency involved: corporations maintain the street drains, state departments control the canals, and development authorities sanction construction upon the floodplains themselves. An audit in 2021 found the Buckingham Canal narrowed below one-third of its old width at several stretches by encroachments, dumping and unauthorised construction.\nThe remedies after every disaster recur with remarkable consistency across cities and decades. They include floodplain zoning with statutory force, lake buffer zones of 30 metres around lakes such as Pallikaranai, telemetry-linked pumping stations and storm drains redesigned for 75 millimetres an hour under the rainfall curves of the Indian Meteorological Department.\nSponge-city programmes in Shenzhen are often invoked, although their land hunger and costs sit uneasily with the finances of Indian municipalities. Until drainage is funded and defended as core infrastructure, urban India will continue rediscovering, each monsoon, the exact geography of its accumulated planning failures."}, {"tid": "chsl-b1-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Antimicrobial resistance", "text": "Antimicrobial resistance, the gradual blunting of medicines that once destroyed bacteria with ease, now stands among the gravest threats monitored by the World Health Organization. A global analysis in The Lancet attributed 1.27 million deaths to resistant infections in 2019 and linked resistance to nearly 4.95 million further deaths. India carries a double exposure in this landscape, combining a heavy burden of infectious disease with antibiotic consumption that climbed by roughly 103 per cent between 2000 and 2015.\nThe mechanism is straightforward biology rather than mystery, operating wherever antibiotics are used carelessly across households, hospitals, poultry farms and aquaculture ponds. Every course of treatment eliminates the susceptible bacteria while sparing the resistant minority, whose descendants inherit the trait and exchange it with neighbouring organisms through gene transfer. Among Klebsiella pneumoniae isolates gathered in Indian intensive care units, resistance to carbapenem antibiotics surpassed 50 per cent in several surveillance networks by 2021.\nPharmaceutical effluents discharged near Hyderabad have carried drug concentrations high enough to select resistant bacteria in open river water. India adopted its National Action Plan on Antimicrobial Resistance in 2017 under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, distributing responsibility among health, agriculture and environment ministries, with uneven coordination.\nReversal demands prescription audits, laboratory capacity, sanitation investment and effluent regulation pressed together, because each open channel reseeds the rest. Without that combined pressure, the surgery, transplantation and chemotherapy that presuppose effective antibiotics will carry escalating risks ahead."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Ayushman Bharat health cover for families", "text": "Good health care is a basic need of every family. But a sudden illness can empty the savings of a poor home in a few days. A serious operation in a big hospital costs a large amount of money. Many families take loans at high interest to pay such bills. Some families sell land or gold to meet the cost of treatment. To help such families, the government runs a large health cover scheme. Under this scheme, each family gets free treatment worth five lakh rupees in a year. The patient does not pay any cash at the hospital counter. The cost of the treatment is paid by the scheme after the patient goes home. Earlier, many poor patients avoided hospitals for this reason.\n\nThe cover first began for the poorest families of the country. With time, more and more groups were added to the scheme. Village health workers and their families were brought under the cover. After that, all senior citizens above the age of seventy were also included. Crores of families now hold the health card of this scheme. A large number of hospitals in every state treat patients under it. A worker can use the card in any part of the country. This is a great help for people who live far from their home state for work. The scheme has reduced the burden of hospital bills on poor families. It has also given them the courage to visit a hospital on time, without fear of heavy debt. The card of the scheme is made free of cost for every family. A family can make the card at a health centre or a service centre near the home. Village health workers help families make the card and use it well. A help desk in the hospital guides patients through each step of the process. The scheme covers the cost of tests and medicines during the hospital stay. It covers treatment for a large number of diseases and operations."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Rural houses under PMAY-G", "text": "A strong and safe house is the dream of every rural family. Many families still live in mud houses with weak roofs and thin walls. Such houses leak during the rainy season and crack in the summer heat. Life inside them becomes hard for children and old people. A national housing scheme helps such families build a pucca house of their own. The family gets money in parts as the work of building moves ahead. Each house must have a toilet and a small kitchen inside it. Most of the houses are given in the name of the woman of the family. This gives her a sense of pride, safety and respect in the village.\n\nThe work under this scheme has moved ahead at a fast pace. More than three crore houses have been built in villages so far. A large number of houses are still under construction in many states. The new house is linked with other basic services of daily life. The family gets a gas connection, a power line and clean water through a tap. The aim of the scheme is to give a safe home to every rural family. The target is to cover all such families by the end of this decade. A good house improves the health of the whole family. It keeps children safe and gives them a quiet place to study. It also gives the family a firm base to plan a better future. The money for the house is paid straight into the bank account of the family. Workers of the village council guide the family at every step of the building work. Houses built under the scheme stand strong during flood and storm. Old people feel safe inside them on cold winter nights. Children of the family get a clean and dry place for study and play. In this way, the scheme is slowly changing the face of villages across the country. The family feels proud to invite relatives and neighbours to the new home."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "One Nation One Ration Card", "text": "Lakhs of people leave their villages to work in other states. A mason from a small village may work in a far city for most of the year. His wife and children may stay back in the village home. Earlier, his ration card worked only at the fair price shop of his own village. He could not take his share of grain from any shop in the new city. His family also faced trouble in taking the full share in his absence. This common problem has now been solved through a simple change in the system. A ration card can now be used at any fair price shop in the whole country.\n\nThe shop keeper checks the thumb mark of the person on a small machine kept in the shop. The machine reads the details of the card. It then shows the share of grain due to the family. The person can take rice or wheat from that shop at the same time. The other members of the family can take the balance share at the home village. The record of every sale is kept in digital form. It is stored on a central system. All ration cards in the country have been put on computers under this reform. Crores of such transfers have taken place since the system began some years ago. Workers can now move to any place for work. They do not fear losing their food. The system also stops wrong entries, fake cards and theft of grain. Each person in the family gets a fixed share of grain every month. The family can plan travel for work, study or treatment without worry about food. This freedom matters the most to poor families with small monthly incomes. At present, this grain is given free of cost to the family. A worker can also find the nearest fair price shop through a mobile app. Any adult member of the family can take the grain with the family card. In this way, food reaches the family wherever its members may live or work."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "DigiLocker and digital documents", "text": "Important papers are very hard to keep safe at home for a long time. A mark sheet can be lost during travel or torn with age. A driving licence can be left behind in another bag or coat. People often pay money to agents to get fresh copies of such papers. A free digital locker service now keeps these papers safe for every citizen. The locker is linked with the mobile number of the user. School boards, colleges and government offices put the papers straight into the locker. The papers kept in the locker are treated as equal to the original paper copies.\n\nCrores of people have opened their digital lockers in the last few years. Hundreds of crores of papers have been stored in these lockers so far. A student can show the mark sheet at a new college from a mobile phone. A driver can show the licence to a traffic officer on the phone screen. Offices accept these papers for forms, checks and other official work. There is no need to carry a heavy file of copies from one office to another. No person can change or fake the papers kept in the locker. This service saves time, paper and money for common people. It also makes daily work with government offices simple, safe and quick. The service is run by the government and is free for all users. Colleges check the papers of students through the locker at the time of admission. Banks and other offices also use it to check the identity papers of a person. A user can share a paper with any office through the phone in a few seconds. The office gets the true copy straight from the record of the board or department. This stops the use of fake mark sheets and false certificates in jobs and admissions. Young people use the locker the most, as they need papers again and again for study and work."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Kavach train safety system", "text": "Kavach is an automatic train protection system that India has developed to prevent collisions and overspeeding on busy railway routes. The system keeps a constant watch over the movement of a train through radio links between the train, the track and the station. If the loco pilot fails to respond to a danger signal, the system applies the brakes on its own. It also acts at once when a train crosses the permitted speed on any section. Kavach was adopted as the national protection system five years ago, and an improved version has since been approved for wider use. The system was designed by the Research Designs and Standards Organisation of the railways after long years of field trials on busy routes.\n\nIn its latest form, approved as version 4.0, Kavach is being fitted on locomotives and along 15,512 route kilometres in the first large phase of installation. Trackside devices and station equipment exchange radio signals with the locomotive many times every second through continuous communication links. The technology is especially useful in fog, when signals are difficult to see and the risk of human error rises sharply. Because the position of every fitted train is known at all times, two trains can be stopped well before they come too close. The system has been designed to the highest safety standard used anywhere in the world. Its steady expansion has supported a clear fall in railway accidents over the past decade. Railway funds of more than two thousand crore rupees have already been spent on the rollout of this safety programme. Accidents on the network have fallen from 135 in a year to 31 in a year within a decade, as better signalling and protection systems have spread across routes."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Millets, the Shree Anna grains", "text": "Millets are a group of small grains such as jowar, bajra and ragi that farmers have grown in this country for thousands of years. These hardy crops need very little water and can survive in dry regions where rice and wheat often fail. Because they are rich in fibre, minerals and protein, millets were formally declared nutri-cereals in 2018. The government then proposed that a full year be devoted to these grains across the world. The United Nations accepted the proposal, which dozens of countries supported, and declared the International Year of Millets.\n\nThe fresh interest in these grains is closely linked with public health and with climate concerns. Millets release sugar slowly in the body, which makes them a better choice for people who must control diabetes. A millet field also places a smaller burden on groundwater, since it can be raised with a fraction of the water that a paddy field demands. Research and training on these crops are led by a national institute at Hyderabad, which has been named a Global Centre of Excellence. The millet movement is connected with crores of small farmers across the dry districts. It aims to raise their incomes and restore a larger place for millets in the daily diet. Millets also suit the local climate, since they grow well in summer heat and on poor soils. Their short season allows farmers to harvest a crop even when the monsoon arrives late. Nutrition experts advise families to mix millets with rice and wheat in regular meals. State agriculture departments supply improved seed and processing machines to farmer groups in dry districts. Government programmes also promote millets through public distribution and school meals in several regions."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Khelo India and the sports pathway", "text": "The Khelo India programme was launched nearly a decade ago to build a strong base for sport in the country. Its purpose was to spot young talent early and support it with proper coaching and facilities. School and university games, training centres and sports infrastructure were brought together under one national framework. The programme began in 2017, and it now supports 2,745 athletes across the country. Athletes identified through the programme receive coaching, equipment, diet support and a monthly allowance for expenses. Under Khelo India, each selected athlete is placed with an accredited academy for training. In this manner, thousands of young sportspersons are being prepared for national and international competition.\n\nThe programme has now been expanded for the next five years. The Union Cabinet has approved a large combined outlay for the revamped scheme and for greater assistance to national sports federations. The approved amount is nearly eight times the outlay of the previous scheme, which shows the scale of the new ambition. The central idea is to create a clear pathway for every talented young player in the country. A child should be able to move step by step from a school playground or a village field to the national team and, in time, to the Olympic podium. State governments run Khelo India centres in districts so that children can train close to their homes. Annual games for schools, universities and para athletes create a calendar of competition. Coaches use sports science, physiotherapy and video analysis to improve the performance of players. The best performers are picked for national camps and foreign tours. The design also reflects the young character of the population, since about two-thirds of the people are below the age of thirty-five."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "SVAMITVA property cards for villages", "text": "In most villages, houses in the inhabited area were never measured or recorded in any formal register. Families lived on their plots for generations, yet they held no legal paper that could prove ownership. This gap led to frequent disagreements between neighbours over small strips of land. It also made it difficult for owners to borrow money from a bank against their homes. The SVAMITVA scheme was launched five years ago to close this gap through modern measurement methods throughout the country. The scheme is run by the Ministry of Panchayati Raj together with the Survey of India and the revenue departments of the states.\n\nUnder the scheme, drones fly over the village and map every house and plot with accuracy. A network of ground reference stations supports the survey and keeps the error within centimetres. Based on these maps, a property card is prepared for each owner as a legal record. Drone surveys have been completed in lakhs of villages across the country so far. 2.42 crore property cards have been issued to owners in 1.61 lakh villages. Each card states the size, boundaries and ownership of the plot in clear terms. A clear title also helps banks lend against rural property with confidence. SVAMITVA thus turns a silent village asset into a document of real economic value. Village councils verify the maps in open meetings before cards are handed over. Women are recorded as joint owners in many states, which strengthens their claim on the family home. Proper documentation protects poor families from land grabbers and from costly court cases. The cards also support the registration of sales and inheritance without long disputes between heirs. Digital maps prepared under the scheme help panchayats plan drains, roads and community buildings with better information."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Gaganyaan human spaceflight programme", "text": "The Gaganyaan programme, through which India seeks to demonstrate human spaceflight capability with indigenous systems throughout, has moved through ground testing and trial flights towards its first uncrewed orbital mission.\n\nThe human-rated launch vehicle, HLVM3, has completed the development and ground testing of all propulsion stages and structures, and the propulsion systems of the crew module and the service module, together with the environmental control system, stand qualified. The crew escape system, which must pull the crew module clear of the rocket within seconds of an emergency, has been proven through the first test vehicle flight, TV-D1. Two integrated air drop tests, conducted with a simulated crew module, have likewise validated the parachutes and the deceleration sequence through which the astronauts return to the sea.\n\nThe four astronaut-designates of the Indian Air Force have completed generic training at Glavkosmos in Russia and are undergoing mission-specific instruction at the Astronaut Training Facility in Bengaluru. The first uncrewed flight, G1, will carry the half-humanoid Vyommitra to test life support and re-entry behaviour before astronauts fly in 2027.\n\nThe missions will lift off from the Second Launch Pad at Sriharikota, where infrastructure, crew access arrangements, escape ziplines and checkout facilities stand commissioned for human flight. Recovery formations of the Indian Navy will retrieve the crew module after splashdown, while ground stations throughout the world track the spacecraft and support the astronauts. An approval of Rs 20,193 crore, granted in September 2024, now covers eight missions and the first module of the Bharatiya Antariksh Station, targeted for 2035."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan", "text": "The PM GatiShakti National Master Plan, launched in October 2021, was framed to end the practice whereby departments planned roads, railways and pipelines throughout the country without sight of one another.\n\nIts foundation is a geospatial platform built by the Bhaskaracharya National Institute for Space Applications and Geo-informatics, which places the maps of all participating departments over common satellite imagery. The platform holds more than 1,800 data layers from 58 Central Ministries and from all 36 States and Union Territories. A planner can therefore check existing highways, forests, water bodies and habitations before a fresh alignment is approved anywhere. The seven engines of the plan are Roads, Railways, Ports, Waterways, Airports, Mass Transport and Logistics Infrastructure.\n\nThe Network Planning Group has examined 421 infrastructure projects worth Rs 20.23 lakh crore against standards of integrated planning and last-mile connectivity. A public platform, opened through the Unified Geospatial Interface in September 2025, has since extended datasets to private firms and citizens.\n\nThe departments themselves now test each proposal against the background of existing infrastructure before funds are committed. State governments run mirror programmes throughout their districts, and households gain when roads, water lines and power lines reach them together. The arrangement has cut the time that large proposals spend moving between departments, since conflicts over land and crossings are settled beforehand. Widespread duplication of surveys has fallen as departments share a single map. Five years after launch, the framework has grown from a mapping exercise into the standard route through which large projects are aligned across governments throughout India."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "National Green Hydrogen Mission", "text": "The National Green Hydrogen Mission, approved in January 2023 with an outlay of Rs 19,744 crore, seeks to place India among the leading producers, consumers and exporters of hydrogen made without fossil fuels.\n\nGreen hydrogen is produced when water is split through electrolysis driven by renewable electricity, and it can replace carbon-intensive fuels in fertiliser plants, refineries and steel furnaces. Such sectors cannot switch easily to direct electrification, so a clean fuel carries particular value for them. The mission sets a production target of 5 million metric tonnes a year by 2030. Most of the outlay, Rs 17,490 crore, funds the Strategic Interventions for Green Hydrogen Transition programme, the incentive framework that supports electrolyser manufacture and hydrogen production.\n\nAwards under the programme have already covered electrolyser manufacturing capacity of 3,000 megawatts a year and production capacity of 8.62 lakh tonnes a year across firms throughout India. The Government of India expects investments above Rs 8 lakh crore and employment above 6 lakh persons through the mission by 2030.\n\nRefineries and fertiliser plants throughout the country are expected to become the earliest large buyers of the new fuel. The mission additionally funds research, testing facilities and skill development for the technicians and engineers the industry will need. Producers must themselves meet the purity requirements announced by the government before their output can qualify as green hydrogen. Ports on both seaboards are being prepared to handle shipments for overseas markets in the approaching decade. Since the country imports most of its crude oil and much of its natural gas, the mission serves decarbonisation and energy security through the same instrument."}, {"tid": "chsl-b2-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Cyclone early warning and forecast accuracy", "text": "The worth of a cyclone warning service is judged by how precisely landfall, track and strength are forecast for coastal districts that must evacuate households beforehand.\n\nThe India Meteorological Department has reported marked gains throughout its forecasting system between the two periods compared in Parliament. Track accuracy improved by 15 to 45 percent, intensity accuracy by 15 to 35 percent and landfall accuracy by 10 to 30 percent between 2020-2022 and 2023-2025. The gains rest upon numerical weather prediction models, multi-model ensembles and observation through satellites, Doppler Weather Radars, ocean buoys and coastal stations. Warnings are assembled through a GIS-based Decision Support System that converts observations into bulletins for district authorities and disaster managers.\n\nMission Mausam, launched in early 2025, is expanding the observation network of weather stations, rain gauges and radars throughout the coastal belt. During Cyclone Ditwah over the Bay of Bengal, the department issued 51 national bulletins and forecast the track and the weakening of the storm with errors well below long-period averages.\n\nCoastal households themselves now receive colour-coded warnings on their phones throughout the season of storms. State governments maintain shelters, stores of food and rescue teams throughout the districts facing the sea. Background preparation, repeated throughout each year, has changed outcomes for the poorest coastal households. Rescue teams afterwards restore power lines and clear roads so that normal life can resume within days. Widespread flooding still damages crops and homes, but deaths from cyclones, which once ran into thousands in severe landfalls, have been held to double digits through a decade of sharper warnings and evacuations."}], "cgl": [{"tid": "cgl-b1-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Trees in our neighbourhood", "text": "Trees stand along our lanes and shade us each day. They cool the air when the sun feels too hot. Their roots hold the soil and keep it in place. Birds build small nests among the green leaves up high. Children play below them and rest in the cool shade. Old men sit near them and talk with their friends. Women sweep the fallen leaves from paths each calm morning. The trees give us fruit in the warm summer months. Some trees bear sweet fruit that we all love dearly. Bees move from flower to flower in the soft light. The air near trees feels fresh and clean to breathe. Rain falls soft on leaves and then on the ground. In dry weeks we pour water near their weak roots. A young plant needs care in its first few years. We guard young plants from goats and from stray cattle. Each home can plant one tree in its front yard. Schools can plant trees along their wide outer boundary walls. Shops can keep small pots with green plants near doors.\nA tree grows slow but it serves us for long. It gives shade to birds beasts and tired road walkers. In hot months its shade brings real rest to all. Dust stays low where trees line both sides of roads. Noise feels less near homes that have trees around them. Green leaves lift our mood when we feel quite low. We should not cut a tree without a clear need. When we must cut one we should plant two more. Dry leaves can be turned into food for the soil. Thick trees help small rain stay longer in the ground. At dusk birds return to them and sing soft songs. In each season the trees change yet stay our friends. Plant a tree today and watch it grow each year. Care for it well and it will care for you. Families gather together beneath spreading branches during pleasant summer evenings. Children remember climbing strong branches together during carefree childhood holidays. Gardeners nurture seedlings carefully because woodlands support rainfall patterns nearby. Morning sunlight filters through foliage creating shifting patterns everywhere below."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The importance of sleep", "text": "Sleep is a gift that our body needs each night. We spend a large part of life in deep sleep. Sleep helps the brain sort all that we learn daily. A tired mind finds it hard to think quite clearly. Good sleep keeps our mood calm through the whole day. It helps the heart rest and beat at ease too. Those who sleep well fall ill far less often here. Sleep gives strength to fight germs that cause ill health. Children need more sleep than grown men and women do. Young minds grow fast when sleep is deep and sound. Late nights make the next day feel dull and slow. Bright screens at night keep the brain wide awake still. A dark room helps us fall asleep much more soon. A cool room also helps the body rest quite well. We should go to bed at the same time daily. A set time trains the body to feel sleepy soon.\nTea or strong drinks at night can spoil our sleep. Heavy meals late at night make sleep hard to get. Light food in the night helps us rest much better. A short walk after meals can calm the whole body. Day naps should be short and not too long here. Long naps in the day can spoil night sleep badly. When we wake fresh we work with full heart daily. We learn new things fast when we sleep quite well. Safe roads need drivers who have had full rest too. Rest is not waste of time but true self care. Each night of good sleep builds health for long term. Let us treat sleep as a friend for all life. Regular routines support recovery because tired muscles rebuild alone overnight. Peaceful bedrooms without noise encourage deeper rest through entire nights. Evening habits influence morning freshness because bodies follow internal rhythms. Parents urge children towards regular bedtimes during growing school years. Dreaming supports learning because memories grow strong during quiet sleep. Workers perform better after rest because attention sharpens quite naturally after. Healthy sleeping patterns protect hearts brains and bodies through lifetimes together. Relaxing pastimes before bedtime prepare minds towards peaceful nightly rest."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A village fair", "text": "A village fair brings great joy to one and all. It is held once a year in the open ground. Men and women come from near and far off places. Children wait for this day with bright eyes and smiles. Shops line both sides of the wide fair ground here. Some shops sell sweets and some sell toys and clothes. The smell of hot food fills the air all around. Big wheels turn slow and lift kids high in air. Drums beat loud and folk songs play through the day. Farmers show their grain tools and fine hand made goods. Women buy bangles pots and cloth in bright rich shades. Old friends meet here and share news from their homes. Games of skill draw crowds who cheer each bold try.\nAt dusk lamps glow and the fair looks quite grand. Sweet shops do brisk trade as crowds move past them. Kids hold toys in hand and beg for more treats. Some men show tricks that make the crowd laugh aloud. Dance and song go on till late in the night. The fair gives work to many poor hands each year. It helps small traders earn well in a few days. It keeps old crafts and old songs alive and strong. For a few days the village feels full of life. When the fair ends all go home tired but glad. They carry sweets toys and tales back to their homes. Next year they will come back to this fair again. Such fairs bind hearts and make village life rich indeed. Traders deck stalls brightly because bright signs draw passing visitors around. Musicians perform folk melodies while dancers entertain gathered audiences nightly. Families buy home goods because fair prices seem modest generally today. Children treasure memories created during fun annual village festivals. Craftsmen show weaving pottery and woodwork before keen watching villagers daily. Evening fireworks brighten excited faces gathered together around main grounds below. Helpers manage drinking water because thirsty visitors need relief all afternoon. Elders tell stories about earlier fairs during fond family talks together. Traders travel long distances because village meetings promise good earnings yearly."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Keeping our streets clean", "text": "Clean streets make our town a fine place to live. Dirt on roads can spread germs and cause ill health. Each of us has a part in this good work. We should throw waste in bins and not on roads. Dry waste and wet waste should be kept quite apart. Shops should keep a bin near their front door daily. Homes should hand waste to the cart each calm morning. We must not spit on paths walls or road sides. Leaves and dust should be swept and cleared each day. Drains should stay clear so water can flow quite free. Blocked drains breed bugs and give off a bad smell. Stray waste draws flies dogs and rats to our lanes. Clean lanes look neat and lift the pride of all. School kids can be taught to keep their area clean.\nTeams of youth can clean a park on rest days. Each lane can take turns to watch its own tidiness. Walls look good when no one sticks bills on them. Trees and clean paths make walks a joy each morning. Guests feel good when they see our streets so clean. A clean town draws more trade and more guests too. Clean streets keep rain water pure for wells and tanks. Small acts done daily by all can change our town. Pick up one bit of waste when you see it. Urge friends to use bins and not the open road. Pride in clean streets is pride in our own home. Let us join hands and keep our streets clean always. Residents cooperate together because local tidiness rests on shared daily effort. Homes sort kitchen waste carefully before town carts arrive every morning. Helpers run public drives urging families towards careful waste habits. Children learn cleanliness early because schools support positive conduct daily. Shop owners keep dustbins outside shops ensuring customers drop wrappers neatly always. Local leaders inspect drains often because blockages create stagnant pools. Morning sweepers deserve respect because their labour protects all from diseases. Families recycle papers bottles and tins rather than burning home rubbish outside. Clean areas invite outdoor walking because pleasant pathways invite regular exercise daily."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How the internet reaches our homes", "text": "When you open a web page at home, the request travels farther and faster than most people imagine. Much of the world's data moves through undersea cables that land in India at Mumbai and Chennai. From those landing stations, fibre cables carry signals across states through ducts buried beside roads and railways. Telecom companies such as Airtel and Jio lease capacity on these backbone networks and extend connections towards smaller towns. In many districts, the government scheme BharatNet has laid fibre to village councils to support public digital facilities. The cable reaches a distribution box in your street, where engineers meticulously splice thinner fibres for individual buildings.\nFrom that box, a slim optical fibre enters your home and connects to a device called a router. The router changes light signals into electrical signals and spreads them through the rooms as wireless waves. Your phone, laptop and television receive these waves and send replies back along the same path. Speed depends on the plan you purchase, the wiring and the distance from the exchange. A common home plan in 2023 offered 100 megabits per second, enough for classes, meetings and films. Engineers measure signal loss in the fibre, because sharp bends, dust or loose joints can slow everything down. When a cable is cut during road work, repair teams locate the break with instruments and fuse the strands.\nNot every home, however, receives the internet through a cable, and many families depend entirely on mobile networks. Towers fitted with panels exchange radio signals with phones, and each tower links back to the backbone by fibre. In hilly villages of Himachal Pradesh, wireless links sometimes substitute cables where digging across rocky slopes proves too costly. Whatever the route, every message is broken into packets, addressed, checked for errors and reassembled at its destination. Understanding this journey helps users complain effectively, choose right plans and appreciate the workers who maintain the infrastructure."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The water cycle and farming", "text": "The water cycle is the continuous movement of water between the sea, the land and the sky above us. Heat from the sun lifts water from oceans, lakes and wet soil, turning it into invisible vapour that rises. As the vapour climbs into cooler air, it condenses around tiny dust particles and forms the clouds we see. When the droplets grow heavy enough, they fall again as rain, hail or snow, depending on atmospheric conditions. Some rainwater runs into streams and rivers, while the rest soaks deep into the ground and replenishes wells. Plants also release moisture from their leaves, and this quiet evaporation process adds considerably to the vapour in the air.\nFor farmers, the timing of this cycle matters far more than the total rainfall recorded in a whole year. The monsoon usually reaches Kerala in early June and then advances slowly across the country over several weeks. A delayed onset can shorten the sowing window for paddy, cotton and pulses in many rainfed farming districts. In 2022, several districts received nearly 40 percent of their seasonal rain within a single fortnight of storms. Such intense bursts cause runoff and flooding, whereas gentle showers spread evenly allow the soil to absorb moisture effectively. Farmers therefore watch predictions from the India Meteorological Department and plan sowing, irrigation and drainage around expected rainfall patterns.\nStored groundwater completes the cycle for agriculture, returning to fields through wells, tubewells and canals during dry months. When rains fail, this reserve decides whether a second crop can be raised or must be abandoned completely. Wise farmers in Punjab harvest rainwater in ponds, maintain field bunds and choose crops suited to the local water supply. Mulching the soil, rotating crops and avoiding wasteful flooding all help conserve every available litre that the cycle delivers. In this way, an ancient hydrological process and modern farming practice remain closely linked in every agricultural region."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Public libraries in the digital age", "text": "Public libraries were once the only places where common readers could borrow books, buying them at high prices. In India, early rulers and chiefs opened book rooms in cities such as Baroda, Kolkata and Madras long ago. The great old libraries of Chennai and Mumbai still draw serious readers and pupils from many towns nearby daily. After independence, many states passed library laws and built networks linking district libraries with smaller branches in towns. These libraries offered newspapers, fact books, tough examination notes and quiet halls where pupils could prepare quietly for hours. Membership often cost very little, and a single card opened shelves that no poor family could ever own.\nThe coming of the internet changed reading habits, and many said that public libraries would simply vanish within years. Instead, successful libraries have reinvented afresh as digital centres where guests use computers, printers and reliable internet connections daily. Members now read electronic books, consult web periodicals and watch video presentations through portals like the National Digital Library. Staff help old guests fill web forms, download certificates and learn basic skills with great patience and care daily. During exam season, study halls in Delhi and Bengaluru remain full from early morning until late at night. In 2021, a rebuilt town library reported that nearly 65 percent of its new members were under thirty.\nThis revival shows that libraries were never just stores of books, but open public spaces for learning and community. They save local history, rare manuscripts and old newspapers that private holdings and paid websites seldom bother to keep. Story hours, author meets and children's classes bring neighbours in towns together and promote reading as a joint habit. Free access remains their biggest strength, because learning in a democracy should not depend wholly on one's wealth. If governments keep funding staff, halls and digital help, public libraries will remain relevant in every coming decade."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The story of tea in India", "text": "Tea is so familiar in India today that it is difficult to imagine the country without this refreshing drink. The plant grew wild in the forests of Assam, where local tribes had used its leaves for centuries. British officials recognised its commercial value after travellers reported the discovery of wild tea bushes in 1823. The company soon established experimental gardens and began importing seeds, plants and skilled tea makers from abroad for trials. Workers were recruited from distant districts and brought to new plantations under harsh conditions that caused great suffering. By the end of the nineteenth century, Assam and Darjeeling teas were being exported to markets across the world.\nGrowing tea demands a warm climate, frequent rainfall, sloping land and acidic soil that drains water very quickly. Tender leaves are plucked by hand, usually by women, who collect the famous two leaves and a bud. The harvested leaves are withered, rolled, fermented and dried in factories located close to the gardens themselves for freshness. Different methods produce black tea, green tea and the prized white tea sold at high prices abroad. In the Nilgiri hills of Tamil Nadu, small growers sell their green leaf to cooperative factories in nearby towns. Auctions held in Kolkata and Guwahati decide prices that affect the incomes of lakhs of plantation families.\nIndependence changed the industry, and local companies gradually replaced British firms as owners of most large tea estates. Tea also moved from elite drawing rooms to railway platforms, offices and roadside stalls in every small town. The cutting chai of Mumbai, served in glasses, became a symbol of the city's hurried working life. Today India remains among the largest producers and consumers of tea anywhere in the world. Behind every inexpensive cup lies a long history of forests, empire, labour and trade worth remembering with care. Nearly 85 percent of the tea produced is consumed within the country itself by its own people every year."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "India's demographic transition", "text": "India is passing through a demographic transition in which falling death rates first raised growth and falling birth rates are slowing it throughout settlements. The transition began when better food, cleaner water and healthcare cut deaths among children and lengthened the lives of households across the countryside thereafter. Birth rates stayed high for decades afterwards, so each schoolroom generation grew larger than the last and schools, towns and marketplaces filled with people.\nAccording to the Census of 2021 and reports of the Registrar General, India counted 1.4 billion people spread across states with contrasting household patterns. Kerala and Tamil Nadu reached low birth rates decades ago, whereas Bihar and Uttar Pradesh still record higher rates and younger structures throughout their districts. The National Family Health Survey reported a fertility rate of 2.0 children per woman in 2020, below the replacement benchmark used across departments countrywide.\nA falling share of children eases pressure on schoolrooms, while a share of grandparents increases demand for healthcare, pensions and support within households and neighbourhoods. Between 2011 and 2036, projections suggest the working age share may climb towards 65 percent before it turns downwards in most states thereafter. This dividend is not straightforward, because workers create wealth only when they are healthy, skilled and matched with worthwhile jobs in workplaces nearby.\nStates that postpone schooling frameworks and neglect women's employment risk wasting the dividend, since fewer contributors must support growing numbers of dependent grandparents. Migration links the regions together, as workers move from Bihar and Jharkhand towards Maharashtra, Gujarat and Delhi in search of wages and livelihoods. Sound frameworks therefore treat the transition as a long shared undertaking, joining schoolrooms, healthcare, settlements and workplaces so that lengthened lives become a strength."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Coastal erosion and climate adaptation", "text": "Coastal erosion is the wearing away of land by waves, tides and storms, and it reshapes coastlines throughout the world wherever settlements stand close to the shoreline. Stronger storms and rising seas now quicken this loss, carrying away beaches, farmlands and grasslands and pushing salt water into wells, ponds and freshwater creeks downstream.\nThe Central Water Commission and the National Centre for Coastal Research have mapped stretches where erosion runs high, medium or low along the mainland shoreline countrywide. A widely cited assessment found that about 34 percent of the mainland coast was eroding between 1990 and 2018, while roughly 26 percent was gaining land. Meanwhile, sea levels around the Indian coastline have risen by about 8.5 centimetres since 1950, and projections suggest sharper rises throughout this century without checks.\nAdaptation begins with protecting what nature already provides, because mangroves, sandbanks, wetlands and grasslands soften waves and hold shorelines together far better than bare concrete walls. Governments and departments in Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh and Maharashtra have planted mangroves, restored wetlands and rebuilt dunes with grasses that bind loose sand firmly. Where towns and harbours need harder defences, engineers build seawalls, breakwaters and embankments, although such structures sometimes shift erosion downstream towards neighbouring villages and beaches nearby.\nFrameworks for adaptation also require better information, since the Indian National Centre for Ocean Information Services warns fishermen and coastal households whenever storms approach the coast. Local bodies in Odisha and West Bengal drill schoolchildren and grandparents in evacuation routines, stock cyclone shelters with drinking water and maintain raised platforms for livestock. Relocating the most exposed households remains the hardest choice, because families fear losing livelihoods, neighbourhoods and birthplaces even when the sea takes land yearly."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The finances of local self-government (panchayats and municipalities)", "text": "Local self-government in India rests on panchayats in villages and municipalities in towns, yet these bodies handle duties with small funds of their own. The Constitution assigns them subjects through the Eleventh and Twelfth Schedules, while the transfer of staff, functions and funds depends on state governments throughout. Gram Panchayats, Panchayat Samitis and Zila Parishads form the three tier structure, and Nagar Panchayats, Municipal Councils and Municipal Corporations serve settlements in towns.\nOwn revenues stay narrow because village bodies may tax houses, fairs and marketplaces, but rates remain low and collections weaker than assessments across districts. Property tax forms the backbone of municipal income in Indore, Surat and Nagpur, although outdated records and widespread exemptions shrink its reach considerably. In 2019, a study by the Reserve Bank of India estimated that municipal revenues stood near 1.0 percent of national income, below federations countrywide. Transfers from the Fifteenth Finance Commission gave bodies grants 4.36 lakh crore rupees between 2021 and 2026 for drinking water, sanitation and rainwater harvesting.\nMost funds nevertheless arrive tied to schemes designed by departments in New Delhi or state capitals, leaving little freedom for councillors to answer local demands.\nHouseholds judge these bodies by daily outcomes, namely streetlights, drains, drinking water, birth records and the upkeep of schoolrooms, lanes and neighbourhood playgrounds throughout their wards. Audits by the Comptroller and Auditor General have flagged delayed accounts, records and understaffed accounts departments in councils across states in recent years. Reforming local finances therefore needs updated tax records, user charges for wastewater and stormwater services, predictable transfers and trained staff in every accounts department before 2030. When panchayats and councils command steadier resources, accountability sharpens, because residents can link the taxes they pay with outcomes they witness."}, {"tid": "cgl-b1-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Precision agriculture and satellite data", "text": "Precision farming uses satellite data, sensors and software to guide farming decisions field by field, replacing uniform treatment of whole farms with measured, targeted action throughout. Satellites operated by the Indian Space Research Organisation, including Resourcesat and Cartosat, photograph farmlands repeatedly and reveal crop growth, soil wetness and stress across districts. The European Space Agency's Sentinel satellites add free photographs every few days, while drones flown by startups in Bengaluru and Hyderabad map smaller fields closely.\nA farmer growing wheat in Punjab or cotton in Maharashtra can study coloured maps that mark patches where crops look pale, dry or weaker than surroundings. Such maps rest on plant growth measurements, and one common measurement comparing reflected light stood at 0.62 in one healthy mustard field surveyed in 2023. Soil testing centres, weather forecasts from the India Meteorological Department and satellite readings together shape advice on seed rates, fertiliser placement and watering schedules for households. Because inputs are placed only where needed, fertiliser use may fall by 15 percent and water savings may reach 20 percent on well managed demonstration farms.\nStartups linked with the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, NABARD and state farm colleges now deliver such advice through phones in languages spoken across the countryside. Small landholdings, patchy internet and costly sensors still slow adoption, since most Indian farm households cultivate plots smaller than 2.5 hectares in fragmented parcels throughout the state.\nTraining through Krishi Vigyan Kendras and farmer producer groups helps households, school leavers and grandparents alike read maps, trust measurements and question faulty advice with confidence. As costs fall and photographs sharpen, satellite guided farming should strengthen livelihoods, protect groundwater and help departments plan procurement, insurance and drought relief more fairly countrywide by 2030."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Jal Jeevan Mission and rural tap water", "text": "When the Jal Jeevan Mission began in August 2019, only a small share of village homes had a tap with clean water. Most women and girls walked long distances to fetch water from wells, ponds and hand pumps. They carried heavy pots on their heads. This went on in summer heat and in the rains. This daily task took their time and also harmed their health. The mission set a simple and clear goal for the whole country. Every rural home should get safe tap water inside its own boundary wall. The water should come in enough quantity on a regular basis through the year. The mission also fixed a clear standard. It covered both quantity and quality of supply. Each person should receive enough water for drinking, cooking, bathing and washing.\n\nSince then, the change in the villages has been very large and visible. More than four out of every five rural homes now have a tap connection. Crores of new connections have been given since the mission started its work. Village committees now plan and manage their own water supply schemes. Women play a leading part in these committees in most of the states. Local people are trained to test the quality of water in the village itself. Simple field kits are used to check whether the water is safe to drink. Schools and anganwadi centres have also been given tap connections on priority. The mission now supports the states with funds, training and technical guidance. The central government has extended the mission for a few more years. Its total approved cost has also been raised to complete the pending work. The focus has now shifted from only giving new connections to running them well. A tap is useful only when water actually flows through it every day. Source protection, repair work and fair water charges keep a scheme alive. The mission therefore asks villages to protect their ponds, wells and streams. It also promotes the reuse of waste water for gardens and small fields. In this way, a mission that began as a promise of taps has become a quiet change in daily village life."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Lakhpati Didi and women's self-help groups", "text": "In village after village, women have come together in small self help groups. Each member saves a small sum of money every week or every month. The group keeps a proper record of its savings in a simple register. Members can borrow from the common fund when a need arises at home. In time, the group also starts to get loans from the local bank. These groups are the base of the national rural livelihood mission. Crores of rural households have joined such groups across the country. The mission trains the women in keeping accounts and running small work. It also helps them meet bankers, buyers and government officers with ease.\n\nA lakhpati didi is a woman member whose family income has crossed one lakh rupees in a year. Her monthly income stays at a steady level through all the seasons. She may earn from farming, animal care, tailoring or a small shop. Some women run flour mills, tea stalls or beauty parlours in the village. Others make pickles, papads, candles or cloth bags for nearby markets. The mission does not hand out this income to any woman directly. It only builds her skill, credit and confidence step by step. Trainers help each woman choose work that suits her home and village. She learns how to price her goods and how to sell them well. Digital training teaches her to receive payments on her phone. She also learns to keep her savings safe in a bank account. A large number of women have already reached the lakhpati level. Many more are moving towards it with support from their groups. The change is visible in small things of daily life at home. Children now stay in school for more years than before. Families spend more on food, health and the repair of their houses. A woman who earns is heard with respect in family decisions. Her success also inspires other women of the village to begin. In this quiet way, the groups are changing both income and confidence. Her group stands behind her like a second family in every need."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "UDAN regional air connectivity", "text": "For many years, air travel in our country was limited to big cities. Small towns had no airport and no flights at all for their people. A person from such a town first took a train or bus to a metro city. Only from there could he or she catch a plane to another place. The UDAN scheme was started to change this old picture of air travel. Its name carries the promise that the common citizen should also fly. Under the scheme, flights were started from small towns and unused airports. The government shares a part of the cost of these new routes. This support keeps the fare within the reach of ordinary families. Airlines are chosen for each route through an open bidding process.\n\nIn ten years, hundreds of new air routes have become operational. Scores of airports, heliports and water aerodromes have joined the map. Lakhs of passengers have flown from towns that never had a flight before. For many of them, it was indeed the first flight of their whole life so far. A patient can now reach a big hospital in a few hours. A student can attend an interview or exam in a far city. Traders carry samples and goods to new markets in the same day. Tourists have begun to visit hills, deserts and temple towns with ease. Local youth have found jobs in airports, hotels and travel firms. The scheme has also helped during floods and other emergencies. Planes and helicopters reach cut off areas with food and doctors. A new and larger version of the scheme has now been approved. Many new airports and heliports are planned in the coming years. Small towns in the hills and the north east will gain the most. Work has also begun on training more pilots and ground staff. Air travel is slowly becoming a normal part of life for small town India. The change shows how a public scheme can open the skies to all. Small airports have also raised the value of land and trade in their towns. The sky, once distant for the common citizen, now feels close to home."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Skill training under PMKVY", "text": "A large number of our youth leave school without any job skill. They look for work but have no certificate to show an employer. The national skill programme was started to fill this wide gap. It offers short training courses free of cost to young people. Training centres run in towns and villages in every district. A student can choose a trade that matches his or her interest. Courses run for electricians, plumbers, welders and car mechanics. There are also courses for tailoring, beauty care and food work. New courses teach youth to work with computers and mobile phones. Some centres train youth to fly drones and repair solar panels. Other courses cover health care, retail sales and hotel services. Each course mixes classroom lessons with real practice on machines. Trainers test the students at the end of the course. Those who pass receive a certificate that employers can trust.\n\nSince the programme began, crores of youth have been trained or guided. In the latest phase alone, lakhs of candidates have finished training. The courses now run in dozens of sectors across all states. A recent study found that most employers are ready to hire trained youth. Many trained students get jobs in factories, shops and offices. Others use their skill to start small work of their own. A trained tailor can open a shop in her own village street. A drone pilot can serve farmers by spraying their fields for a fee. The programme also gives credit for skill learned at home or at work. A carpenter with years of practice can get a certificate after a short test. This respect for old skill raises the confidence of workers. Women form a large share of the students in many trades. Special centres train youth from poor and remote districts on priority. The aim is simple and clear for every young Indian. No youth should sit idle only because he never got a chance to learn. Skill, once gained, stays with a person for the whole of life. It turns a search for any work into a choice of better work."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Amrit Bharat Station Scheme redevelopment", "text": "The railway station is often the first face of a city that a traveller sees. For decades, however, most Indian stations remained crowded, dim and difficult for elderly passengers to use. The Amrit Bharat Station Scheme was launched to rebuild this everyday infrastructure in a planned manner. Under the scheme, 1,337 stations across the country have been identified for phased redevelopment. The work goes well beyond fresh paint and new signboards on old buildings. Each station is treated as a city centre that must serve passengers and the surrounding neighbourhood together. Wider entrances, cleaner platforms and better waiting halls form the basic core of the redesign. Foot overbridges are being widened, and lifts and escalators are added for Divyangjan passengers. Free Wi-Fi, improved lighting and clear digital information boards are now standard features. Local art, heritage and products are displayed so that the station reflects the identity of its region.\n\nProgress under the scheme has moved from planning to visible completion in many states. Works have been completed at 155 stations, while construction continues at hundreds of others. In May 2025, the Prime Minister dedicated more than a hundred rebuilt stations to the nation in a single event. The Railways allocated Rs 12,118 crore for station rebuilding in a single recent year, and spending continues to rise. Priority is given to stations that handle heavy passenger traffic and long-distance trains. Wherever land permits, the redesign provides parking, bus bays and space for auto-rickshaws outside. This integration reduces the chaos that passengers earlier faced while changing transport modes. Rooftop solar panels and rainwater harvesting are included in the standard design package. The larger idea is that a station should earn revenue, serve the city and respect its environment together. If the current pace holds, the familiar Indian station will look and feel very different within this decade."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "PM POSHAN school meal programme", "text": "The midday meal is one of the largest school feeding programmes anywhere in the world. Under PM POSHAN, one hot cooked meal is served to 11.20 crore children every school day. The beneficiaries study in Balvatika and Classes I to VIII in government and government-aided schools. These schools number 10.36 lakh, spread across every state and union territory. The meal is expected to do far more than simply satisfy the hunger of a child. A full stomach improves attention in class and reduces the number of children who drop out. For many poor families, the school meal is a strong reason to send daughters to school regularly. Nutrition norms fix the quantity of protein and calories that each meal must provide. Rice, wheat, pulses and vegetables are combined to meet these norms.\n\nThe government revised the material cost of the meal with effect from the first of May. The rate is now Rs 6.78 per child per day for the primary stage. For the upper primary stage, the corresponding rate stands at Rs 10.17 per child. The revision added roughly a thousand crore rupees to the central budget for the scheme in one year. Foodgrains are supplied through the Food Corporation of India at a heavily subsidised cost. The annual foodgrain subsidy for the programme is close to nine thousand crore rupees. Cooks and helpers, most of them women from the local community, prepare the meal in school kitchens. Many states add eggs, milk or fruit from their own budgets to enrich the menu. Kitchen gardens in schools supply fresh vegetables and teach children the basics of growing food. Regular social audits protect both quality and safety. The programme thus joins the goals of education, nutrition and local employment in a single plate.\n\nMany states supplement the central menu with eggs, milk or fruit according to local food habits. Attendance registers show that enrolment improves where meals are served hot and on time. The programme thus functions as a nutrition intervention, an enrolment incentive and a source of respectable local employment."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Startup India decade of growth", "text": "When the Startup India initiative was launched in 2016, recognised startups in the country numbered barely a few hundred. The picture a decade later is entirely different in scale and in spread. More than 2,50,046 startups have now been recognised by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade. These young companies have reported the creation of over 25.42 lakh jobs across sectors. The most striking feature of the movement is its geography rather than its size alone. Startups are now present in more than 770 districts, which means nearly every district has at least one. Founders are emerging from small towns where salaried government work was once the only ambition. Women lead a large share of these recognised ventures, mainly in education, food and wellness. Recognition brings tax benefits, easier compliance and access to a government-backed fund of funds. Public procurement rules were relaxed so that young firms could bid for government orders. A national portal connects founders with mentors, investors and incubation centres in one place.\n\nThe decade also saw the state simplify the rules under which all businesses operate. Tens of thousands of compliances were reduced, and many minor offences were decriminalised for firms. Campus incubators now encourage students to test business ideas before they graduate. Deep-tech ventures in space, defence and semiconductors have begun to attract serious private capital. Challenges remain, mainly in early-stage funding outside the large metro regions. Many founders also struggle with delayed payments from large corporate buyers. Even so, the recognised startup has become a normal career choice for the Indian graduate. The movement has shifted the national imagination from seeking jobs to creating them. State governments have added their own startup policies and support funds. District-level startup cells now guide first-time founders through registration and basic paperwork."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Mission LiFE and mindful consumption", "text": "At the Glasgow climate conference in 2021, India placed an unusual idea before the world. Along with technology and finance, the Prime Minister proposed a change in everyday human behaviour. The idea was named LiFE, or Lifestyle for Environment, and it later grew into Mission LiFE. Its central argument is simple enough for any citizen to understand and apply. The planet is harmed less by what people need than by what they waste without thinking. Mindless consumption must therefore give way to mindful and deliberate use of resources. The movement describes its supporters as Pro-Planet People who adjust daily habits consciously. Switching off idle appliances, carrying a cloth bag and repairing goods are offered as starting points. Traditional Indian practices are presented as early examples of this philosophy in action. Earthen lamps, seasonal food and the repair of clothes all conserved resources long before the term existed.\n\nThe global launch of the movement took place in October 2022 in the presence of the UN Secretary-General. Since then, the idea has been carried into schools, offices and resident welfare groups. The mission lists simple actions across seven themes, from saving water to reducing e-waste. Government campaigns ask citizens to adopt these actions and record them on a public portal. Critics note that individual habits cannot substitute for industrial policy or clean energy investment. Supporters answer that demand shapes supply, and thrifty consumers push markets towards durable goods. The deeper claim of the mission is cultural rather than technical in nature. It asks a rising middle class to define progress by well-being rather than by how much they buy. Whether the phrase endures or not, the question it raises will outlive the campaign itself. Schools now hold weekly activities in which children practise these habits and carry them home. Village panchayats and city wards have begun to honour households that save water and power."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "National Quantum Mission", "text": "The National Quantum Mission, approved by the Union Cabinet in April 2023 and steered by the Department of Science and Technology, with an outlay of Rs 6,003.65 crore for the period up to 2030-31, commits India to a technology whose operating principles depart from those of classical computation. Quantum systems exploit superposition and entanglement, properties that allow certain problems in cryptography, materials simulation and optimisation to be addressed at speeds unattainable through conventional architectures. The mission therefore positions itself within a worldwide competition in which governments throughout the developed world have committed comparable resources.\n\nIts central engineering target is the construction of intermediate-scale quantum computers carrying 50 to 1000 physical qubits within eight years. Two technological platforms, superconducting circuits and photonic systems, are to be developed in parallel throughout the programme. Satellite-based quantum communication over distances exceeding 2,000 kilometres and inter-city quantum key distribution through trusted nodes form the communication objectives.\n\nQuantum sensing promises measurements of magnetic fields, gravity and time with a sensitivity that classical instruments cannot approach. Such sensitivity carries applications throughout navigation, mineral exploration, medical imaging and defence.\n\nImplementation rests upon four Thematic Hubs, established in leading institutions during 2024-25 and dedicated respectively to computing, communication, sensing and metrology, and materials and devices. Each hub coordinates researchers across universities and laboratories, while industry participation is sought for fabrication, cryogenics and control electronics. The mission documents acknowledge candidly that India entered this field behind the United States, China and several European programmes. Their wager is that concentrated funding, retained talent and patient institution-building can compress a decade of disadvantage into a shorter interval."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Production Linked Incentive schemes", "text": "The Production Linked Incentive framework, announced in 2020 with a combined outlay of Rs 1.91 lakh crore, inverted the traditional logic of industrial subsidy throughout the Indian economy. Instead of supporting investment proposals in advance, the State undertook to reward verified production and incremental sales after factories had delivered output. Fourteen sectors were selected, spanning electronics, pharmaceuticals, automobiles, telecommunications equipment, speciality steel, textiles, food processing and solar modules.\n\nThe design assumed that scale, rather than protection, was the binding constraint upon Indian manufacturing competitiveness.\n\nBy December 2025, some 836 applications had been approved under the schemes across the participating sectors. Cumulative investment reported by participating firms stood at Rs 2.16 lakh crore, against production and sales valued at Rs 20.41 lakh crore. Exports attributable to the schemes reached Rs 8.3 lakh crore, while employment, direct and indirect together, was reported at 14.39 lakh positions. Incentives actually disbursed amounted to Rs 28,748 crore, a fraction of the headline outlay, since payments follow audited output rather than announcements. Mobile telephones supply the clearest illustration, India having moved from net importer to an exporter to the United States within the scheme period.\n\nAssessments of the framework nevertheless remain divided among economists and industry associations throughout India. Critics observe that assembly has expanded faster than the domestic component ecosystems on which durable competitiveness depends. Delays in disbursement and shifting eligibility interpretations have themselves generated avoidable uncertainty for mid-sized participants, while supporters reply that supplier migration follows anchor investment with a lag, as the experience of China and Vietnam demonstrates. The schemes run into the next decade, and their final evaluation will turn upon whether subsidised scale graduates into unsubsidised capability."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "AGNIPATH scheme and Agniveers", "text": "The AGNIPATH scheme, unveiled in June 2022, restructured the intake of soldiers below officer rank throughout the Indian Armed Forces. Recruitment that had proceeded through periodic rallies under service-specific rules was replaced by a single framework under which volunteers, designated Agniveers, enrol for a four-year engagement. Candidates between 17.5 and 21 years of age may apply, and the first-year intake was fixed at 46,000 across the Army, the Navy and the Air Force. Enrolment is governed by the respective Service Acts, and training, allowances and risk benefits follow the terms announced at induction.\n\nThe financial architecture distinguishes the scheme from the pensionable service it partially replaces throughout the forces. Agniveers contribute 30 per cent of their monthly emoluments to a Seva Nidhi fund, matched by an equal government contribution throughout the engagement. On exit after four years, each Agniveer receives a Seva Nidhi package of Rs 11.71 lakh, accumulated with interest and exempt from income tax. Up to 25 per cent of each batch may be enrolled into the regular cadre, subject to merit and organisational requirements, for a further minimum engagement of 15 years.\n\nThe remainder depart with a skill certificate and documented service intended to assist civilian employment.\n\nThe government defended the design as a means of lowering the average age of the forces and containing the pension burden that increasingly constrains defence modernisation budgets. Reservation in central armed police forces and preferences announced by several State governments were presented as the absorption pathway for released Agniveers. Critics within and outside the veterans' community questioned the adequacy of four years for unit cohesion and the employment prospects awaiting discharged volunteers. The scheme's durability will ultimately be judged by outcomes for the cohorts themselves rather than by the arguments surrounding its announcement."}, {"tid": "cgl-b2-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan", "text": "The campaign against tuberculosis, consolidated under the TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan, records statistics that place India simultaneously at the centre of the global burden and at the centre of the global response. Over the past decade, TB incidence within the country has declined by 21 per cent and mortality by 25 per cent, against a worldwide decline of barely 12 per cent. Treatment coverage has risen to 92 per cent of estimated cases, narrowing the population of patients whom the system never reaches. These movements reflect a deliberate shift from passive detection towards active case-finding throughout vulnerable communities.\n\nThe intensified Abhiyaan conducted since December 2024 has screened more than 20 crore persons in high-risk populations throughout the states. Screening yielded 32.65 lakh detected cases, of which 10.9 lakh were asymptomatic patients identified only through chest radiography and molecular confirmation. \n\nGovernment funding for the programme expanded from Rs 640 crore in 2015-16 to Rs 6,356 crore in 2025-26, financing free diagnostics, drugs and nutritional support. Under the Nikshay Poshan Yojana, every notified patient receives Rs 1,000 monthly throughout treatment, transferred directly into bank accounts. Some 7.16 lakh Ni-kshay Mitras, comprising individuals, institutions and elected representatives, have adopted patients to supply additional nutritional and vocational support.\n\nThe remaining obstacles are concentrated in drug-resistant disease, undernutrition and the social stigma that still delays care-seeking in poorer households. Private practitioners, who treat a substantial share of patients, have been drawn into mandatory notification systems throughout urban India. Programme managers argue that the final phase demands convergence between health services, nutrition missions and municipal governments. The elimination target, set ahead of the global schedule, will test whether administrative intensity can be sustained after the campaign phase closes."}], "rrb": [{"tid": "rrb-b1-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A journey by train", "text": "The morning train stood ready at the crowded platform early. We reached the busy station and carried our heavy bags. Father bought the tickets for us at the booking window. We settled into our coach and found our seats quickly. Our coach was clean and tidy with broad and soft seats. A gentle breeze drifted in through the open coach windows. Soon the guard appeared, waving his green flag at everyone. The engine whistled loudly and the train began moving slowly. Villages and yellow fields slipped past our windows one by one. Meadows and rivers and little hamlets followed one another swiftly. Cattle grazed in the meadows beside the narrow winding track. Children ran beside the fences, waving gaily at the riders. Mother unpacked our tiffin and shared bananas with fellow riders. Father poured sweet tea from a flask into steel tumblers. I gazed at rivers shining silver under the morning sunlight.\nHeavy grey clouds gathered over the distant hills very slowly. Gentle rain pattered softly upon the carriage roof above us. Soon the sunlight returned and the wet meadows sparkled brightly. Farmers waved from their paddy fields as our train passed. We opened a story book and read it aloud together. An elderly man told us tales of his younger days. His gentle manner made the journey seem shorter and merrier. At midday we crossed a mighty river on an iron bridge. Little boats drifted gently upon the calm and shining river. Later our train halted briefly at a little town station. Vendors sold hot snacks and bananas upon the busy platform. We bought some bananas and ate them with great delight. The golden fields glowed softly as the evening sun sank low. At last our home town appeared and we gathered our heavy bags. We stepped down gladly, tired after our happy long journey. The family went home glad after that pleasant journey together."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The village pond", "text": "Our village pond lies beyond the ancient mango orchard nearby. Its waters remain calm, green and quiet in every season. Broad stone steps descend gently to the water at one corner. Village women arrive at daybreak carrying brass and steel vessels. Men bathe their buffaloes and cattle near the shallow margin. Little boys splash about and laugh loudly in the shallows. White ducks paddle in single file behind their mother duck. Lotus flowers open gently upon the still and glassy surface. Frogs croak upon broad lotus leaves after the evening rain. A banyan tree shades the whole bank with its branches. Elderly men gather there to discuss crops, weather and prices. Birds descend at evening to drink and splash at the margin. Silver fish rise and sparkle suddenly in the morning sun. The pond supplies water to every field around our village. Cattle amble down at dusk to drink the cool water.\nIn summer the pond shrinks slowly and its bed dries up. Wide cracks appear in the dark mud beside the steps. Boys play cricket matches upon the dry bed every evening. Then monsoon clouds arrive and heavy rain fills the pond. Little streams hurry in from every field and fill it. Soon the level rises and covers the bottom steps again. Green algae spreads over the surface in the rainy season. The pond appears fresh, full and lovely after the rains. At night the full moon shines upon the quiet water. Its light creates a silver pathway across the dark surface. We often sit upon the steps and watch this scene. The air feels cool and carries the smell of earth. Our elders remind us to keep the pond clean always. Nobody dumps rubbish or dirty water into the pond ever. The pond serves our whole village faithfully in many ways. We cherish our pond dearly and protect it with care."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Good manners in public places", "text": "Good manners make life pleasant for each and every person. We should speak softly and kindly to all around us. A smile and a greeting cost nothing but mean much. We should stand in a proper queue and await our turn. Pushing others in a crowd is rude and very wrong. We should help old people cross the busy road safely. We should offer our seat to an old person gladly. We must never spit or throw litter upon the road. We should always put waste paper and peels in dustbins. Loud talk in a hospital can disturb the sick people. We should keep our mobile phones silent in quiet places. It is polite to listen when another person is speaking. We must not interrupt others in the middle of talk. Saying please and thank you shows a gentle kind heart. We should hold the door open for those behind us.\nGood manners are needed most in buses and trains daily. We should never play loud music inside a shared coach. Keeping our feet off the seats keeps them clean too. We must speak with respect to drivers and other staff. A polite request gets help much faster than rude words. We should thank the driver when we leave the bus. In the market we must not cheat or shout loudly. Fair dealing and honest words win trust from all people. We should care for public parks and keep benches clean. Flowers in a garden are for all to enjoy together. Plucking them for oneself spoils the joy of other people. True manners come from a kind and caring inner heart. Children learn manners by watching elders at home and school. Elders should set a good example in their daily conduct. A well mannered person is welcome in every place always. Good manners cost little but they earn respect everywhere daily."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The seasons of India", "text": "India has three main seasons during the whole calendar year. Summer arrives first, bringing hot days and warm dry winds. The summer sun shines fiercely and the earth feels very hot. People wear light cotton garments and drink water very often. Mangoes and melons ripen in the heat of this season. Children enjoy their holidays and play in the cool evening shade. Then dark rain clouds gather and the monsoon season approaches. Rain falls heavily and fills the rivers and ponds again. Farmers happily sow their seeds in the wet soft fields. Green crops cover the land as far as anyone sees. Frogs sing at night and peacocks dance in the rain. The air turns cool and fresh after each heavy rain shower. Swollen rivers often flood the low fields near their small villages. People carry umbrellas and wear coats when they go out. Winter follows the rains with cold winds and clear blue skies.\nCold winds blow down from the snowy mountain hills in winter. The nights grow longer and the days become quite short. Families sit near fires and drink hot tea at night. Warm woollen clothes keep us safe from the cold winter air. Fog covers the fields and roads on many frosty cold mornings. Oranges and apples fill the markets in this fine season. Farmers harvest their crops and store the grain with care. Every season brings its own gifts and its own special duties. Summer gives ripe fruits and the rains give us sweet water. Winter gives us rest and cool days for hard daily work. Life in India follows the slow and steady rhythm of these seasons. People plan their farming and festivals by the turning year. Crops and clothes and food change with each new season always. Yet every season has beauty for those who watch very closely. We should enjoy each season and respect its great natural power. The turning seasons teach us patience and hope every year."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Forests and the animals that live in them", "text": "Forests cover about 21.7 per cent of the land and they shape the climate of whole regions today. Tall sal and teak trees stand beside dense evergreen groves in the wetter districts throughout the country. In the dry west, thorn forests of acacia and khejri survive on scant rain and sandy soil. The Sundarbans shelter mangrove forests where roots stand in salt water and mud flats shine at noon. High in the Himalayas, forests of pine, deodar and oak clothe the cold slopes above green valleys. Thick undergrowth of ferns, creepers and shrubs gives small creatures safe hiding places from watchful predators and invertebrates nearby. Fallen leaves rot slowly on the forest floor and feed the soil that feeds the trees.\nBengal tigers, elephants and wild buffalo graze in clearings while leopards watch silently from the long grass. The forests of Gir still protect lions, and Kaziranga guards the great one horned rhinoceros of Assam. Monkeys swing through the branches, and woodpeckers tap dead trunks in search of insects hidden inside bark. Bright butterflies, kingfishers and hornbills add colour to forests that shelter nearly 80,000 kinds of animal life. Many animals are nocturnal hunters, while others feed by day and rest through the hottest hours quietly. Frogs, snakes and crocodiles haunt the forest streams, and fish dart beneath roots along muddy banksides and backwaters. Each species fits a place in the food web, from tiny termites to the largest carnivores alive.\nForests give people timber, honey, resins and medicines, and they hold rainwater in soil and roots. Yet deforestation, forest fires and poaching have destroyed wide tracts and pushed many species towards extinction today. Sanctuaries and national parks in the Western Ghats now protect breeding grounds, migration corridors and the last old growth stands. Villagers, guards and students have joined hands in reforestation drives that plant native saplings each monsoon season. If these forests survive with their animals, future generations will themselves inherit a living treasure beyond all price."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The mobile phone and society", "text": "The mobile phone has changed daily life in India more deeply than any other recent invention here now. Two decades ago a telephone call meant visiting a booth, but today phones connect 900 million users. Farmers check mandi prices each week on their smartphones before they carry their harvest towards the nearest town market. Students attend online classes, watch recorded lessons and download books through applications that cost very little money. Office workers answer messages within 5 minutes, and business now moves at a pace once thought impossible. Through UPI, a vegetable seller in a small town accepts instant payment without handling any paper cash. Families separated by work stay close through video calls that carry faces, voices and festivals across great distances.\nGrandparents now send photographs of grandchildren, and Diwali greetings travel through phones faster than posted cards did. In emergencies, one quick call brings an ambulance, the police or neighbours rushing towards the right house at once. Women running small businesses take orders, confirm deliveries and accept UPI payments from buyers in far off towns. The phone is also a bank, a map, a torch, a newspaper and a camera together. Entertainment follows us everywhere, since songs, serials and cricket matches stream onto small screens throughout the whole long day. Yet this constant connection carries costs that households across the country have begun to notice, talk through and discuss.\nChildren spend long hours on games while their attention weakens and outdoor play slowly loses its place. Family conversations shrink when every member stares at a glowing screen during meals and evening rest together. False messages spread quickly through WhatsApp groups, and checking facts before sharing has now become a shared duty today. Many schools in Delhi now restrict phones in classrooms so that lessons proceed without needless distraction daily. Used with clear boundaries, the phone remains a servant of society rather than its silent new harsh master. Across India, the wise course is balance, enjoying its benefits while protecting attention, health and real warm company."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The great rivers of India", "text": "The great rivers of India begin mostly in high mountains and journey slowly towards the distant seas beyond. The Ganga rises high in the Himalayas and flows 2,525 kilometres across the northern plains towards the east. Along its course the Yamuna, the Ramganga and the Ghaghara join it with waters from many tributaries. The Brahmaputra enters from the north east, runs 2,900 kilometres in all, and floods Assam every monsoon. These rivers carry rich silt from the mountains and spread it over floodplains that farmers prize greatly. Their wide alluvial plains grow rice, wheat and sugarcane for cultivation in hundreds of towns and villages. Boats have carried grain, salt and timber along these rivers since trade began in early times.\nIn the peninsula, the Godavari flows 1,400 kilometres and earns love as a sacred southern stream today. The Krishna and the Kaveri water the dry plateau lands and fill tanks in towns and fields. The Narmada runs westwards between rocky hills and reaches the sea through a broad and deep estuary. Dams on these rivers store monsoon water, turn turbines for electricity and feed canals in dry months. Irrigation from river canals has turned thirsty districts green and raised harvests across generations of farming families. River water also fills city reservoirs, and households depend on rivers far more than they realise daily.\nPilgrims gather on river ghats at dawn, and celebrations along the banks join faith with flowing water. Fisheries in these rivers feed lakhs of families, while dolphins, turtles and crocodiles still survive in quieter stretches today. Yet untreated sewage, industrial waste and plastic now poison stretches that once ran clear and sweet. Sand mining weakens banks, embankments fail in floods, and channels grow shallow where silt settles unchecked within. Cleaning the rivers demands strict laws, working treatment plants and respect from every citizen who uses them. Guarded with care, these rivers will keep nourishing the land and its people for centuries to come."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The Olympic Games", "text": "The Olympic Games are the greatest sports festival on earth, joining nations in peaceful competition every four years. Their roots lie in ancient Greece, where athletes raced at Olympia in honour of their gods long ago. The modern Games began at Athens in 1896 after a nobleman revived the old idea with great success. Since then the Games have grown steadily, and recent editions have welcomed about 11,000 athletes from everywhere. The programme now covers 33 sports, from sprinting and swimming to gymnastics, wrestling and weightlifting events included today. Host cities build stadiums, pools and villages, and millions of spectators watch the ceremonies with great excitement worldwide. The opening procession, the oath and the lighting of the flame express friendship, excellence and fair play.\nFor athletes, reaching the Olympics demands years of preparation, discipline and sacrifice that few outsiders ever see clearly. Training begins before dawn in small towns, where coaches correct technique and families arrange food, rest and travel. A single final may last seconds, yet it carries the hopes of an entire sporting generation behind it. Medals bring honour, but personal records and honest effort also earn respect from competitors and crowds alike. Indian athletes have won medals in hockey, shooting, wrestling and boxing, raising hopes across the whole country. Their victories inspire schoolchildren to take up sport and demand better grounds, coaches and equipment in towns. Television and the internet carry every race into homes throughout the world within moments of its finish.\nThe Games also teach hard lessons about defeat, patience and the courage to begin training once again. Doping and corruption have troubled the movement, and strict testing now guards the fairness of every contest. Hosts must control costs carefully, since grand stadiums can burden a city long after all the crowds depart. At their best, the Games unite strangers who cheer together and forget their quarrels for a fortnight. That spirit of unity through sport explains why the Olympic flame still draws the world each new cycle."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Ocean currents and the monsoon", "text": "The great ocean currents of the Indian Ocean themselves move surface waters northwards and southwards in broad circuits throughout the seasons of the year. Along the Somali Coast and the East African Coast, cold upwelling currents lift deep waters rich in nourishment towards the sunlit surface layers throughout springtime. Each summer the Somali Current strengthens, reverses its northward flow and races swiftly along the steep coastlines at speeds approaching 1.8 metres a second. This cold stream cools the Arabian Sea, holds back the coastal rainfall and warns forecasters that the monsoon circulation may shift southwards for weeks.\nMeanwhile the tropical waters of the Bay of Bengal grow hotter throughout April and May, and surface heat climbs steadily beyond 30 degrees Celsius. Moisture gathers above this warm pool, and low pressure deepens over the vast mainland while high pressure strengthens south of the distant equatorial belt. The pressure contrast drives cross-winds from the southern Indian Ocean, and these winds swing westwards throughout their long passage before they strike the coastlines. When these laden winds meet the steep barrier of the Western Ghats, they rise, cool and shed rainfall throughout the coastal grasslands and foothills below them.\nInland, the monsoon trough stretches from the heated northwestern plains towards the Bay of Bengal, and thunderstorms cluster along its whole length for weeks. Rainfall totals themselves differ sharply, since Cherrapunji and Mawsynram record near 1,200 centimetres while parched northwestern districts receive barely 25 centimetres in some years. The Indian Meteorological Department tracks these currents with satellites, weather ships and instruments, and its forecasts guide working households throughout the sowing season ahead.\nOcean heat also steers the season, for currents store sunshine throughout springtime and release warmth slowly while households and reservoirs await the first rains. In 2023 the department recorded that a warm pool near the equator had shifted rainfall patterns across 15 southeastern districts of the dry peninsula. Thus currents, winds and rainfall form one interlinked system, and small shifts offshore can reshape harvests, fisheries and livelihoods throughout the vast mainland."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The archaeology of the Indus valley", "text": "Across the broad floodplains of the Indus Valley, farming settlements grew into planned cities whose street grids still astonish modern scholars throughout the world. The earliest towns rose around 3300 BCE beside river channels that brought snowmelt, silt and trade goods downstream towards the waiting great settlements themselves. By 2600 BCE the mature cities of Mohenjo Daro and Harappa held granaries, storehouses and workshops, neatly arranged throughout their crowded, brick walled quarters and lanes. When John Marshall announced the finds in 1922, the Archaeological Survey of India began careful digging that uncovered streets, drains and household wells.\nThe Great Bath at Mohenjo Daro, built with bitumen coated bricks and staircases on two sides, shows craftsmanship planned for large public gatherings and rites. Standardised burnt bricks measuring 7 by 14 by 28 centimetres appear throughout these cities, their shared proportions underscoring frameworks of common building practice everywhere. Covered drains ran beneath lanes barely 1.5 metres wide, household bathrooms fed wastewater through terracotta pipes, and wells stood within courtyards throughout the densest old brick neighbourhoods. At Lothal a dockyard linked the settlement with river trade, while seals carved from steatite travelled downstream towards distant ports across the open sea.\nCraftworkers shaped beads of carnelian, tools of copper and bronze, and shell ornaments whose workmanship matches pieces found throughout Mesopotamia and the Gulf beyond. Dholavira, set between seasonal streams, stored rainwater in rock cut reservoirs and channels that guarded households against drought throughout long hot rainless summers. Rakhigarhi spreads across 350 hectares in the plains of Haryana, and its mounds still conceal granaries, workshops and burial grounds awaiting slow careful excavation.\nAfter 1900 BCE the great cities thinned as rivers shifted, trade weakened and households drifted towards smaller settlements in the far eastern plains. No single cause explains the change, since climate shifts, weakening counterparts in trade and stressed farmlands all pressed upon the proud cities themselves. Today the Archaeological Survey of India and UNESCO guard these sites, while new surveys, satellite photographs and laboratory tests sharpen old frameworks of knowledge."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "India's changing energy mix", "text": "For decades coal fired power stations supplied most of India's electricity, and smoky plants themselves near the coalfields shaped landscapes, livelihoods and households alike. In 2010 the National Solar Mission set targets countrywide for rooftop panels and solar parks, and planners in Rajasthan and Gujarat mapped wastelands for arrays. Costs then dropped downwards as factories scaled up, auctions sharpened prices worldwide and solar tariffs slid towards 2.5 rupees for each unit sold. By 2024 the country had crossed 200 gigawatts of renewable capacity, and solar, wind and hydropower together pressed against the strongholds of Coal India.\nCoal still supplies 55 per cent of all power, since hundreds of stations across India run throughout the night and underpin factories themselves, households and railways. Yet the mix keeps broadening, for wind turbines along the shorelines of Tamil Nadu and Gujarat now feed demand across three states at dusk. High in Ladakh, thin air and fierce sunshine suit solar fields, while reservoirs in the headwaters of Himalayan rivers steady hydropower output throughout the winter months. Biogas from cattle dung, geothermal pilots in rocky districts and tidal experiments along two coastlines add growing shares throughout this decade.\nThe problem is timing, since sunshine fades at nightfall while demand peaks, and grid frameworks must balance fluctuating supply against shifting household demand daily. Batteries, pumped reservoirs and stronger transmission lines answer this gap, and the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy funds substations throughout the wide countryside. Green hydrogen pilot plants near ports and steel towns store surplus power for weeks, turning windy nights into fuel for heavy lorries and ships themselves.\nThe national target of 500 gigawatts of clean capacity by 2030 now guides auctions, installations and grid frameworks themselves throughout the power sector. India has pledged net zero by 2070, and the International Solar Alliance, launched from Indian soil, spreads solar frameworks amongst tropical countries throughout the world. The transition will test households and factories throughout the land, yet cleaner power promises steadier bills, clearer skies and energy security for coming generations."}, {"tid": "rrb-b1-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "How the body adapts to high altitude", "text": "When lowlanders climb towards settlements at 3,500 metres in Ladakh, the thin mountain air starves their bloodstream of oxygen throughout the cold Ladakh nighttimes. Breathing quickens at once, the heartbeat strengthens and the body flushes its cheeks as Sherpa climbers strain to draw deeper breaths through dry windpipes. Headaches, dizziness and broken sleep trouble most newcomers themselves, since the brain dislikes shortage and signals sharp distress throughout sleeplessness and fading appetites. Guides in Leh therefore plan staircases of ascent, and wiser parties sleep lower than their highest climb throughout the first slow fortnights in Ladakh.\nWithin 20 days the bone-marrow answers, and red cells themselves thicken the bloodstream so that each heartbeat carries more oxygen throughout the Himalayan heights. Haemoglobin, the red pigment inside those cells, rises by nearly 2.5 grammes in each decilitre as the adjustment deepens throughout the slow Ladakh weeks ahead. Breath by breath the lungs adapt, and climbers draw in more air at rest while their chest frameworks strengthen throughout the long Himalayan season. Blood vessels in the lungs narrow under low oxygen, a response that safeguards fluid balance yet strains the heart throughout long, hard Himalayan climbs.\nPopulations born in the Andes, the Ethiopian Highlands and the Tibetan Plateau carry inherited adjustments that lowlanders from India cannot imitate within a few weeks. Sherpa mountaineers of the Himalayas, whose households have farmed high-altitude settlements for centuries, show calm breathing and stronger heartbeats at the greatest Everest heights. In 1953 Hillary and Tenzing reached the summit of Mount Everest at 8,848 metres, yet even their superb fitness rested on weeks of staging.\nAbove 5,000 metres the body consumes itself, appetite fades, muscles themselves thin and even highlanders still rest often throughout their long working days there. Descending remains the surest remedy, since blood oxygen near 85 per cent at height climbs back towards normal within hours in the Leh valleys below. Understanding these thresholds helps planners themselves design frameworks for roads, mines and observatories throughout the Himalayas, and teaches mountaineers to respect the unforgiving heights."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Vande Bharat Express trains", "text": "The Vande Bharat Express is a modern train made in our own country. The first such train ran between the capital and a famous temple city. It was flagged off with great pride by the whole nation. Since then, these trains have spread to almost every state. More than one hundred and sixty services now run each week. They connect big cities with smaller towns in every region. Over two hundred and seventy districts are touched by these trains. The train is built fully in our own factories by our own engineers. Its body is sleek and its colours are bright and fresh. Passengers feel happy when they see it arrive at the platform.\n\nThe train is full of features that make travel easy and safe. Its doors open and close on their own at each station. The seats can turn to face the direction of travel. The toilets are clean and work without water wastage. Each coach has cameras fitted for the safety of passengers. A screen shows the speed and the name of the next station. The train can run fast but it also stops very smoothly. Safety systems watch the track ahead all the time. The driver gets alerts well before any danger point. Last year, the train reached the valley from the plains for the first time. People there had waited for this day for a very long time. A journey in this train saves time on every route. Trips that took a full day are now done in some hours. Students use it to reach exams in far off cities. Traders use it to visit markets and return the same day. Patients reach big hospitals in comfort and on time. Tourists find it easy to plan short trips on weekends. The railways plan to add many more such trains soon. New routes will link more towns in the coming years. This train has become a symbol of a new and confident nation. It shows what our own skill can build for our own people."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Metro rail growth in Indian cities", "text": "A metro train is now a common sight in our big cities. It runs on tracks raised above the busy roads below. Some lines also run in tunnels deep under the ground. The coaches are clean, cool and bright from inside. People of all ages use the metro for daily travel. Office workers reach their offices on time every morning. Students use it to reach schools and colleges with ease. The fare is low and the trains come every few minutes. No one has to wait long on the wide platforms. A smart card or a phone ticket opens the entry gates.\n\nAbout a decade ago, metros ran in only five cities. Their total length was less than two hundred and fifty kilometres. Today the network has grown many times over in size. Metro lines now run in twenty six cities of the country. Their total length is more than a thousand kilometres. India now has the third largest metro network in the world. More than a crore passengers ride the metro every day. The yearly budget for metro projects has also grown greatly. New lines are under work in many more cities today. Trains on some busy routes now run without a driver. The metro has changed the way city people think of travel. Roads below carry fewer cars and buses than before. The air of the city is a little cleaner as a result. Travel time is fixed and no traffic jam can delay a metro. Women and elderly people find the metro safe and easy. Shops and offices grow fast near the new stations. Land near a metro line gains value in a few years. Rain or heat does not trouble the metro passenger at all. The city also saves fuel when people choose the train. A metro line is costly to build but serves lakhs for decades. It is now the backbone of travel in a modern Indian city."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "PM-eBus Sewa electric buses", "text": "The city bus is the daily vehicle of the common man. In many towns, though, buses are old, few and irregular. People wait for long hours at stops in heat and rain. The central government has launched a scheme to change this. It will put ten thousand new electric buses on city roads. These buses will run in one hundred and sixty nine cities. The buses will be air conditioned and fully electric. They will be run by private firms under a partnership model. The firms will buy, run and maintain the buses for years. The government will support the running cost for ten years. Cities with more than three lakh people can join the scheme. All capital cities of states and hill regions are covered.\n\nAn electric bus makes no smoke and very little noise. It costs less to run than a diesel bus each day. The battery is charged at depots built for the scheme. New depots and power lines will come up in every city. Towns that never had a bus service will get priority. Such towns will see an organised bus service for the first time. The scheme will create thousands of direct jobs in cities. Drivers, mechanics and depot staff will be hired in large numbers. Women drivers and conductors will also get a fair chance. Old diesel buses will slowly leave the roads of these towns. For the passenger, the change will be felt every day. Buses will come on time and at fixed gaps all day. A mobile app will show where the bus is right now. Clean and cool buses will draw people away from private vehicles. Roads will then carry less traffic in peak hours. The air of the city will get cleaner year by year. Students and workers will save money on daily travel. The scheme treats the humble city bus with new respect. It sees public transport as a right of every citizen. In time, the electric bus may become the face of our towns."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Namo Bharat Delhi-Meerut RRTS", "text": "Namo Bharat trains now link Delhi with Meerut in under an hour. The full line between the two cities is eighty two kilometres long. The whole corridor was opened to the nation this year. The train runs much faster than a normal local train. Earlier, the same road journey took two to three hours. Buses and cars on the highway move slowly in traffic. The new train has ended that daily struggle for thousands. A part of the line had opened a few years ago. People liked it so much that they waited for the full line. The service is the first of its kind in the whole country.\n\nThe train is built to run at a very high speed. Its coaches are wide, bright and fully air conditioned. Seats are soft and there is space for standing passengers too. Big windows give a clear view of fields and towns outside. The stations are modern and have lifts and escalators. Screens show the time of the next train at all hours. The line is run by a special corporation for the region. The project cost many thousands of crores of rupees. The local metro trains of the second city share the same tracks. They use the same stations as the fast trains as well. This sharing of one line by two services is new for us. The new link is changing life in the whole region fast. Workers from the smaller city can take up jobs in the capital. They return home the same evening to their families. Students reach colleges in the capital without leaving home. Shops near the stations see more buyers each month. Land values along the line have risen in a short time. Fewer cars on the highway means cleaner air for all. More such corridors are planned for other busy routes. This service shows the future of travel between nearby cities. Fast, clean and on time is its simple promise to people."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-05", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Dedicated Freight Corridors", "text": "Freight trains in India long shared their tracks with passenger trains. The result was predictable: goods trains waited in loops while express trains passed, and cargo moved slowly across the country. The Dedicated Freight Corridor programme was conceived to end this structural conflict on the busiest routes. It built separate double-line corridors on which only freight trains would run, at higher speeds and with heavier loads. The network now stands complete at 2,843 kilometres after the final western sections were opened. The Eastern Corridor runs 1,337 kilometres from Ludhiana to Sonnagar. The Western Corridor covers 1,506 kilometres from Dadri near Delhi to Jawaharlal Nehru Port. A special purpose company was created to build and operate the system as a single national programme.\n\nThe engineering of the corridors differs visibly from that of the ordinary network. Bridges and formation are built for much heavier axle loads than conventional lines permit. Double-stack container trains, impossible on ordinary routes, run routinely on the western corridor. A single such train carries freight equal to more than 250 trucks on the highway. Electric locomotives haul longer rakes at steady speeds without the constant braking of mixed traffic. On an average day, over four hundred freight trains now use the two corridors. Transit times between the northern hinterland and the western ports have fallen sharply as a consequence. Coal, foodgrains, cement, fertilisers and containers form the bulk of the traffic carried. Planners expect logistics costs to decline as industry shifts long-haul cargo from road to rail. A further eastern corridor has been proposed in a recent budget speech. Private logistics firms have begun building warehouses and terminals along the completed corridors. The completed network is therefore best read as the first stage of a longer transformation. Training programmes for loco pilots and maintenance staff accompany the expansion of traffic each year."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-06", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Border Roads Organisation", "text": "The Border Roads Organisation was raised on 7 May 1960 to build infrastructure in the remotest corners of the country. It works under the Ministry of Defence, but its roads serve the civilian population as directly as they serve the armed forces. Over six decades, the organisation has constructed more than 64,100 kilometres of roads in border areas. It has also built over eleven hundred bridges, several tunnels and more than twenty airfields. Its field projects operate across eleven states and three union territories, from the western deserts to the highest snow-bound passes. The organisation has also executed road works in several friendly foreign countries over the decades. Its motto, Shramena Sarvam Sadhyam, declares that everything is achievable through hard work.\n\nThe works list of recent years reads like a catalogue of difficult geography. Its longest road tunnel, at 9.02 kilometres, carries traffic under a high pass through the year. Another tunnel in the eastern hills keeps a strategic road open even in heavy snow. In the last financial year, the organisation recorded its highest-ever expenditure on works. Snow clearance on high passes is a standing winter duty performed with specialised machines. Roads built for strategic reasons routinely transform the economy of the valleys they enter. Villages gain access to markets, hospitals and schools that were earlier days away on foot. Tourism follows the road into regions that outsiders could barely reach a generation ago. Local youth find employment as the organisation hires heavily from project areas. Camps of the organisation run schools, medical posts and water supply for villages near the work sites. For many border villages, its doctor and its road are the first services of the state they ever see. The border road is thus simultaneously an instrument of defence and of development. Its builders work where altitude, cold and landslides test both men and machines every single season."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-07", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Inland waterways revival", "text": "India moved goods along its rivers for centuries before the railway age arrived. That inheritance was largely abandoned during the twentieth century, and water transport shrank to a few localised pockets. The present revival began with the declaration of 111 National Waterways across twenty-three states and four union territories. A national authority is the nodal agency charged with developing and regulating this network. Operational waterways have increased from barely three a decade ago to thirty-two at present. Cargo carried on these waterways rose from 18 million tonnes in 2013-14 to 145.84 million tonnes last year. Terminals for loading and unloading have grown from fifteen to twenty-five in the same period. Luxury river cruise vessels on Indian rivers have increased from five to twenty-five.\n\nThe economics explain why governments are investing in this unfashionable mode of transport. Moving a tonne of cargo by water costs a fraction of the cost of moving it by road. Fuel consumption and emissions per tonne-kilometre are likewise far lower on a river vessel. The Jalvahak scheme supports the shift by reimbursing up to thirty-five per cent of operating costs for cargo moved on selected waterways. Dredging maintains navigable depth on the Ganga and Brahmaputra through the dry season. Modern terminals at Varanasi, Sahibganj and Haldia anchor the eastern waterway corridor. Community jetties along the banks serve the small traders and farmers of riverine villages. Projects worth more than fifteen hundred crore rupees have been identified for the next phase of development. The waterways council, which met at Kochi this year, coordinates investment between the Centre and the states. Passenger movement on the rivers has also grown quietly alongside the cargo traffic. New jetties are bringing daily ferry services to towns that depended entirely on crowded roads. If the present growth continues, the river will again become a working highway of Indian commerce."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-08", "diff": "medium", "topic": "One District One Product", "text": "Every district of India possesses a product for which it has been known for generations. It may be a weave, a spice, a fruit, a craft or a processed food. The One District One Product initiative was launched to convert this scattered reputation into organised income. Under the programme, 1,243 products have been selected from 775 districts across the country. The selected items span textiles, agriculture, food processing, handicrafts and several other sectors. Each district administration prepares an action plan for its chosen product. Support covers common facility centres, testing laboratories, packaging units and marketing assistance. The initiative works jointly with the Districts as Export Hubs programme of the commerce ministry. Export promotion committees at state and district level identify buyers in overseas markets.\n\nThe intention is to correct a familiar imbalance in the rural economy. Producers in small towns traditionally sold raw material cheaply to traders from large cities. Branding and packaging under a district identity allow the value addition to remain at home. A dedicated section on the Government e-Marketplace, the ODOP Bazaar, lists these products for institutional buyers. Products carrying a Geographical Indication tag receive particular attention under the programme. Training programmes teach artisans the quality standards that export markets demand. E-commerce onboarding has carried district products to customers who would never visit the district itself. Challenges persist in logistics, in consistent quality and in the working capital available to small producers. Even so, the programme has given district administrations a concrete economic agenda beyond routine administration. It treats local skill not as a relic to be preserved but as an asset to be sold. State governments now organise buyer meets and exhibitions where district producers negotiate directly with exporters. Financial institutions have designed small working-capital loans suited to the production cycles of these units. The measure of its success will be the incomes of the weavers, farmers and craft workers behind the labels."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-09", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Deep Ocean Mission and Samudrayaan", "text": "The Deep Ocean Mission, approved by the Union Cabinet in June 2021 with an outlay of Rs 4,077 crore, extends the national scientific establishment into the least examined territory under Indian jurisdiction. Its components range across deep-sea mining reconnaissance, ocean climate services, marine biodiversity documentation and the engineering of crewed submersibles. The mission is implemented by the Ministry of Earth Sciences through institutions that include the National Institute of Ocean Technology at Chennai. Scientific opinion throughout the participating departments holds that the mineral and biological wealth of the deep ocean will acquire strategic weight throughout this century, and the mission funds the ships, submersibles and sensors through which that examination will proceed.\n\nThe Samudrayaan programme supplies the mission with its most demanding artefact, the crewed submersible MATSYA-6000. The vehicle is designed to carry three persons to a depth of 6,000 metres and to sustain them for twelve hours in normal operation. Its personnel sphere, fabricated from titanium alloy of 80 millimetre thickness through electron-beam welding, measures 2.1 metres in internal diameter. The sphere was developed in collaboration with the space establishment, whose experience with pressure vessels informed the design. Wet trials of the complete vehicle were conducted in harbour waters during January and February 2025 at Kattupalli, while preparatory dives accumulated operating experience throughout the team.\n\nTwo scientists of the institute descended to 4,025 metres\n\nThe remotely operated vehicle ROSUB 6000 has separately been tested to 5,289 metres over the polymetallic nodule fields. Survey instruments have mapped the allotted nodule site at depths near 5,271 metres in the Central Indian Ocean. Emergency endurance of ninety-six hours, redundant life-support systems and conservative certification protocols govern every stage of the descent programme. India's deep-ocean capability, if these trials culminate successfully, will place the country within a small group of states that operate crewed vehicles at such depths."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-10", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Mumbai-Ahmedabad bullet train", "text": "The Mumbai-Ahmedabad High Speed Rail corridor, under construction since its foundation ceremony in September 2017, will carry the first bullet trains operated anywhere in India. The corridor extends over 508 kilometres through Maharashtra, Gujarat and the Union Territory of Dadra and Nagar Haveli. Trains designed for 350 kilometres per hour will operate at 320, reducing the inter-city journey to one hour and fifty-eight minutes on limited-stop services. Twelve stations structure the route, from the subterranean terminal at Bandra Kurla Complex through Thane, Surat and Vadodara to Sabarmati. The implementing corporation, the National High Speed Rail Corporation Limited, executes the project with Japanese Shinkansen technology and financing, while training Indian engineers in the operating disciplines that the technology demands.\n\nThe engineering inventory explains both the cost accumulated and the time consumed throughout construction. Approximately 465 kilometres of the alignment run upon viaducts above the densely occupied coastal plain. A tunnel of 21 kilometres, including 7 kilometres beneath Thane Creek, required undersea excavation techniques unfamiliar to Indian contractors, while the Mumbai terminal descends some thirty metres underground within one of the most congested commercial districts in Asia. Track construction employs the Japanese slab system, installed for the first time throughout any Indian railway, and expenditure had reached Rs 86,939 crore by December 2025 against the sanctioned project cost.\n\nOfficial schedules now target the opening of services in August 2027, initially upon the Surat-Vapi section. Skeptics throughout the engineering press observe that land acquisition disputes and systems integration have displaced every earlier deadline, while the government answers that viaduct completion, station structures and track laying now advance simultaneously across the alignment.\n\nWhatever the precise opening date the corridor constitutes a technology-transfer programme as much as a transport project. Its operating discipline, maintenance standards and passenger expectations will reset the benchmark against which the legacy railway measures itself."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-11", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Chenab Bridge and USBRL", "text": "The Chenab Bridge, inaugurated in June 2025 upon the Udhampur-Srinagar-Baramulla Rail Link, stands 359 metres above the riverbed of the Chenab in Reasi district. That elevation exceeds the height of the Eiffel Tower by approximately 35 metres, making the structure the highest railway arch bridge anywhere in the world. The bridge extends 1,315 metres across the gorge between the hillsides at Kauri and Bakkal, with a main arch span of 467 metres. It was executed by the Konkan Railway Corporation Limited at a cost of Rs 1,486 crore. Its design life is assessed at 120 years, an unusually long horizon for infrastructure in such terrain.\n\nThe structural system had to answer conditions that have defeated lesser designs throughout the Himalayan region. Wind speeds of 260 kilometres per hour are accommodated within the design calculations, alongside the seismic forces of one of India's most active earthquake zones. The arch was assembled from both banks through cable cranes of exceptional capacity, its two halves meeting over the gorge with centimetre precision. Blast considerations, exceptional for a civilian railway structure, entered the design specification because of the strategic character of the route. Paint systems and inspection arrangements were selected to function throughout decades of freeze-thaw cycles at altitude.\n\nThe bridge completes the operational logic of the 272 kilometre USBRL project, executed at a cost approaching Rs 43,780 crore through 36 tunnels aggregating 119 kilometres and 943 bridges, and the Anji Bridge, India's first cable-stayed railway bridge, was inaugurated on the same occasion.\n\nVande Bharat services between Katra and Srinagar Strategists throughout the government describe the link as a permanent all-weather alternative to the single highway connecting Kashmir with the remainder of the country. For the residents of the valley, the railway terminates a dependence upon that highway which winter closures had repeatedly exposed."}, {"tid": "rrb-b2-12", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Railway electrification near completion", "text": "The broad-gauge network of Indian Railways has advanced its electrification to 99.6 per cent, a threshold that converts a century-old mixed-traction system into an almost uniformly electric railway. Between 2014 and 2026, some 48,072 route kilometres were electrified, against 21,801 route kilometres accomplished during the entire period before 2014. A comparison published by the International Union of Railways in June 2025 placed only Switzerland, at 100 per cent, ahead of India among major railway systems. The remaining unelectrified fragments lie principally upon low-density routes where conversion economics remain marginal throughout the network.\n\nThe financial and environmental consequences register throughout the operating accounts of the railway. Diesel consumption for traction declined from 293 crore litres in 2015-16 to 108 crore litres in 2024-25, releasing recurring savings across every operating division. Electric traction also recovers energy through regenerative braking upon descending gradients, a facility diesel traction cannot replicate. The railway has simultaneously commissioned approximately 1,161 megawatts of solar capacity and 103 megawatts of wind capacity to green its purchased electricity. Assessments prepared by NITI Aayog for the Government of India estimate that rail transport emits approximately 89 per cent less carbon dioxide per unit of freight than road transport.\n\nCompletion of the programme reorders the strategic dependencies of the transport sector throughout the country. Petroleum imports cease to condition the marginal cost of railway operations, while exposure shifts towards the coal and renewable mix of the electricity grid. Maintenance establishments of the Ministry of Railways, organised around diesel locomotives, must themselves convert towards electric fleets and their distinct skill requirements, while operating departments contemplate uniform locomotive deployment across divisions that earlier maintained separate traction pools.\n\nDepot towns built around diesel sheds face an economic transition of their own as fleets change over. The electrification programme thus closes as an energy project whose consequences will be worked out within railway operations for decades."}], "hcm": [{"tid": "hcm-b1-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "My daily routine", "text": "I wake up early in the morning each day. I wash my face and brush my teeth first. Then I drink a glass of warm water slowly. I go for a short walk near my home. The fresh air makes me feel calm and fresh. I come back home and take a quick bath. My mother gives me a hot cup of tea. I eat bread and eggs for my first meal. Then I sit at my desk to read. I read my books with care for some time. I write notes in a small book each day. I help my mother with small work at home. I sweep the floor of my room with a broom. I keep my books and pens in their place. At noon we all eat lunch as one family. We talk about our day at the table. I rest for a short while after lunch. I then go out to meet my close friends. We play in the open field near our homes.\nI wash my hands and face well in the evening. I light a small lamp and sit to study. I do my class work with full care and focus. My father asks me about my day at work. I tell him all the things I did that day. We eat our night meal at the same time daily. The food is simple but warm and fresh always. I walk in our small yard after the meal. The cool air at night feels soft and calm. I plan the next day in my mind slowly. I write the plan in my small note book. I then brush my teeth and wash my feet. I say good night to my mother and father. I go to bed at the same time each night. Good sleep gives me strength for the next day. A fixed routine keeps my life calm and neat. It helps me do all my work on time. I feel happy when my day goes as planned. A good routine is the key to a good life. I also keep my clothes ready the night before. This saves time and keeps my morning free from rush. I fold my shirt and keep it on the chair. I shine my shoes and keep them by the door. A calm start makes the whole day feel light. I take short breaks when I work for long hours. A short rest keeps my mind fresh and sharp. I end my day with a sense of calm and peace."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The park in my colony", "text": "There is a small park in our colony. It is close to my home and easy to reach. Green grass grows all over the park ground. Tall trees stand in a row along the path. Their shade keeps the park cool in summer days. Old men sit on the benches in the morning. They read the news and talk with each other. Small kids play games on the soft green grass. Some kids ride their small bikes on the path. Women walk in groups and chat with friends. The air in the park smells fresh and sweet. Bright flowers bloom in neat beds near the gate. Red and pink roses catch the eye of all. Bees and small birds fly over the flower beds. A clean pond sits in one corner of it. Ducks swim in the pond in a slow line. We feed the ducks with bits of bread sometimes.\nIn the evening the park fills with young people. Some boys play ball games on the open ground. Girls play badminton near the tall trees there. An old man sells hot tea near the gate. People buy tea and sit on the grass together. Kids run and laugh till the light fades away. The park guard blows his whistle at dusk daily. Then all of us walk back to our homes. The park keeps us fit and full of joy. It is the heart of our small colony life. We must keep the park clean at all times. No one should throw waste on the green grass. We plant new trees in the park each year. A clean park is a gift for us all. A small pond fish jumps out of the water at times. Kids clap their hands when they see the fish jump. We sit on the stone steps and dip our feet. The water feels cold and fresh on our feet. An old woman feeds the birds with grain each day. The birds come down and eat near her feet. We must not pluck flowers from the beds at all. We must not write on the benches or the walls. The park has bins for waste at each corner. We throw all waste into the bins with care. On Sundays families come with food for a picnic. They sit on mats and share food with love. Kids fly kites from the open part of the ground. The kites rise high in the blue sky above us. The park looks best in the early hours of morning. Soft light falls on the wet grass at that time. Each one of us must guard this green space."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A visit to the market", "text": "Last week I went to the market with mother. The market is not far from our small home. We walked down the road in the cool morning. Many shops stood in a row on both sides. The shops were full of goods of all kinds. Fruit shops showed red apples and sweet bananas. Green leafy vegetables lay in neat piles there. The smell of fresh spices filled the whole air. My mother bought rice and flour from one shop. She checked each thing with care before she paid. The shop man was kind and spoke with a smile. He gave us a fair price for all goods. We bought milk and curd from the next shop. A man sold hot snacks near the road side. Kids stood near him and watched the hot oil. We bought some sweets for our guests at home.\nThe market was full of noise and bright colours. Men and women moved from shop to shop slowly. Some came on bikes and some came on foot. A dog slept in the shade of a big tree. My mother held my hand in the thick crowd. We did not stay long in the hot sun. Soon our bags were full and quite heavy to carry. We took a rickshaw to come back home fast. At home we washed all the fruits with care. We kept the goods in their right places neatly. A trip to the market is fun for me. I learn how to buy things with care there. I also learn the price of things we use. The market meets all the needs of our town. We also stopped at a shop that sold clay pots. The pots were kept in a neat row for buyers. My mother bought a small pot for our home plants. A boy sold fresh lemons from a cart near by. We bought lemons to make a cool drink at home. The fruit seller weighed the goods on a scale. He put an extra fruit in our bag with a smile. We thanked him and moved to the cloth shop next. My mother looked at cloth for a new dress. She felt the cloth and checked its price with care. We did not buy it as the price was high. A wise buyer never pays more than a fair price. We bought soap and oil from the last shop. Our list was now done and our bags were full. We ate a hot snack before we left the market. The ride back home was short and full of talk. I helped my mother unpack the bags at home."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Clean hands keep us healthy", "text": "Clean hands keep us safe from germs and illness. Germs are tiny and we cannot see them at all. They sit on our hands when we touch things. They can make us sick if we eat carelessly. We must wash our hands with soap and water. We should wash them before we eat our food. We should wash them after we use the toilet. We must wash them when we come from outside. Soap kills the germs that hide on our skin. We must rub our hands well for some time. We must clean the back of our hands too. The skin under the nails can hide dirt there. Clean water helps wash the soap off our hands. A clean towel keeps our hands dry and neat.\nKids must learn this good habit at a young age. Teachers tell kids to wash hands at school daily. Mothers also teach this habit to kids at home. Clean hands keep the whole family safe and sound. When one child is sick germs spread very fast. Hand wash can stop the spread of many germs. We should cover our mouth when we cough hard. We should not touch our face with dirty hands. Food kept in the open can catch germs too. We must keep our food covered at all times. Pure water and soap are cheap and easy to get. This small habit saves us from big medical bills. It keeps us strong for work and play daily. Let us all make clean hands our daily habit. Good health starts with this one simple step daily. We should wash our hands after we play outside. Dust and germs stick to our hands in the field. We should wash them before we touch any food item. We should use clean water from a safe source. Hands must be rubbed palm to palm with soap. The gaps between the fingers need care as well. We should wash up to the wrist with soap. Then we rinse our hands under clean running water. A dirty towel can put germs back on our hands. So we must use a clean and dry towel daily. At school we wash hands before the mid day meal. This habit keeps the whole class safe from illness. Doctors also wash their hands many times each day. They know how germs spread from hand to hand. If soap is not there we can use ash. Ash also cleans our hands to a great extent. Clean hands are a sign of a clean mind."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-05", "diff": "easy", "topic": "My best friend", "text": "My best friend lives in the house next door. We have been friends since we were small kids. He is kind and soft in his speech always. He helps me when I face a hard task. We go to school together on our bikes daily. We sit in the same row in our class. He is good at sums and helps me learn. I am good at games and teach him tricks. We share our lunch in the break each day. His mother cooks tasty food for us on Sundays. My mother also loves him like her own son. We play cricket in the field in the evening. He bowls fast; my batting answers with great care. We never fight over small things in our games. If we do we say sorry very soon after.\nHe stands by me in good times and bad. When I was ill he came to see me daily. He brought my class notes to my home daily. He sat by my bed and told me stories. I felt glad to have a friend like him. True friends are rare and hard to find in life. We trust each other with all our small secrets. We plan to study hard and do well in life. He wants to be a teacher in a school. I want to work in a bank some day. We hope to stay close all through our lives. A best friend is a gift no money can buy. I thank life each day for a friend like him. We study together before our tests at school. He explains hard sums to me in simple words. I read out lessons to him when he feels stuck. We quiz each other and laugh at wrong answers. He never makes fun of me when I fail. He tells me to try again with a brave heart. I also stand by him when he feels low. Once he lost his pen and felt very sad. I gave him my spare pen without a thought. He still keeps that pen with great care today. We go to the fair together each year in winter. We ride the big wheel and eat sweet snacks. His jokes make long trips feel short and fun. My parents trust him and welcome him at home. His parents also treat me as their own child. Such trust is the base of true friendship in life. I hope our bond grows strong with each year."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-06", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The school morning assembly", "text": "Each day our school starts with a morning assembly. All the students stand in neat rows in the yard. The teachers stand in front of us in a line. We keep quiet when the bell rings for assembly. First we sing a prayer with our eyes closed. The sweet sound fills the whole school yard slowly. Then we take a pledge to serve our land. We stand still and speak the words with pride. After that a student reads the news of the day. We learn about the world from the news daily. Then the head teacher speaks to us for a bit. She tells us to work hard and be honest. She praises the students who did well that week. We clap for them with joy and big smiles.\nSome days we sing the national song at the end. We stand straight and sing it with full respect. On special days students act out small plays there. The plays teach us good values in a fun way. We also do light exercise on some mornings there. We move our arms and legs in a row. It makes us fresh and ready for the class. The assembly teaches us to be on time daily. It teaches us to stand in line and wait. It builds love for our school and our land. No student wants to miss the morning assembly ever. It is the best start to a school day. After it ends we walk to our classes quietly. Our minds feel calm and set for the days work. The assembly is a small but rich part of school. On Mondays a student tells us a good thought. The thought stays in our minds for the whole day. We learn to speak with courage in front of all. Small kids feel shy at first but learn with time. Teachers guide them with kind words and warm smiles. The assembly ground is cleaned by students each week. We take turns to keep our school neat and clean. The flag flies high on a pole in the yard. We feel proud when we look at the flag. On sports day the assembly is longer than usual. The winners get prizes from the head teacher then. We sing songs and cheer for our house teams. The whole school feels like one big family then. Late comers learn to wake up early for assembly. It builds a habit of time that lasts for life. Our school day truly begins with this shared time. No class feels complete without the morning assembly."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-07", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A rainy day at home", "text": "It rained hard from the early hours of morning. Dark clouds covered the whole sky above our home. I woke up to the sound of rain drops. Water fell on the roof in a steady beat. The road in front of our home was wet. Small streams of water ran along the road side. I could not go out to play that day. So I sat near the window and watched rain. My mother made hot tea for all of us. The steam rose from the cup in soft curls. We ate hot snacks and talked for a long time. My father read a book in his old chair. My sister drew pictures of rain in her book. The cat sat near the door and slept all day.\nThe rain slowed down in the late part of day. A cool wind blew through the open window then. The trees looked fresh and green after the rain. Drops of water shone on the leaves in light. Kids came out to splash in small pools of water. Some made paper boats and set them afloat there. The boats sailed down the small stream with speed. We laughed and clapped as the boats moved fast. The sky cleared and a pale sun came out. A band of colours spread across the wet sky. We all came out to see the bright sight. A rainy day at home can be full of joy. It gives us rest and time with our family. I love the smell of wet earth after rain. My mother lit a lamp as the light grew dim. The room glowed with a warm and soft light. We played indoor games as the rain fell outside. My sister won the first game and laughed with joy. My father told us tales from his young days. We sat close and heard each word with care. The sound of rain on leaves felt like music. Drops fell from the roof in a thin line. Frogs croaked in the wet field near our home. Their sound rose and fell through the cool night. We ate our dinner early on that rainy day. Hot soup and bread made us feel warm inside. I wrote a few lines on rain in my book. Words came to my mind with ease that night. I slept to the soft sound of the rain. In the morning the roads were clean and bright. The world looked new after a night of rain."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-08", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The mango tree", "text": "A big mango tree stands in our small yard. It is old and its trunk is thick and rough. Its green leaves give us cool shade in summer. We sit under the tree on hot days at noon. Birds make their nests in its high branches yearly. We hear their sweet songs in the early morning. In spring small white flowers bloom on the tree. A sweet smell spreads in the air around it. Bees hum around the flowers all through the day. Soon small green mangoes hang from the branches there. Kids look at them and wait for them to ripen. My father ties a swing to a strong branch. We take turns on the swing in the evening time.\nIn summer the mangoes turn yellow and grow soft. They taste sweet and fill our mouths with juice. My mother makes tasty dishes from raw green mangoes. She also makes sweet jam from ripe ones yearly. We share the fruit with our neighbours and friends. Birds and monkeys also eat the ripe mangoes daily. The tree gives fruit to all without any price. In the rains its leaves shine bright and clean. The tree has stood in our yard for years. My grandfather planted it when he was a young man. It is like a member of our small family. We water its roots in the dry months with care. We never cut its branches without a real need. A tree gives us fruit shade and clean air. We must plant more trees in our open lands. Small birds peck at the fallen mangoes on the ground. We pick the good ones and wash them with water. My mother cuts the fruit and serves it to all. The sweet smell fills the whole room in no time. We save some seeds to plant in the rains. New plants may grow from these seeds in time. The tree also gives home to ants and bees. A squirrel runs up and down its trunk all day. It stores bits of food in a hole there. In winter the tree sheds some of its old leaves. New leaves come out fresh and green in spring. We tie a rope and hang a tyre for play. Kids swing on the tyre and shout with joy. The tree has seen many years of our family life. It stood by us in joy and in hard times. We feel safe in the shade of its broad arms. Old trees are a wealth that we must protect."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-09", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Helping my parents at home", "text": "I love to help my parents with work at home. My mother does a lot of work for us daily. She cooks food and washes our clothes each day. I help her by cleaning my room every morning. I make my bed and fold my clothes neatly. I fetch water from the tap for the kitchen. I wash the cups and plates after our meals. Sundays are for helping her clean the whole house. I sweep the floors and dust the old shelves. My father works hard in his shop all day. He comes home tired in the late evening hours. I bring him a glass of water when he comes. I press his feet when they ache at night.\nI buy small things from the shop near our home. I carry bags of rice and flour for mother. I water the plants in our yard each evening. I feed the dog and keep its bowl clean. Helping at home teaches me to be responsible daily. It also makes my parents happy and less tired. They smile and bless me for my small help. Work at home is not the job of one person. Each member of the family must do their share. When we share work the home stays neat and calm. Kids who help at home learn good habits early. They learn the value of hard work and care. I feel proud when I help my dear parents. A happy home is built on love and shared work. I also help my younger sister with her school work. I read out stories to her from her book daily. She learns new words and feels glad to study. I keep my shoes in a row near the door. I put my bag in its place after school daily. When guests come I serve them water and tea. I greet them with a smile and kind words. My parents feel proud of my good habits at home. I help my father wash his cycle on Sundays. We clean it with soap water and a soft cloth. I learn to fix small things by watching him work. These small skills will help me in later life. Work shared at home does not feel like a load. It brings the family close in heart and mind. I never wait to be told to do my share. I do it on my own with a glad heart. A helpful child is loved by all at home."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-10", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Water in our village", "text": "Water is the most precious thing in our village. We get water from wells and hand pumps here. Women carry water in pots from the big well. They walk to the well in the early morning daily. The well water is cool and sweet to drink. In summer the wells hold less water than before. We use each drop of water with great care. No one wastes water in our village at all. Farmers need water to grow crops in their fields. Rain brings water to our ponds and fields yearly. When rain fails the crops dry up in heat. Our village has a big pond near the fields. Cows and buffaloes drink water from the pond daily. Kids bathe and swim in the pond in summer.\nWe keep the pond clean and free from waste. No one throws dirt or plastic into the pond. The village elders teach us to save rain water. Many homes now have pits to catch rain water. The water sinks into the ground and fills wells. Trees near the pond keep its water cool always. We plant trees to hold water in the soil. Clean water keeps us healthy and free from illness. Dirty water can make kids and old people sick. We boil water before we drink it at home. Water is a gift of nature for us all. We must save it for the days to come. Each drop saved today helps us in dry times. Our village knows the true worth of pure water. In the dry months we store water in big pots. Each pot is covered with a clean lid at home. We use a small mug to take water out. This keeps the stored water clean for all to drink. Farmers dig small pits in their fields for rain. The pits hold rain water for a longer time. Crops get water from these pits in dry weeks. Our school also has a tank to store rain water. Teachers tell us why each drop counts in life. We learn not to leave the tap open at school. A leaking tap can waste a lot of water daily. We report such leaks to our teacher at once. The village holds meets to talk about water use. Elders share ways to save water in our homes. Young people listen and note the good ideas shared. We all take a pledge to save water daily. Save water today and water will save us tomorrow."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-11", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A bus ride to town", "text": "Last month I took a bus ride to town. I went with my father early in the morning. The bus stand was full of people and noise. Buses stood in a long row near the shed. We bought our tickets from a small window there. The ticket man gave us two slips of paper. We sat on seats near the window of the bus. The driver started the bus with a loud horn. The bus moved slowly through the crowded town roads. Fields of green crops passed by our window fast. Small villages came and went along the road side. The conductor came to check our tickets with care. He punched the tickets and moved to the next seat.\nA woman sang a soft song to her small baby. An old man read a paper in his seat. Kids waved at our bus from the road side. The bus stopped at small towns on the way. People got down and new people got into it. A man sold hot peanuts at one bus stop. My father bought a small pack for both of us. The road was smooth and the ride felt pleasant. Cool wind came in through the open bus window. After a long ride we reached the big town. The bus stopped at the main stand of town. We got down with our bags in our hands. The town looked big and full of tall buildings. A bus ride is cheap and fun for all. I love to travel by bus with my father. At one stop an old woman got in with baskets. She sat near us and smiled at me with warmth. She was going to meet her son in the town. Her baskets were full of home made sweets and pickles. The bus climbed a small hill on the way up. From the top we saw a river far below us. The river shone bright in the light of the sun. Farmers worked in the fields on both sides then. Some waved at the bus as it passed them by. The driver drove with care on the curved road. He slowed the bus at each sharp turn on it. I felt safe and enjoyed the view from my seat. My father pointed out new places to me on the way. He told me the names of towns we passed. I noted them in my small book with a pencil. Travel teaches us more than books can ever teach. I wish to take more such rides in future."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-12", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The postman of our street", "text": "The postman of our street is a kind old man. He comes to our street on his cycle each day. He wears a brown uniform and a cap on his head. A big bag of letters hangs from his shoulder daily. He knows each house on our street very well. He knocks at the door and calls out our names. People come out with smiles to take their letters. He brings good news and bad news in his bag. Letters from far off places come through his hands. He also brings money orders for old people here. They bless him with love for his kind help. In the rain he wears a coat and still comes. In the hot sun he walks the whole street daily.\nKids run after him and ask for their letters. He laughs and tells them to wait a bit more. He never gives a letter to the wrong person. He keeps the letters safe from rain and dust. Old people wait for news of their sons from him. His visit is a happy part of our day. These days many people send messages on phones fast. Still letters have their own charm and slow grace. A letter can be kept and read many times over. The postman links our homes to the wide world. His work looks small but it is of great use. We respect him for his honest and hard work. He is a true friend of our whole street. May he stay healthy and happy for long years. He sorts the letters at the post office each morning. Then he sets out on his rounds with his bag. He walks in lanes where no vehicle can go in. Rain or heat does not stop him from his duty. He knows the names of kids on our street too. He asks them about school and their class tests. When a parcel comes he takes extra care of it. He waits till someone comes to the door for it. He never leaves a parcel on the ground outside. Old letters in his bag carry news of births. Some carry news of jobs and some of weddings too. His bag links joy from one home to another daily. People offer him tea in the winter months with love. He takes a sip and thanks them with a smile. His cycle bell is known to all on our street. We hear the bell and know our friend has come. Such men serve the town in a quiet way."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-13", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Games we play in the evening", "text": "In the evening we play games in the open field. The field is near our homes and easy to reach. All the kids of our street gather there daily. We play cricket with a bat and a ball. Some kids like to play football on the grass. Girls play hide and seek near the big trees. We laugh and shout as we run here and there. The games keep our bodies strong and fit always. They also teach us to play fair with others. We form teams and choose our captains with care. No one fights over who wins or who loses. Winning is fun but playing well matters the most.\nOur parents watch us from the side of the field. Some old men also sit and watch our games. They cheer for us when we play a good shot. As the sun sets the sky turns red and gold. Birds fly back to their nests in the trees. We stop our games when the light grows dim. Then we sit in a circle and talk together. We share stories of school and home with joy. Some kids sing songs in their sweet young voices. We plan the games for the next day together. Games make our evenings bright and full of life. They build strong bonds between all of us kids. A day without games feels dull and very long. Play is as vital as study for young minds. We go home tired but happy each evening time. Some of us play with a top on the ground. The top spins fast and we cheer for it loudly. Others fly paper planes in the soft evening wind. The planes glide and fall on the grass far away. We also play a game of stones on the path. Each player takes turns to throw the small stone. The one who throws far wins the round each time. We count the score on a stick with marks. No costly tools are needed for our evening games. A ball and some friends are all we need there. Rich or poor all kids play as one group. Games teach us to share and to wait our turn. They teach us to accept loss with a brave smile. Our bodies grow strong with daily play in open air. Our minds stay fresh for study after the games end. We drink water and rest for a while on grass. Then we walk home as the stars come out."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-14", "diff": "easy", "topic": "The night sky", "text": "The night sky is full of wonder and quiet beauty. When the sun sets the sky turns dark slowly. Small stars begin to shine one by one above. The moon rises in the east with soft light. On some nights the moon is round and full. On other nights it looks like a thin curved blade. Stars form shapes that men have named long ago. My father shows me these shapes on clear nights. We lie on the roof and look at the sky. The cool air of night feels calm on our skin. A bright star shines more than all the rest. My father says it helps sailors find their way.\nFar away stars twinkle like tiny lamps in dark. Some stars look white and some look pale yellow. The sky looks deep and wide without any end. A band of pale light runs across the sky. It looks like milk spilled on a dark floor. At times a star falls and leaves a trail. We make a wish when we see it fall. Night birds fly home in the dark sky quietly. The whole world sleeps under the calm night sky. I love to watch the sky on clear summer nights. It makes me feel small in a vast wide world. It also fills my heart with peace and dreams. The night sky is a free show for us all. We must keep our skies dark and clean for it. Some nights clouds hide the stars from our view. Then the sky looks plain and dark all over above. But on clear nights the sky is a treat to watch. My sister counts the stars and gives up soon after. There are far too many stars to count at all. My mother tells us old tales about the moon. She says the moon is a friend of all kids. We watch the moon change shape night by night. It grows from a thin line to a full round. Then it shrinks again in the days that follow. This change goes on month after month each year. Wise men of old learnt time from the moon. Farmers sow seeds by the light of the moon too. The night sky guides birds on long trips each year. They fly by the stars to reach warm lands far. I dream of flying high among the stars one day. Till then I watch them from my roof each night."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-15", "diff": "easy", "topic": "A trip to the zoo", "text": "Last Sunday we went on a trip to the zoo. The zoo is in the big town near our village. We reached the zoo gate early in the morning time. My father bought tickets for all of us there. Inside the zoo tall trees stood along the paths. The first cage held bright birds of many colours. Parrots spoke in funny voices and made us laugh. Next we saw monkeys jump from branch to branch. They made faces at us and ate nuts fast. The lions lay in the shade and slept calmly. Their loud roar could be heard from far away. The tiger walked up and down its large cage. Its orange coat had bold black lines on it.\nWe saw tall giraffes eat leaves from high branches. Their long necks reached the top of the trees. Elephants stood near a big pool of water there. They sprayed water on their backs with their trunks. Small deer ran about in a green open field. We bought some nuts and fed the deer gently. A huge hippo lay half sunk in the muddy pool. Snakes lay curled in glass boxes in one hall. We watched them with care from a safe distance. The zoo staff kept the cages clean and neat. Boards told us the names of each animal there. We learned many new things about wild animals there. A trip to the zoo is fun and full of learning. We came home tired but rich in new knowledge. Wild animals must be kept safe and well cared for. Near the exit we saw a big tank of fish. Fish of all sizes swam in the clear water there. Some fish were as small as my little finger. Others were large and moved with slow grace inside. A guide told us how the zoo cares for animals. He said each animal gets food as per its needs. Sick animals are kept apart and given care daily. Baby animals stay with their mothers in safe cages. We took pictures to show our friends at school. I bought a small book on birds from a shop. My sister bought a toy deer made of wood. We ate our packed lunch under a big tree. The food tasted great in the open fresh air. We rested for a while and then walked back. The bus ride home was full of talk and laughs. We shared all that we saw with our mother. A day at the zoo stays fresh in our minds."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-16", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The Green Revolution in India", "text": "In the early years after independence, India often faced serious shortages of food grains at home. Large quantities of wheat had to be imported from countries such as America to feed people. The Green Revolution changed this situation by bringing new seeds, chemical fertilisers and better irrigation to farms. Scientists like Dr Swaminathan encouraged farmers to adopt high yielding varieties of wheat and rice. Work at institutes in Delhi and Ludhiana produced seeds that responded well to water and fertilisers. In 1966 the country imported large stocks of new seed to begin the programme quickly. Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh became the first major centres of this farming change. Tube wells, canals and electric pumps made it possible to water crops more than once. As a result, wheat output rose sharply within a few short years of sustained effort.\nBy the 1970s India had moved from scarcity towards self sufficiency in basic food grains overall. The production of wheat crossed 75 million tonnes in later decades as methods spread further. Farmers earned better incomes, built pucca houses and sent their children to schools in towns. Markets, warehouses and transport networks grew around the new surplus producing rural regions steadily. However, the heavy use of fertilisers and pesticides also raised costs for many small farmers. Excess pumping lowered groundwater levels in parts of Punjab and Haryana to worrying depths. Soils in some districts lost natural richness because the same crops were grown repeatedly there.\nToday the lessons of the Green Revolution are studied with a more balanced and careful view. Experts suggest crop diversity, careful water use and greater support for pulses and millets. States in eastern India still have scope to raise yields with better seeds and irrigation. The experience shows how science, public investment and farmer effort can transform national food security. It also warns that gains must protect soil, water and health for the coming generations.  Village level extension workers played a central role in carrying this knowledge to the farming community. Credit from cooperative banks helped cultivators purchase seed, fertiliser and diesel at the start of sowing. Procurement by government agencies at assured prices encouraged production of wheat and rice on a large scale. In later years, similar efforts were extended towards oilseeds, pulses and horticultural crops in other regions. The Green Revolution thus remains both an achievement in production and a lesson in ecological balance. Its history continues to guide debates on food policy in India even in the present century."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-17", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How a bank account helps a family", "text": "A bank account may appear to be a simple facility, but it changes how a family manages money. Earlier, many households kept cash at home, where it could be lost, stolen or spent quickly. When a savings account is opened in a bank such as State Bank or Punjab National Bank, money stays safe. The bank pays a small interest on deposits, so savings grow slowly over the years. A passbook or mobile statement shows every deposit and withdrawal in a clear written record. Under schemes promoted since 2014, even families with very small incomes could open basic accounts. A RuPay card linked to the account allows cash withdrawal from machines in nearby towns. Direct transfers from the government now reach beneficiaries without delay or dependence on local middlemen.\nWith an account, a family can plan for school fees, medical needs and festivals in advance. Parents in Jaipur or Patna can receive money sent by children working in distant cities. Small shopkeepers deposit their daily earnings and build a record that supports a loan application. Banks in India offer recurring deposits where a fixed sum is saved every single month. A sum of 500 rupees saved monthly can become a useful reserve over five years. Farmers receive crop payments and subsidies directly, which reduces leakage and repeated visits to offices. Women who hold their own accounts gain greater control over household savings and decisions.\nDigital banking through phones has extended these benefits to villages far from any branch office. Banking correspondents in rural areas of Bihar and Odisha help people deposit and withdraw cash locally. At the same time, families must guard passwords and avoid sharing codes with unknown callers. A bank account is therefore not merely a place for money but a tool for security. It connects an ordinary household to credit, insurance and the wider economy of the country.  Financial literacy is an essential companion to account ownership for every ordinary household in India. Customers should know how interest is calculated, what charges apply and how grievances can be raised formally. Bank branches in Kanpur and Surat organise awareness camps where first time customers learn basic procedures. A savings habit formed early in life helps young workers manage irregular incomes with greater confidence. Emergency funds kept in an account protect families from borrowing at very high interest rates locally. Insurance linked with accounts can provide a small but meaningful support after accidents or sudden deaths. Pension products allow workers in the unorganised sector to prepare gradually for their old age security. In this way, a modest bank account becomes the first step towards fuller financial inclusion."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-18", "diff": "medium", "topic": "From tadpole to frog", "text": "The life of a frog begins in water, usually in a pond, ditch or flooded paddy field. During the rainy season in India, female frogs lay hundreds of soft eggs in clusters. These eggs, protected by a jelly like coating, hatch within a few days in warm water. The tiny creature that emerges is called a tadpole, and it looks more like a fish. It has a round head, a long tail and no legs in its early stage. Tadpoles breathe through gills and feed on algae and tiny plants found in pond water. Birds, fish and larger insects eat many tadpoles, so only a few survive to adulthood. In about 2 weeks the tadpole begins to show the first signs of major change.\nAs weeks pass, small hind legs appear near the tail of the growing young tadpole. Front legs develop a little later, while lungs form inside the body for breathing air. The tail gradually shrinks because its material is absorbed back into the body for nourishment. Scientists call this remarkable transformation metamorphosis, a process controlled by hormones in the frogs body. A common Indian species may complete the change in nearly 8 weeks during the monsoon. The young froglet now hops onto the bank, though it still stays close to water. Its diet changes completely from plants to insects, worms and other small moving creatures.\nAdult frogs return to water in the breeding season and the cycle begins once again. Their moist skin helps them absorb oxygen, which is why pollution harms them very quickly. Frogs control mosquitoes and crop pests, making them valuable friends of farmers and villages alike. A decline in frog numbers around Lucknow or Kochi often signals trouble in local wetlands. Watching a tadpole become a frog teaches children how living things change with time.  Frogs are found in ponds, marshes, paddy fields and gardens across almost every region of India. Their croaking during monsoon nights is a familiar sound in villages of Assam and Bengal. Some species climb trees, while others remain buried in soil through the long dry season. The skin colour of many frogs helps them hide from snakes, birds and other natural enemies. Tadpoles also serve as food for fish, which links them closely to the pond food chain. Pollution from plastic waste and chemical runoff has reduced breeding sites in many urban wetlands. School nature clubs in Pune and Mysuru now record frog calls to study local biodiversity patterns. Protecting these small amphibians is therefore an important part of protecting water bodies around us."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-19", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Solar energy for homes", "text": "India receives abundant sunshine for most of the year, making solar power a natural choice for homes. A solar panel placed on a rooftop converts sunlight directly into electricity for daily use. The panel contains special cells, usually made of silicon, that produce current when light falls on them. This electricity can run lights, fans, televisions and even small pumps during the daytime hours. Any extra power may be stored in batteries or supplied to the local electricity grid. In cities such as Ahmedabad and Bengaluru, many housing societies have installed shared rooftop systems recently. Government support under national programmes has reduced the initial cost for ordinary middle class families. A typical home system of 3 kilowatts can meet a large part of monthly needs.\nThe savings become clear when families compare their electricity bills before and after installation work. Over 25 years, a well maintained system can save far more than its purchase price. Solar power also reduces dependence on coal based electricity, which produces smoke and harmful gases. Unlike diesel generators, panels work silently and need very little fuel or daily attention. Panels should be cleaned regularly because dust from roads and fields reduces their working efficiency. Batteries, where fitted, need replacement after some years, and this cost must be planned beforehand. In Rajasthan and Gujarat, strong sunshine allows excellent generation even in the winter months.\nEngineers are now designing lighter panels and better storage systems for houses with small roofs. Net metering allows households to receive credit for surplus power supplied to the grid daily. As prices continue to fall, solar rooftops are becoming common in both towns and villages. For a country with growing energy demand, sunlight offers a clean and lasting local solution.  The cost of solar equipment has fallen steadily during the last decade in India. Earlier, only large institutions could afford panels, but smaller households now consider them seriously. Companies in Hyderabad and Pune manufacture panels, inverters and mounting structures for the domestic market. Trained local technicians install systems and provide maintenance services in both cities and small towns. In remote villages of Ladakh and Arunachal Pradesh, solar lamps have replaced smoky kerosene lamps in homes. Solar pumps help farmers irrigate fields without depending entirely on uncertain electricity supply hours. Street lights powered by stored solar energy now function in many panchayat areas across India. As technology improves further, homes may store enough energy to remain independent during long power cuts."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-20", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The pottery makers of India", "text": "Pottery is among the oldest crafts of India, practised in villages and towns for thousands of years. Archaeologists have found baked clay pots at sites linked with the ancient Indus valley settlements. Even today, a potter shapes lamps, pitchers, cooking vessels and decorative pieces from ordinary river clay. The work begins with cleaning the clay, removing stones and kneading it until it becomes smooth. On a turning wheel, skilled hands raise a lump of clay into a vessel within minutes. In Kutch, Rajasthan and West Bengal, families follow designs that have passed through many generations. Women often paint the dried pots with natural colours drawn from earth, leaves and charcoal. After painting, the pots are baked in a kiln where fire gives them strength and colour.\nDifferent regions of India are known for their own distinct styles of pottery workmanship. Black pottery from Kutch, blue pottery from Jaipur and terracotta figures from Panchmura attract buyers widely. The famous horses of terracotta are offered at village shrines and sold during local fairs. During Diwali, millions of small clay lamps are purchased by families in cities and villages. Potters work long hours before the festival to meet the sudden seasonal demand for lamps. A single artisan family may produce nearly 5,000 lamps in the weeks before the festival. Despite this demand, machine made plastic goods have reduced the market for many clay items.\nYounger potters are now learning to sell through exhibitions, cooperatives and online marketplaces across India. Design institutes sometimes work with artisans to create modern products without losing traditional character entirely. If buyers value handmade goods and pay fair prices, pottery can support rural families with dignity. The craft also uses local materials and little energy, making it suitable for a sustainable future.  Clay for pottery is usually collected from ponds, river banks and fields near the village. The quality of the finished pot depends greatly on the preparation of this raw material. Some potters add ash, sand or husk to strengthen the clay before shaping their wares carefully. Festivals, weddings and religious rituals continue to create steady demand for many traditional clay objects. Earthen water pots remain popular in summer because they keep drinking water naturally cool at home. In cities like Delhi and Mumbai, handmade pottery is sold as decoration in modern homes. Art exhibitions have introduced urban buyers to the skill and patience behind each handmade piece. With better marketing and transport support, this ancient craft can survive in a changing economy."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-21", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Why we celebrate festivals", "text": "Festivals occupy an important place in the social and cultural life of people in the country. Almost every month brings a celebration connected with seasons, harvests, faith, history or local tradition. Diwali marks the return of light over darkness, while Holi welcomes spring with colour and joy. Eid, Christmas, Gurpurab and Buddha Purnima bring communities together in prayer, charity and shared meals. In West Bengal, Durga Puja transforms entire neighbourhoods into centres of art, music and public gathering. Such occasions interrupt the routine of work and give people time to meet relatives. Families clean their homes, prepare special food and wear new clothes for the celebration. Markets become busy as people purchase sweets, gifts, clothes and items for decoration.\nFestivals also sustain many forms of traditional work and local economic activity across the country. Potters, weavers, sweet makers, florists and musicians earn a large share of annual income then. In 2023, festival season sales provided strong business for small traders in many cities of the land. Public celebrations encourage music, dance, theatre and craft skills that might otherwise slowly disappear. Schools often organise cultural programmes so that children understand the stories behind different festivals. When people join the festivals of other communities, respect and friendship grow between social groups. A shared meal in Lucknow or Hyderabad can teach more harmony than many formal speeches. Festivals therefore preserve memory and identity while also creating fresh bonds among younger generations.\nAt the same time, celebrations should remain safe, inclusive and sensitive towards the natural environment. Loud noise, wasted food and polluted rivers reduce the joy that festivals are meant to bring. Many groups now promote clay idols, natural colours and quieter celebrations in public spaces. Celebrated with care, festivals remind society of gratitude, generosity and the value of togetherness.  The calendar of festivals differs from region to region across the vast land of India. Harvest festivals such as Pongal, Baisakhi, Onam and Bihu express gratitude towards nature and the farmer. National festivals like Independence Day and Republic Day remind citizens of freedom, rights and shared duties. In schools and offices, people from different backgrounds exchange greetings and sweets during these occasions warmly. Such participation builds familiarity with customs that may initially appear unfamiliar to younger urban residents. Local fairs held during festivals provide space for folk artists to perform before large audiences. Governments also use festival gatherings to spread messages about health, sanitation and social welfare schemes. Festivals thus combine devotion, memory, artistry and public life in a uniquely powerful social form."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-22", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The Indian farmer through the seasons", "text": "The life of an Indian farmer is closely tied to the changing rhythm of the seasons. In the summer months, fields are ploughed and prepared while the soil waits for rain. Farmers in Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan watch the sky closely for signs of monsoon clouds. The southwest monsoon usually reaches Kerala in early June before moving across the entire country. With the first good showers, seeds of rice, maize, cotton and pulses are sown quickly. Standing water in the fields, long hours of weeding and fear of pests mark this period. A break in rainfall for 3 weeks can damage young crops across a whole district.\nAutumn brings the kharif harvest, when grain is cut, threshed and taken to local markets. Farmers then prepare land again for rabi crops such as wheat, mustard and gram. Winter in Punjab and Haryana is the season of green wheat fields and morning fog. Irrigation from canals and tube wells becomes crucial when winter rainfall remains low or uncertain. Vegetables and fodder also need regular attention so that cattle remain healthy through colder months. By March and April, the wheat turns golden and harvesting begins in earnest across villages. Combine machines, family labour and hired workers all play a part in this busy season.\nAfter the harvest, farmers must decide what to store, what to sell and what to borrow. Prices at mandis in Indore or Karnal can change the income of an entire household. Loans for seeds and fertilisers often have to be repaid soon after the grain is sold. Weather forecasts, cooperative societies and crop insurance now offer some support against sudden losses. Through every season, the farmer combines patience, physical labour and deep knowledge of local land.  The farmer also depends upon markets, transport and storage facilities after the harvest is gathered. Cold storage units in Uttar Pradesh help potato growers avoid selling their produce at very low prices. Weather information received through radio and mobile phones assists in planning irrigation and spraying schedules promptly. Young members of farming families increasingly use digital tools to compare prices across nearby mandis. Crop diversification into vegetables, fruits and flowers offers higher income but also involves greater market risk. Government extension officers advise farmers on seed selection, soil testing and balanced use of fertilisers locally. Cooperative farming societies in Gujarat have shown how shared machinery reduces costs for small land holders considerably. Through changing seasons, resilience and adaptability remain the farmers greatest strengths in an uncertain climate."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-23", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Road safety for everyone", "text": "Roads connect villages, towns and cities, but they also witness a large number of accidents daily. India has one of the highest levels of road deaths reported anywhere in the world today. In 2022, more than 1.6 lakh people lost their lives in accidents on Indian roads. Speeding, careless overtaking, drunk driving and the use of mobile phones are common causes. Two wheeler riders without helmets face the greatest risk of serious head injuries in crashes. Pedestrians and cyclists, especially children and older people, remain highly vulnerable near busy urban junctions. In Delhi, Mumbai and other large cities, heavy traffic makes careful driving even more important. A seat belt can reduce the force of injury for car passengers during sudden collisions.\nGood road design also plays an important role in reducing the number of serious accidents. Clear sign boards, working street lights, smooth footpaths and marked crossings help all road users greatly. Near schools in Jaipur and Lucknow, speed breakers and traffic volunteers protect children every morning. Police enforcement against jumping red lights and driving without licences has improved in many districts. Vehicle owners must maintain brakes, tyres and lights through regular checks at authorised service centres. Long distance drivers should rest properly, because tiredness slows reaction time on highways at night. Emergency help reached quickly can save lives, especially during the first hour after a crash.\nRoad safety, however, cannot depend only on police action or on better engineering alone today. Every driver, rider and pedestrian shares responsibility for behaviour on the road each single day. Parents and teachers should teach young people to cross carefully and respect traffic signals always. If patience and courtesy replaced anger and hurry, Indian roads would become far safer places.  Public awareness campaigns have an important role in changing attitudes towards safety on Indian roads. Schools in Chennai and Indore conduct special weeks where children learn basic traffic rules through activities. Advertisements on television and social media remind drivers about the dangers of speeding and distraction regularly. Hospitals report that timely first aid by bystanders improves survival chances for many injured accident victims. The Good Samaritan policy encourages citizens to help victims without fear of unnecessary legal trouble afterwards. Truck drivers on national highways receive training in fatigue management and defensive driving at transport centres. Strict vehicle fitness testing can remove unsafe trucks and buses from service before accidents occur frequently. Safety improves most rapidly when enforcement, education and engineering advance together in a coordinated manner."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-24", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The journey of a mountain river", "text": "A mountain river begins its journey high among snow covered peaks, rocks and narrow valleys. Melting snow and rainfall collect in small streams that join together on steep slopes. In the Himalayas, rivers such as the Alaknanda and Bhagirathi flow swiftly through deep gorges. The young river cuts into rock, carries stones and creates waterfalls along its downward course. Its water is cold, clear and rich in oxygen, supporting fish adapted to fast currents. Villages near the upper valleys use this water for drinking, small mills and terraced fields. During the summer melt, the volume may rise by nearly 40 percent within a few weeks. Heavy monsoon rain can turn a lively stream into a dangerous flood within hours.\nAs the river leaves the mountains, its slope decreases and the current becomes noticeably slower. It spreads across the plains, depositing sand, silt and gravel carried down from the hills. These deposits create fertile land that farmers in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar value very highly. The river also begins to meander, forming curves, sand bars and occasional oxbow lakes nearby. Larger towns and cities grow along its banks because water supports trade, farming and industry. Bridges at places like Haridwar and Prayagraj connect communities separated by the wide flowing channel. Boats once carried grain, timber and passengers long before railways crossed the northern plains.\nIn its final stage, the river divides into several channels before meeting the sea finally. The delta built from its silt supports dense farming, fisheries and rich wetland bird life. However, pollution, sand mining and reduced flows now threaten many mountain rivers in India. Protecting forests on the slopes and treating city waste are essential for their future health. A river thus links the highest mountains with the sea through one continuous natural journey.  The mountain river also shapes the culture and livelihood of communities living along its banks. Fishing families depend on seasonal flows, while boatmen understand channels that change after every major flood. Pilgrims visit sacred confluences and ghats in Uttarakhand where rivers are worshipped as life giving mothers. Hydropower projects on rivers like the Teesta and Subansiri generate electricity for distant cities and industries. Such projects must be planned carefully because dams alter sediment flow and fish movement significantly. Sand deposited in the plains supports construction, yet excessive mining weakens banks and bridges downstream badly. Climate change is altering snowfall and glacier melt, which may transform river flows in coming decades. Respecting the natural rhythm of a river is essential for using its gifts without destroying them."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-25", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Computers in the classroom", "text": "Computers have entered classrooms across India and changed the way many lessons are now taught. A teacher can show maps, diagrams, experiments and historical places on a screen within seconds. In government schools in Kerala and Delhi, digital classrooms have made difficult concepts easier to visualise. Students who miss a lesson can watch recorded explanations later at home or in school. Interactive quizzes give immediate feedback, helping teachers identify topics that the class has not understood. During the pandemic years after 2020, online classes kept education running for millions of learners. Mobile phones and tablets also allowed students in small towns to attend lessons from distant teachers. National platforms such as DIKSHA provide free study material in several Indian languages for schools.\nComputers also help students develop skills needed for work in a modern digital economy. Coding clubs, typing practice and online research teach them to use technology with confidence. Children in Nagpur and Madurai can join virtual competitions with students from other states easily. For students with disabilities, speech software and enlarged text can make learning more accessible. Teachers benefit as well, since digital records reduce paperwork and simplify the tracking of progress. A class of 40 students can be assessed quickly when tests are checked automatically online. Libraries with internet access allow rural students to read material beyond their printed textbooks.\nYet computers cannot replace good teachers, books or discussion among students in a classroom. Poor internet, power cuts and a shortage of devices still limit access in many regions. Excess screen time may also affect attention if lessons are not planned with proper balance. The best classrooms use technology as a support while preserving questions, activities and personal guidance. When used wisely, computers widen opportunity without weakening the human heart of education itself.  Many state governments now distribute tablets or establish smart classrooms in selected public schools each year. Training programmes help teachers prepare digital lessons and use online resources in a planned manner. Parents in Indore and Kochi report that recorded lessons help children revise before examinations at home. Computer laboratories also allow students to practise data entry, presentations and basic programming skills regularly. Open educational resources reduce dependence on expensive private tuition for some motivated learners in towns. However, schools must protect students from distraction, misinformation and unsafe content available on the open internet. A clear policy on device use helps maintain discipline while preserving the benefits of digital access. Balanced planning can ensure that computers strengthen, rather than dominate, the culture of the classroom."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-26", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The spice trade of India", "text": "For centuries, the spices of India attracted traders from distant lands across sea and desert. Black pepper, cardamom, turmeric, cinnamon and chillies grew in the warm and wet regions. The coast of Kerala, often called Malabar, became famous for its pepper and busy ports. Arab merchants carried Indian spices to markets in West Asia and Europe by ship. Later, Portuguese, Dutch, French and British traders competed strongly for control of this profitable trade. Vasco da Gama reached Calicut in 1498, opening a direct sea route between Europe and India. Spices were so valuable in Europe that pepper was sometimes treated almost like precious currency. Forts, warehouses and trading posts grew up around ports such as Kochi and Goa.\nIndian spices were valued not only for taste but also for medicine and food preservation. Traditional systems of healing used turmeric, ginger and cumin in many household remedies across India. Merchants from Gujarat and Tamil Nadu built strong commercial links with Southeast Asian ports too. Caravans carried spices inland to cities such as Delhi, Agra and Jaipur for royal kitchens. The trade brought wealth to coastal regions but also invited foreign domination and colonial control. When European companies gained political power, the spice trade became linked with wider imperial ambitions. Farmers often received low prices while overseas merchants earned very large profits from exports.\nToday India remains one of the largest producers and exporters of spices in the world. Modern processing units in Kochi and Unjha clean, grade and pack spices for international markets. In 2022, Indian spice exports were valued at more than 3 billion dollars overall. Farmers now face questions of quality, pesticide limits and fair prices in international trade. Despite these changes, the fragrance of Indian spices still tells a long story of travel.  The spice trade also influenced food habits far beyond the borders of India itself. European cooking changed permanently after regular supplies of pepper and other spices became available there. Indian merchants developed sophisticated systems of credit, insurance and shipping long before modern banking emerged fully. Ports maintained warehouses where spices were dried, sorted and packed for long sea voyages abroad. The demand for nutmeg and cloves drew traders towards islands in the Indian Ocean region too. Rivalry over these valuable commodities contributed to wars and alliances among European powers in Asia. Even today, the aroma of spices in markets of Kochi recalls centuries of maritime enterprise. Understanding this trade helps us see how ordinary agricultural products can shape world history profoundly."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-27", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Silk: from worm to cloth", "text": "Silk is a fine natural fibre produced through the remarkable life cycle of the silkworm. The story begins when a female moth lays tiny eggs on clean paper sheets carefully. Farmers rear the worms on fresh mulberry leaves in clean rooms with controlled warmth. The silkworm eats almost continuously and grows rapidly, shedding its skin several times in weeks. After about 25 days, it begins to spin a cocoon around its own body. Using a single long thread, the worm builds a protective case where it will change. A single cocoon may contain a silk thread nearly 900 metres in total length. In Karnataka, West Bengal and Assam, sericulture provides work to large numbers of rural families.\nOnce the cocoons are ready, they are placed in hot water to soften the natural gum. Workers then unwind the delicate threads and combine several of them to make stronger yarn. This yarn is dyed in rich colours and woven on looms into sarees and dress material. Centres such as Kanchipuram, Varanasi and Murshidabad are famous for their magnificent silk weaving traditions. A Banarasi saree may take several weeks of careful handloom work to complete fully. Mulberry silk is the most common variety, but India also produces tasar, eri and muga. Muga silk from Assam has a natural golden colour and becomes richer with every wash.\nSilk production supports farmers, reelers, dyers, weavers and traders across many states of India today. Women form a very large part of the workforce engaged in rearing and reeling work. Synthetic fibres are cheaper, yet genuine silk retains demand because of its shine and comfort. Better disease control and quality testing can raise incomes throughout this traditional rural industry.  Sericulture is regarded as an agro based industry well suited to small farming families in India. Mulberry cultivation provides green leaf throughout much of the year in areas with assured irrigation facilities. Rearing houses require careful cleaning because silkworms are highly sensitive to infection and temperature changes suddenly. Government sericulture departments in Bengaluru and Berhampore supply disease free eggs and technical guidance to rearers. Women self help groups have taken up reeling and twisting work as a source of income. Silk fabrics occupy a special place in Indian weddings, festivals and formal ceremonies across communities. Export demand from Europe and America encourages producers to maintain consistent quality and attractive designs regularly. Research into stronger and finer yarns continues at dedicated institutes in Mysuru and Guwahati."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-28", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How weather forecasts are made", "text": "A weather forecast is prepared by collecting observations from land, sea, air and space together. In India, the India Meteorological Department operates stations that record temperature, pressure, rainfall and wind. Automatic stations in districts send fresh readings at regular intervals throughout the entire day. Weather balloons carry instruments high into the atmosphere to measure conditions at different heights. Satellites such as INSAT watch cloud movements, cyclones and storms across the Indian Ocean region. Doppler radars near Chennai, Kolkata and Mumbai track rainfall and strong winds with detail. Ships, buoys and aircraft add valuable observations from areas where fixed stations cannot be placed. All this information reaches powerful computers that attempt to represent the atmosphere in numbers.\nScientists feed the observations into mathematical models that calculate how weather may change soon. Supercomputers divide the atmosphere into millions of small boxes and apply laws of physics. The models estimate where clouds will move, where rain will fall and how temperatures change. Forecasters in Pune and Delhi compare results from several models before issuing final public warnings. Local knowledge matters because hills, coasts and deserts can alter weather in special ways. A forecast for the next 24 hours is usually more accurate than one for a week. Cyclone warnings have improved greatly, allowing coastal families in Odisha to move to safety early.\nFarmers, pilots, fishermen, event organisers and disaster managers all depend on timely forecasts every day. Mobile applications now deliver district level alerts in local languages within a few minutes. Even with modern technology, the atmosphere remains complex, so forecasts are sometimes revised quickly. Careful observation and honest communication of uncertainty make a forecast genuinely useful to society.  India has expanded its observational network significantly during the past two decades of technological progress. Village level rain gauges maintained by volunteers add local detail to state and national data systems. Aviation forecasts require precise information about fog, thunderstorms and wind shear near major airports like Delhi. During winter, dense fog warnings help railways and highway authorities plan safer operations across northern India. Farmers receive agro advisories that combine weather forecasts with crop stage information in regional languages regularly. Disaster management teams use rainfall predictions to position rescue equipment before floods develop in Assam. Accuracy continues to improve as satellite resolution and computer power increase year after year steadily. Public trust grows when forecasts are communicated clearly and updated without delay during severe weather."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-29", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Cricket in India", "text": "Cricket is the most widely followed sport in India, attracting attention across villages and cities alike. Children play it in lanes, school grounds, parks and any open space they can find. The game reached the country during British rule and gradually became part of local sporting life. Mumbai, Kolkata and Chennai developed strong traditions of club and domestic cricket very early. Players such as Gavaskar and Kapil Dev inspired generations of young people to dream big. That victory in 1983 changed the confidence and popularity of the home side completely. Later triumphs, including the 2011 win at home, strengthened its place in public life. Television brought matches into millions of homes and made leading players familiar national figures.\nDomestic tournaments such as the Ranji Trophy remain the foundation for selecting talented young players. Coaches in academies across Indore, Ranchi and Bengaluru train boys and girls with modern methods. The Indian Premier League, started in 2008, mixed sport with entertainment and large commercial investment. It gave unknown players from small towns a stage before huge crowds and television audiences. Women's cricket has also grown rapidly, with better contracts, leagues and international recognition in recent years. A crowd of 50,000 people at a major stadium shows the emotional power of the game. Cricket provides employment to players, umpires, ground staff, commentators, trainers and many others.\nYet the dominance of cricket sometimes reduces attention and funding available for other sports in the country. Hockey, football, badminton, wrestling and athletics also deserve grounds, coaches and public encouragement everywhere. Cricket itself teaches teamwork, patience, discipline and the ability to accept defeat with grace. At its best, the game unites people across language, region and religion in shared joy.  Cricket administration in the country is handled by national and state associations that organise tournaments at many levels. The national board coordinates international fixtures, domestic competitions and player contracts centrally. Young players often begin in school teams before progressing through district and state age group tournaments. Fitness standards, video analysis and sports science have become regular parts of professional preparation in India. Former players now work as coaches, selectors, commentators and mentors for the next generation of cricketers. Fans follow matches through radio, television, newspapers and mobile applications in almost every Indian language available. Victories are celebrated collectively, while defeats lead to intense public discussion about selection and strategy nationwide. This deep emotional involvement explains why cricket functions as a shared national conversation in India."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-30", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Saving the tiger", "text": "The tiger is the largest wild cat in the world and a symbol of India's forests. At the beginning of the twentieth century, tigers were found across most of the country. Hunting, loss of forests and a decline in prey reduced their numbers to alarming levels. A national census in the early 1970s suggested that only about eighteen hundred tigers remained in India. Alarmed by this fall, the government launched Project Tiger in 1973 to protect the animal. Jim Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand was among the earliest reserves under this important programme. Special protection was given to forests where tigers could breed without disturbance from human activity. Guards were trained and villages were sometimes relocated from the core areas of reserves.\nOver the decades, the number of tiger reserves in India has grown to more than 50. Parks such as Ranthambore, Kanha, Bandhavgarh and Sundarbans protect different kinds of tiger habitats carefully. Camera traps and scientific counting methods now help experts estimate tiger populations more accurately. According to the 2022 estimation exercise, India holds around thirty-six hundred tigers in the wild. This success has made India home to a majority of the world's remaining wild tigers. Tourism around tiger reserves supports hotels, guides, drivers and local shops in nearby towns. Forest staff risk their lives while protecting animals from poachers and illegal trade networks.\nSaving the tiger also protects entire forests, rivers and many other species living within them. Challenges remain, including conflicts when tigers move into farms and villages near reserve boundaries. Corridors between forests must stay open so that young tigers can disperse safely elsewhere. The future of the tiger depends on balancing conservation with the needs of local people.  The protection of tigers requires cooperation between forest departments, scientists, local communities and the judiciary. Compensation schemes for livestock loss can reduce anger among families living near protected forest areas substantially. In Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra, village eco development committees participate in tourism and forest protection activities regularly. School children visiting reserves learn that a tiger reserve protects water sources and carbon storing forests too. Anti-poaching camps, wireless networks and trained tracker dogs strengthen field protection in remote forest divisions. International cooperation helps control illegal trade in tiger parts across borders with Nepal and Myanmar effectively. Research using radio collars has improved understanding of tiger movement, territory and behaviour in wild habitats. Continued success will require patience, funding and respect for both wildlife and forest dwelling people."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-31", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How milk reaches the city", "text": "Every morning, millions of litres of milk travel from rural farms to homes in Indian cities. The journey begins in villages where farmers milk cows and buffaloes in the early hours. At a village collection centre, milk is tested for fat content and basic quality standards. Farmers in Gujarat, Punjab and Uttar Pradesh pour their milk into clean cans for weighing. Payment is often linked to the quantity and richness of milk supplied by each farmer. From the village centre, insulated tankers carry the milk to a district processing dairy plant. Chilling the milk quickly to about 4 degrees is essential to prevent it from spoiling. Cooperative unions such as Amul have organised this system for farmers since the year 1946.\nAt the dairy plant, milk passes through cleaning, pasteurisation, standardisation and careful quality testing procedures. Pasteurisation heats the milk to kill harmful bacteria while keeping its natural food value intact. Machines then fill milk into pouches or bottles under strict conditions of cleanliness and safety. Different products are prepared for customers, including toned milk, full cream milk and curd. Trucks leave the plant before dawn so that supplies reach shops across Delhi and Ahmedabad early. Retailers, delivery workers and small tea stalls all depend upon this punctual daily supply chain. A city like Mumbai may receive more than 50 lakh litres on a single busy day.\nBehind this smooth system stand millions of small farmers, most of whom own only a few animals. Cooperatives give them bargaining power, veterinary services and a regular income throughout the year. Delays, heat or poor roads can disturb the chain and cause heavy losses quickly. When we buy a pouch of milk, we rarely think of this long journey.  The dairy sector also creates employment in transport, packaging, veterinary care and equipment supply across India. Private dairies compete with cooperatives in many states, giving farmers alternative buyers for their daily produce. Quality laboratories check for adulteration because consumer trust is essential for the entire milk business nationally. During summer, demand for curd, lassi and ice cream rises sharply in cities like Jaipur. Chilled storage at retail points prevents spoilage and maintains taste throughout the long distribution chain daily. Farmers receive training in clean milking practices, balanced cattle feed and regular vaccination of their animals. Breed improvement programmes aim to raise productivity without ignoring the value of hardy indigenous cattle breeds. This organised system has helped India become the largest milk producing country in the world."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-32", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Forests bring the rain", "text": "People often say that forests bring the rain, and there is strong science behind this belief. Trees release water vapour from their leaves into the air through a process called transpiration. In a large forest, millions of leaves together add enormous moisture to the atmosphere above. This moist air rises, cools and helps in the formation of clouds over the region. Forests in the Western Ghats and Northeast India influence rainfall patterns across surrounding districts significantly. Roots hold the soil together and allow rainwater to sink slowly into the ground below. As a result, springs and streams continue to flow for longer periods after the monsoon. A forest floor covered with leaves also reduces the speed of rainwater during heavy showers.\nWhen forests are cut over large areas, the local climate may become hotter and drier. Bare soil heats quickly, rainfall runs off rapidly and the risk of floods increases downstream. In parts of Madhya Pradesh and Odisha, restored forests have improved water availability for nearby villages. Trees also absorb carbon dioxide, provide shade and support insects that pollinate crops on farms. Nearly 24 percent of Indias land is recorded as forest and tree cover today. Monsoon winds bring most moisture from the ocean, so forests alone cannot create rain everywhere. However, healthy forests help recycle moisture and support rainfall within the wider regional system effectively.\nProtecting forests therefore means protecting farms, rivers and the water security of millions of people. Village communities in Meghalaya guard sacred forests that preserve rare plants and natural springs carefully. Planting trees is useful, but protecting existing mature forests is even more important for rainfall. The old saying reminds us that human prosperity is deeply connected with living green landscapes.  Forest communities have long understood the connection between tree cover and dependable local water sources. Traditional protection of groves in Rajasthan has preserved moisture and biodiversity in otherwise arid landscapes remarkably. Research stations in Dehradun measure how different forest types affect humidity, soil moisture and stream flows. Reforestation with mixed native species supports this cycle better than plantations of a single fast growing tree. Urban residents also benefit when forests around cities moderate temperature and capture rainfall for reservoirs nearby. In Shillong and Coimbatore, citizens groups campaign to protect catchment forests that supply municipal drinking water systems. Every large forest functions like a slow and natural water tower for the surrounding countryside. Recognising this service gives economic and emotional reasons to conserve forests beyond timber value alone."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-33", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The lighthouse keeper", "text": "Along dark and rocky coasts, a lighthouse guides ships safely through the night and storms. Its powerful beam warns sailors about dangerous rocks, shallow water and the nearby shoreline ahead. In earlier times, a keeper lived inside or beside the lighthouse throughout the whole year. Keepers in places such as Mahabalipuram and Dwarka maintained lamps through lonely and difficult nights. Their duties included cleaning the lens, trimming the lamp and recording weather in a register. Before electricity, oil lamps and later pressurised burners produced the light that ships depended upon. The keeper had to climb many stairs several times each night to check the lamp. A mistake or delay could place an entire ship and its crew in danger.\nLife in a lighthouse station was quiet, disciplined and often separated from towns and families. Supplies of food, water and fuel arrived by boat whenever the sea allowed safe landing. Keepers learned to read clouds, winds and tides with remarkable accuracy through long daily experience. They also helped rescue sailors when vessels were wrecked near their stretch of coast. In India, some historic lighthouses are more than 150 years old and still stand tall. The beam from a modern lighthouse may be visible from nearly 40 kilometres at sea. Automation has now replaced resident keepers at many stations with sensors and remote control systems.\nEven in the age of satellite navigation, lighthouses remain useful as reliable visual guides for ships. Tourists visit famous lighthouse towers to enjoy wide views of the sea and coast. The image of the keeper still stands for vigilance, duty and service in isolation. Behind every safe arrival at port, such quiet workers once played an essential part.  The architecture of a lighthouse is designed for visibility, strength and resistance to severe coastal weather. Thick circular walls withstand powerful winds, while the lamp room at the top remains protected carefully. Staircases wind upward inside the tower, allowing the keeper to reach the light in all conditions. Different lighthouses use distinct colour patterns and flash sequences so sailors can identify their exact location. Charts and nautical publications list these identifying characteristics for every major light along the Indian coast. The Directorate General of Lighthouses in New Delhi oversees maintenance and modernisation of these navigational aids nationally. Heritage lighthouses at Chennai and Kollam attract students interested in maritime history and coastal engineering alike. Their continuing presence connects modern shipping with centuries of seafaring tradition along Indian shores."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-34", "diff": "medium", "topic": "The travelling library", "text": "In many villages, books do not wait for readers inside a building but travel to them. A travelling library may be carried in a van, a cart, a boat or even on bicycles. It visits schools, community halls and village squares according to a fixed weekly time table. Children in remote parts of Assam and Rajasthan eagerly await the arrival of the library van. Inside, shelves hold story books, picture books, novels, magazines and volumes for competitive examinations. A librarian registers each borrower and helps readers choose books suited to their age and interest. In Kerala, boat libraries once served villages connected by backwaters and narrow canals. Such services began in some regions more than 70 years ago to spread basic literacy widely.\nThe travelling library does much more than supply reading material to isolated rural communities. It creates a meeting place where children hear stories, discuss books and develop regular reading habits. Women who cannot travel to a distant town library can borrow books near their homes. Health, farming and government scheme information also reaches villages through leaflets carried by the van. A collection of 2,000 books can serve several thousand readers when it circulates properly throughout the year. Volunteers in Maharashtra sometimes take book bags to construction sites for children of migrant workers. Digital tablets are now included in some mobile libraries for access to recorded lessons.\nBooks wear out, fuel costs rise and trained staff are not always easy to find. Local panchayats, schools and reading groups can help by protecting books and preparing reading spaces. When a library travels, it carries imagination and knowledge across barriers of distance and income. For many first generation readers, that small visiting van opens the door to a wider world.  Reading habits developed through mobile libraries often continue long after formal schooling has ended for villagers. Farmers borrow manuals on seeds and animal care, while unemployed youth seek books for examination preparation regularly. Storytelling sessions organised beside the parked van encourage parents to read aloud to their young children. Some services in Tamil Nadu and Karnataka allow readers to request specific titles for the next visit. Donations from publishers, universities and citizens help replace damaged books and widen the available collection steadily. Librarians maintain simple records that reveal which subjects are most popular in each served village. These insights help planners purchase useful books rather than merely increasing the number of volumes randomly. A travelling library therefore acts as a small cultural institution moving continuously through rural India."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-35", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Yoga in modern life", "text": "Yoga developed in India as a discipline connecting the body, breathing and the mind together. Ancient texts describe postures, meditation and ethical practices meant to create balance in daily life. In the modern world, people face long working hours, mental stress and very little movement. Office workers in Delhi, Bengaluru and Pune now attend morning classes before beginning their busy schedules. Simple postures stretch the muscles, improve posture and reduce stiffness caused by continuous desk work. Breathing exercises encourage slower and deeper breathing, which can calm the nervous system considerably. Even 20 minutes of regular practice may help people feel more alert and composed. The International Day of Yoga is celebrated on 21 June in countries around the world.\nScientific studies suggest that yoga can support flexibility, balance, sleep quality and general physical fitness. Doctors sometimes recommend gentle yoga to patients recovering from back pain or lifestyle related problems. However, difficult postures should be learned under trained guidance, especially by older people and beginners. Yoga is not a substitute for medical treatment, but it can complement a healthy routine. Schools in several Indian states include yoga periods to help children concentrate and remain active. Sports teams also use selected exercises for recovery, breathing control and mental preparation before matches. Unlike many fitness activities, yoga requires little equipment and can be practised almost anywhere.\nModern studios and online classes have made yoga widely available, sometimes at high commercial prices. Yet its central values remain simplicity, discipline, self awareness and respect for ones own limits. People of different ages and abilities can adapt the practice to their personal needs safely. In a hurried age, yoga offers a quiet method to restore attention and inner balance.  Yoga has also entered workplaces where wellness programmes encourage short breaks for stretching and mindful breathing. Such sessions can reduce the sense of exhaustion that builds during long hours of screen based work. In Mumbai and Chennai, community halls offer inexpensive classes for senior citizens in the early morning. Practitioners emphasise that progress is gradual and that competition with others has no place in yoga. Regular practice may improve body awareness, encouraging healthier choices about food, rest and daily activity overall. Books and television programmes have introduced basic yoga concepts to households far from any formal studio. Teachers caution that online instruction cannot always correct posture or identify individual physical limitations properly. When practised with patience and sound guidance, yoga remains a practical resource for modern living."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-36", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The Election Commission and free elections", "text": "The Election Commission of India, headquartered at Nirvachan Sadan in New Delhi, holds the constitutional responsibility for conducting free elections to Parliament, State Legislatures, and the offices of President and Vice President across the country. Created under Article 324 of the Constitution on 25 January 1950, just one day before India became a Republic, the Commission began as a single member body headed by Sukumar Sen. Over time, Parliament expanded the institution. Since 1993 it has normally worked as a three member body comprising the Chief Election Commissioner and 2 Election Commissioners.\nWith more than 970 million registered voters in 2024, India administers the largest democratic exercise anywhere on Earth. It requires polling stations in Himalayan villages, desert settlements, forest clearings, and dense metropolitan neighbourhoods alike. The Commission prepares electoral rolls, recognises political parties, allots symbols, announces schedules, and enforces the Model Code of Conduct throughout the campaign period in every constituency.\nElectronic Voting Machines were first used on a limited scale in Kerala during 1982 and were expanded nationwide by 2004. They are now paired with Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail units for added transparency. Booth level officers from State Government departments verify households door to door. Meanwhile, District Election Officers and Returning Officers supervise nomination, scrutiny, polling, counting, and declaration of results under strict legal frameworks. Security deployment, transport planning, communication networks, and training for nearly 15 million polling personnel make each general election a logistical undertaking comparable to a nationwide administrative campaign.\nDespite these strengths, debates continue about appointments, campaign finance, social media regulation, misinformation, and the growing cost of reaching voters across vast constituencies in States like Uttar Pradesh and Maharashtra. Reforms proposed by committees, courts, and civil society groups in New Delhi have repeatedly examined simultaneous elections, state funding, inner party democracy, and stronger disclosure rules for candidates with criminal records. The Supreme Court of India has intervened at several points, directing disclosure of assets, liabilities, and antecedents. This allows voters in Bihar, West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, and Karnataka to judge candidates with fuller information before polling day.\nUltimately, the credibility of the Commission rests not only on constitutional safeguards and a tenure protection of 6 years for Commissioners, but also on public trust. That trust is built through consistent, even handed, and transparent action. When counting in 543 parliamentary constituencies concludes peacefully and losing candidates accept outcomes, the institution demonstrates why procedural fairness matters as much as the final result itself in sustaining democratic faith."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-37", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Moving plates and the rise of the Himalayas", "text": "The Himalayas stretch nearly 2,400 kilometres from Kashmir and Ladakh in the northwest through Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Nepal, Sikkim, Bhutan, and Arunachal Pradesh in the east. They are the product of an immense collision between moving plates of the Earth. According to plate tectonics, the Indian Plate once lay far south in the Indian Ocean, separated from Asia by the ancient Tethys Sea. The sediments of that sea were later folded, faulted, and lifted into high mountain ranges. Geologists estimate that India began moving northwards more than 140 million years ago, travelling at speeds of nearly 15 centimetres a year before slowing considerably as continental collision approached during the Eocene epoch.\nWhen the two landmasses finally met around 50 million years ago, neither could sink easily beneath the other. The crust therefore thickened, buckled, and rose steadily into the Greater Himalayas, the Lesser Himalayas, and the outer Shivalik ranges. Evidence for this dramatic history is visible in marine fossils found near Spiti, in folded limestone bands across Ladakh, and in deep river gorges cut by the Indus, Sutlej, Kali Gandaki, and Brahmaputra systems.\nInstruments maintained by the Geological Survey of India, the Wadia Institute in Dehradun, and universities in Delhi and Roorkee now measure present day movement. They use Global Positioning System stations fixed across the mountain belt. Those measurements show that India still pushes into Asia by roughly 4.5 centimetres each year, while the Himalayas themselves continue rising by several millimetres annually in certain actively deforming sectors. Much of this stored strain is released through earthquakes. The devastating Nepal earthquake of 2015, which measured 7.8 in magnitude, reshaped valleys, settlements, and infrastructure across central Nepal and northern Bihar.\nBecause rivers, glaciers, forests, and nearly 50 million people depend directly upon these young and fragile mountains, understanding plate movement is not merely an academic exercise for laboratories in India and abroad. Engineers designing tunnels in Himachal Pradesh, dams in Uttarakhand, roads in Arunachal Pradesh, and rail links in Jammu work throughout difficult terrain. They must account for fractured rock, landslides, seismic zoning, and sudden changes in river courses.\nThe story of the Himalayas therefore connects deep time, measured in hundreds of millions of years, with present day questions. These questions concern safety, settlement, water security, and environmental stewardship across northern South Asia as a whole. Meanwhile, continued convergence ensures that future earthquakes, glacial lakes, and slope failures across Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, and neighbouring Nepal will remain linked to forces operating far beneath the visible landscape over human timescales."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-38", "diff": "hard", "topic": "India's semiconductor mission", "text": "Semiconductors, the tiny chips that control phones, cars, trains, medical devices, satellites, and defence systems, have become strategic assets. Their supply chains now shape trade relations between the United States, China, Taiwan, Japan, and South Korea. India imports a substantial share of its electronics and nearly all advanced chips. It launched the India Semiconductor Mission in December 2021 under the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology in New Delhi.\nThe programme carries an outlay of 76,000 crore rupees and offers fiscal support of up to 50 percent for fabrication plants, compound semiconductor units, assembly facilities, and design linked incentives across participating States. Its stated ambition is to move India from a large consumer market towards a credible manufacturing and design hub within the global value chain.\nGujarat emerged as an early centre after Micron Technology announced an assembly and testing facility at Sanand in 2023. Meanwhile, proposals linked with Tata Electronics in Dholera and Assam advanced discussions about fabrication, packaging, employment, and supplier ecosystems. Such plants demand uninterrupted power, ultrapure water measured in millions of litres daily, vibration free construction, and specialised gases. They also need thousands of engineers trained in process technology, materials science, and yield management at exacting global standards.\nInstitutions including IIT Bombay, IIT Madras, IISc Bengaluru, and the Semiconductor Laboratory in Mohali are therefore expanding research, fabrication access, and workforce programmes to support an industry that India largely missed during earlier decades. Design strength remains an important advantage, since engineers in Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Noida, and Pune already contribute to chip design for multinational firms even though manufacturing capacity at home has stayed limited. Challenges remain formidable, because a modern fabrication plant can cost more than 10 billion dollars and technology cycles move within 2.5 years. Established producers in Taiwan and South Korea possess decades of accumulated operational knowledge.\nWater recycling targets above 90 percent, reliable logistics through ports in Gujarat and Maharashtra, and long term policy stability will determine whether announced projects in Sanand, Dholera, and Jagiroad become durable industrial clusters. If India succeeds, the mission could reduce import vulnerability and create high skill employment. It could also strengthen electronics manufacturing in Tamil Nadu and Karnataka, and secure supplies for railways, space missions, and renewable energy systems nationwide. Analysts in New Delhi, Mumbai, and Bengaluru therefore judge the mission less by announcements than by sustained yields, local supplier depth, export readiness, and whether design startups can access fabrication without depending entirely upon overseas foundries."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-39", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Crop insurance and the economics of risk", "text": "Farming in India remains exposed to drought, flood, hailstorm, pest attack, and unseasonal rainfall. A single failed season can therefore erase savings, force distress borrowing, and push households in Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, or Rajasthan towards long term indebtedness. Crop insurance attempts to spread this individual risk across a large pool of farmers, insurers, and governments. It converts an unpredictable catastrophic loss into a predictable premium paid before sowing begins each season.\nUnder the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana, launched in 2016 by the Ministry of Agriculture in New Delhi, farmers pay only 1.5 percent for rabi crops and 2 percent for kharif crops. The remaining premium is shared between the Union Government and State Governments. Insurance companies selected through bidding carry the obligation to assess damage and settle claims after harvest across notified districts.\nThe economics of the scheme depend upon accurate yield estimation, timely weather data, transparent loss assessment, and participation by farmers whose fields face differing levels of risk within the same insurance unit. Satellite imagery supplied through agencies in Hyderabad, drone surveys in Gujarat, and crop cutting experiments supervised by State Agriculture Departments are increasingly combined. The aim is to reduce disputes about whether losses arose from localised calamities or widespread shortfalls.\nCritics argue that delayed claims, low awareness in Odisha and West Bengal, and the withdrawal of some States from the scheme at different points have weakened trust among cultivators who need liquidity immediately after crop failure. Supporters respond that claim payouts have often exceeded premiums collected by several multiples during drought years, demonstrating that risk pooling can transfer substantial resources towards affected regions when verification systems function properly.\nA deeper difficulty is moral hazard and adverse selection, because farmers who expect losses may enrol more readily. Meanwhile, low risk farmers sometimes withdraw, raising average costs across the insured pool over successive seasons. Weather index products, piloted in parts of Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Telangana, attempt to pay claims automatically when rainfall recorded at reference stations falls below agreed thresholds such as 75 percent of normal. Although index insurance reduces field inspection costs, it can disappoint a farmer whose own field floods while the district station records only moderate rainfall during the same critical week of crop growth. Sustainable crop insurance, nearly a decade after the 2016 launch, therefore requires fast payments, credible data, affordable premiums, and complementary investment in irrigation, drainage, seed resilience, and climate advisories throughout vulnerable agricultural districts. Pilot districts in Maharashtra and Gujarat show that combining insurance with advisories, soil health cards, and timely credit can reduce distress sales after harvest failure more effectively than compensation delivered many months later."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-40", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Wetlands as stores of carbon", "text": "Wetlands, including marshes, floodplains, mangroves, peatlands, lakes, and tidal flats, store disproportionately large quantities of carbon. They do so because waterlogged soils slow decomposition and allow plant material to accumulate underground for centuries without fully breaking down. Global assessments suggest that peatlands cover barely 3 percent of land surface yet hold nearly 30 percent of soil carbon, a concentration that makes their drainage or burning especially damaging for the climate system.\nIn India, wetlands such as Keoladeo in Rajasthan, Chilika in Odisha, Loktak in Manipur, and the Sundarbans in West Bengal are rich carbon stores. They combine this storage with fisheries, flood moderation, groundwater recharge, bird habitat, and livelihoods for surrounding communities. Mangrove soils along the coasts of Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh, and Odisha can bury carbon at rates approaching 0.2 to 0.6 tonnes per hectare annually under favourable conditions.\nWhen wetlands are drained for housing, industry, intensive aquaculture, or poorly planned infrastructure, oxygen enters formerly saturated soils and microbes consume stored organic matter rapidly. Carbon dioxide then returns to the atmosphere after long periods of stable burial. Fires in dried peatlands in Indonesia during 2015 illustrate the danger clearly. Pressure on floodplains around Bengaluru, Chennai, and Srinagar shows how quickly development choices can convert an ecological asset into a continuing source of emissions and flood risk.\nThe Ramsar Convention, which India joined in 1982, now lists more than 80 Indian sites covering roughly 1.3 million hectares, although designation alone cannot prevent encroachment, pollution, altered inflows, or invasive species from degrading ecological character. Restoration projects in Punjab, Haryana, and Tamil Nadu increasingly measure success not only through bird counts and water quality, but also through soil carbon retained across seasons of flood and drought.\nUnlike tree planting, which stores much carbon above ground in biomass vulnerable to fire and harvesting, wetland protection preserves dense below ground stocks. These stocks have accumulated slowly over hundreds or even thousands of years in some landscapes. Climate policy from the Ministry of Environment in New Delhi therefore increasingly links wetland inventories, State Wetland Authorities, and carbon accounting frameworks with urban planning in Guwahati, Kochi, Indore, and other rapidly expanding cities. Protecting a marsh may appear less visible than building a seawall, yet each hectare conserved can avoid emissions equivalent to several tonnes of carbon dioxide. It can also buffer storm surges and sustain migratory birds. Carbon finance pilots in Odisha, West Bengal, and Gujarat after 2020 suggest that verified wetland protection could channel payments towards panchayats and fishing communities, provided monitoring costs stay below 1.5 percent of project value annually."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-41", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Sanitation and public health in cities", "text": "Urban sanitation determines whether dense settlement in cities such as Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, and Kolkata supports healthy productivity. Poor sanitation instead concentrates exposure to contaminated water, untreated waste, overflowing drains, and disease bearing vectors near homes and workplaces. The Swachh Bharat Mission Urban, launched on 2 October 2014 by the Government of India, sought to eliminate open defecation, expand household toilets, and improve door to door collection. It also sought to process a rising share of municipal solid waste across statutory towns.\nBy 2023, official dashboards reported construction of more than 63 lakh household toilets and community facilities. Meanwhile, daily waste generation in urban India had approached nearly 1.6 lakh tonnes, requiring collection, segregation, transport, processing, and safe disposal every single day. These figures reveal both administrative scale and the continuing operational challenge of maintaining systems after construction targets have been announced, photographed, and formally certified by local bodies.\nPublic health research from institutions in New Delhi, Pune, and Chennai repeatedly links inadequate sanitation with diarrhoeal disease, intestinal worms, stunting among children, lost school attendance, and avoidable expenditure by households already facing insecure wages and high rents. Contaminated groundwater in parts of Patna, Lucknow, and Indore, together with faecal sludge emptied informally into drains, shows why toilets alone cannot solve the problem. The problem extends through pits, sewers, pumping stations, treatment plants, and worker safety systems. Manual scavenging, though legally prohibited under Acts passed in 1993 and 2013, persists in hazardous forms when municipalities and contractors fail to mechanise sewer cleaning or provide protective equipment, training, and dignified rehabilitation to sanitation workers.\nCities in Gujarat and Telangana have experimented with scheduled desludging, tracking vehicles, and faecal sludge treatment plants designed to serve settlements not connected to conventional underground sewer networks. Behaviour also matters, because segregation at source in Kochi, Mysuru, and Alappuzha has succeeded where households, resident associations, schools, and municipal staff share clear routines. They share feedback and accountability for wet waste, dry waste, and hazardous material as well. A processing rate that rises from 17 percent in 2014 towards 75 percent or more means little if collected material is remixed during transport or rejected by facilities lacking markets for compost, recyclables, and refuse derived fuel.\nDurable urban sanitation therefore depends upon finance, engineering, public health surveillance, worker dignity, and citizen cooperation. These must operate together throughout neighbourhoods rather than during short cleanliness campaigns alone. Experience from Surat after 1994 and from Indore after 2017 shows that sustained administrative attention, ward level accountability, and citizen feedback can transform sanitation outcomes within 5 years even without extraordinary technological investment."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-42", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Deep-sea fishing and its limits", "text": "Deep sea fishing takes vessels far beyond shallow coastal waters. In those distant zones, depth, pressure, darkness, and distance from shore create both valuable catches and serious ecological and human risks for crews. Indian fleets operating from Veraval in Gujarat, Kochi in Kerala, Chennai in Tamil Nadu, and Visakhapatnam in Andhra Pradesh pursue tuna, squid, deep water shrimp, and other species across the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal. Within the Exclusive Economic Zone, which extends 200 nautical miles from the coast under international law, India holds sovereign rights over marine resources while also bearing responsibility for conservation and management.\nModern trawlers use sonar, satellite navigation, refrigerated holds, and synthetic nets to locate shoals quickly. They sometimes remain at sea for 30 to 45 days before returning with marketable catch. The biological limits of deep sea ecosystems are stricter than their vast appearance suggests. Many species grow slowly, mature late, and reproduce infrequently in cold water where food supplies remain sparse throughout much of the year. Orange roughy fisheries elsewhere in the world collapsed after intensive harvesting removed individuals that were already 50 or even 100 years old, illustrating why catch expansion without stock assessment can destroy future income rapidly.\nBottom trawling can also damage corals, sponges, and seamount communities around Lakshadweep and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. Meanwhile, discarded bycatch wastes juvenile fish, turtles, and other organisms drawn into nets designed for a narrow commercial target. Fuel subsidies, rising diesel prices above 90 rupees per litre in several ports, and competition from foreign vessels near maritime boundaries further complicate the economics facing Indian boat owners and traditional fishers.\nRegulation through the Indian Maritime Zones Act, State Marine Fishing Regulation Acts, and proposed national legislation has attempted to balance competing interests. These interests include mechanised fleets, artisanal communities, export markets in Japan and Europe, and conservation duties monitored from New Delhi. Scientific surveys by institutes in Kochi and Mumbai in 2022 and 2023 recommended precautionary catch limits, closed seasons of 61 days on parts of the coast, and better vessel tracking for safety.\nSustainable deep sea fishing therefore depends on respecting regeneration rates, protecting breeding habitats, and reducing wasteful bycatch by at least 25 percent where feasible. It also depends on ensuring that profits do not rest upon exhausting slow renewing marine capital. Cooperatives in Kerala and Gujarat have consequently begun discussing selective gear, onboard observers, traceability standards demanded by buyers in Europe, and shared cold chain facilities that reduce pressure to maximise volume on every voyage."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-43", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The stepwells of western India", "text": "Stepwells, known regionally as vav in Gujarat, baoli in Rajasthan and Delhi, and bawdi in parts of Maharashtra and Karnataka, are subterranean structures. They combine water storage, social gathering, religious symbolism, and remarkable architectural imagination within a single descending form. Built where rainfall is seasonal and groundwater lies deep below arid surfaces, these wells allowed communities in Ahmedabad, Patan, Jaipur, Jodhpur, and Bundi to reach water as levels fell during hot months before the monsoon returned each year.\nBroad staircases, layered pavilions, carved galleries, and cool chambers transformed a practical necessity into a civic monument. Such monuments were often commissioned by queens, merchants, and local rulers between the 7th and 19th centuries across western India. Rani ki Vav at Patan, constructed during the 11th century under Queen Udayamati of the Solanki dynasty, extends nearly 64 metres in length and descends through 7 levels of sculpted panels. Chand Baori at Abhaneri in Rajasthan, with approximately 3,500 narrow steps arranged across 13 levels, demonstrates the geometric discipline that medieval builders achieved. They worked without modern surveying instruments, mechanised excavation, or reinforced concrete construction techniques.\nAdalaj Vav near Ahmedabad, completed in 1498, blends Hindu and Islamic architectural motifs through carved pillars, ornamental niches, and a five storey descent that remains noticeably cooler than the sunlit settlement above during peak summer. These structures were not isolated curiosities, since scholars have documented more than 3,000 stepwells across India. Many now stand silted, encroached upon, polluted, or disconnected from the aquifers that once sustained surrounding neighbourhoods and caravan routes.\nTheir decline accelerated during the 19th and 20th centuries as piped supply, private borewells, municipal systems, and colonial public health concerns shifted attention away from communal groundwater architecture. Restoration efforts led by the Archaeological Survey of India, State Tourism Departments in Gujarat and Rajasthan, and heritage groups in Delhi since 2014 have revived interest in stepwells as working lessons for water sensitive urban design.\nWhen cleaned and connected with recharge systems, selected baolis in Delhi and Ahmedabad can demonstrate the value of decentralised storage, shade, and evaporation control. Together with community stewardship, these features reduce dependence upon distant reservoirs during periods of scarcity. In an era when groundwater in parts of Rajasthan has fallen below 30 metres and urban demand rises by nearly 2.5 percent annually in some districts, the stepwell offers historical evidence that beauty and utility need not compete. Scholars at universities in Ahmedabad, Jaipur, and Delhi argue that stepwells deserve recognition not as ornamental ruins but as sophisticated groundwater institutions whose maintenance once involved ritual calendars, craft guilds, and village obligations."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-44", "diff": "hard", "topic": "How central banks control inflation", "text": "Central banks control inflation primarily by influencing the cost and availability of money throughout the banking system. In doing so, they shape spending, borrowing, investment, and expectations among households and firms in India, America, Europe, and other major economies. The Reserve Bank of India, established in 1935 and headquartered in Mumbai, conducts monetary policy through a Monetary Policy Committee created in 2016 with 6 members who vote on the policy repo rate. Since an amendment to the Reserve Bank of India Act, the government has set an inflation target of 4 percent for consumer prices, with a tolerance band extending 2 percentage points above and below that central figure.\nWhen the committee raises the repo rate, commercial banks in Delhi, Bengaluru, Chennai, and Kolkata generally raise lending rates. This makes mortgages, vehicle loans, and business credit more expensive for borrowers across sectors. Higher rates are intended to cool demand gradually, discourage leveraged purchases, and support the rupee against imported inflation. They also signal that the central bank will not allow temporary price shocks to become permanent expectations among workers and businesses.\nConversely, when growth weakens sharply and inflation remains contained, rate reductions can lower a typical borrowing cost from 9.5 percent towards 8.75 percent. Such cuts encourage investment in factories, housing, logistics, and services across States such as Gujarat, Maharashtra, and Tamil Nadu. The Reserve Bank also uses open market operations, cash reserve requirements first formalised decades earlier, standing deposit facilities, and liquidity adjustment operations to manage conditions in overnight money markets each working day. These technical tools matter because a policy announcement changes real behaviour only when liquidity in the banking system transmits the signal consistently to deposit rates, bond yields, and retail lending products.\nInflation in India often originates from food and fuel. A monsoon failure in Madhya Pradesh, supply disruption in onions from Maharashtra, or crude oil rising above 90 dollars per barrel can push prices independently of domestic credit conditions. Raising interest rates cannot produce rainfall, repair transport bottlenecks, or expand storage for tomatoes and potatoes, although it can prevent second round effects when firms raise prices and workers demand compensating wages throughout the economy.\nCoordination with fiscal policy in New Delhi nevertheless matters, since targeted supply measures, buffer stocks, trade decisions, and reduced borrowing can support the central bank without forcing rates to carry the entire burden of stabilisation. Credibility accumulated through transparent communication since 2016 is itself an instrument, because anchored expectations reduce the output sacrifice required whenever inflation rises above 6 percent."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-45", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Retreating glaciers of the Himalayas", "text": "Glaciers across the Himalayas are retreating under rising temperatures, changing snowfall patterns, and deposition of black carbon. The retreat has consequences for rivers that sustain farms, cities, hydropower, and ecosystems from Ladakh through Uttarakhand, Nepal, Sikkim, and Bhutan. The Hindu Kush Himalaya Assessment, released in 2019 by ICIMOD in Kathmandu, warned that even if global warming were limited to 1.5 degrees Celsius, roughly one third of regional glacier ice could disappear by 2100. Under higher emissions pathways, losses could approach 75 percent in the eastern Himalayas, where smaller glaciers in Arunachal Pradesh and Sikkim respond especially quickly to sustained warming during summer melt seasons.\nIn India, monitored glaciers such as Gangotri in Uttarakhand, Dokriani in the Garhwal region, and several systems in Himachal Pradesh have shown terminus retreat measured in metres per year over recent decades. Scientific teams from the Geological Survey of India, universities in Jammu and Dehradun, and institutes in Bengaluru combine several kinds of evidence. They use field stakes, satellite imagery dating back to 1980, automatic weather stations, and hydrological gauges to separate seasonal snow from long term ice loss.\nTheir findings are not uniform, because debris covered tongues in the Karakoram, including parts of the Siachen region, have sometimes behaved differently from cleaner glaciers in Uttarakhand and Himachal Pradesh exposed directly to warmer air. Nevertheless, the broad pattern since 2000 shows negative mass balance in many basins, thinning rates near 0.5 metres water equivalent annually in selected catchments, and expanding proglacial lakes behind unstable moraine dams. The South Lhonak lake outburst in Sikkim during October 2023, which damaged the Teesta III dam at Chungthang, demonstrated the danger. Stored meltwater can convert gradual change into sudden destruction downstream.\nFor the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra basins, glacier retreat initially increases runoff during peak melt decades before reducing dry season reliability once ice reserves become substantially smaller than historical volumes recorded around 1970. Nearly 240 million people live within the greater Himalayan region, while around 1.65 billion depend upon its river systems downstream in India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, and China according to widely cited regional assessments.\nAdaptation therefore requires early warning systems in Uttarakhand and Sikkim, safer hydropower design in Himachal Pradesh, and diversified storage. It also requires emissions reductions negotiated in forums from New Delhi to Geneva and New York. Protecting mountain communities cannot wait until shrinking ice makes present assumptions about irrigation, drinking water, and flood risk unusable across northern India. Monitoring networks expanded after 2019 now transmit data from stations above 4,000 metres in Ladakh and Uttarakhand. This allows scientists in Dehradun and Bengaluru to issue earlier warnings when lake levels rise by 0.5 metres seasonally."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-46", "diff": "hard", "topic": "The language families of India", "text": "India possesses extraordinary linguistic diversity. The Census of 2011 recorded more than 19,500 raw linguistic affiliations, which officials grouped into 121 languages spoken by at least 10,000 people each across the Union. Most of these languages belong to four principal families, namely Indo Aryan across northern and central regions and Dravidian across southern States. The others are Austroasiatic in parts of eastern and central India and Tibeto Burman throughout Himalayan and northeastern districts.\nHindi, Bengali, Marathi, Telugu, Tamil, Urdu, Gujarati, Kannada, Odia, and Malayalam together account for the overwhelming majority of mother tongue speakers. Meanwhile, hundreds of smaller languages sustain local knowledge in Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Assam, Meghalaya, and Arunachal Pradesh. The Constitution of India recognises 22 languages in the Eighth Schedule, a list that expanded from 14 in 1950 through amendments in 1967, 1992, and 2003 as communities secured recognition.\nIndo Aryan languages such as Hindi in Uttar Pradesh, Punjabi in Punjab, Assamese in Assam, and Odia in Odisha developed through long contact with Sanskrit, Prakrit, Persian, and numerous regional speech forms over many centuries. Dravidian languages, including Tamil in Tamil Nadu, Kannada in Karnataka, Malayalam in Kerala, and Telugu across Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, possess literary traditions extending back more than 2,000 years in the case of classical Tamil inscriptions and Sangam literature.\nAustroasiatic languages like Santali, Mundari, and Ho preserve agricultural vocabularies, oral epics, and ecological knowledge among Adivasi communities in Jharkhand, Odisha, West Bengal, and Chhattisgarh. However, many younger speakers face pressure to shift towards dominant regional languages. Tibeto Burman languages spoken in Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Sikkim, and the Northeast display remarkable internal variety across short geographic distances shaped by mountains, valleys, migration, and historical trade. Language policy in schools operated from New Delhi, State Capitals such as Patna, Lucknow, Guwahati, and Thiruvananthapuram, and local bodies must therefore balance national communication, mother tongue learning, employment mobility, and cultural survival within classrooms serving highly mixed populations.\nResearch supported by UNESCO and Indian universities suggests that children who begin literacy in a familiar home language during the first 5 or 6 years often transfer reading skills more securely into additional languages later. Yet at least 197 Indian languages have been classified as endangered in international assessments since 2010, particularly where speaker communities number below 10,000 and intergenerational transmission weakens under economic and educational pressure. Documenting grammars, recording oral histories in Nagaland and Mizoram, and preparing digital corpora can assist revival. Nevertheless, living use in homes, markets, media, and schools remains decisive."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-47", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Space debris around the Earth", "text": "Space debris consists of defunct satellites, spent rocket stages, fragments from collisions, and tiny particles travelling at extremely high velocities. These objects move in orbits that are increasingly essential for communication, navigation, weather forecasting, defence, and scientific observation around Earth. The United States Space Surveillance Network and agencies in Europe, Russia, Japan, and India track more than 34,000 objects larger than 10 centimetres. Statistical models, meanwhile, estimate over 1 million fragments between 1 and 10 centimetres in low Earth orbit.\nAt relative speeds approaching 15 kilometres per second, even a paint fleck measuring 0.5 centimetres can damage solar panels, puncture equipment, or force the International Space Station to alter altitude to avoid a predicted conjunction. India, through ISRO in Bengaluru and the NETRA project approved in 2019, has expanded radar and telescope capacity to protect satellites launched from Sriharikota and operated for users across India.\nThe debris problem intensified after events such as the 2007 anti satellite test by China and the 2009 collision between Iridium 33 and Cosmos 2251. India's Mission Shakti demonstration followed in March 2019 at an altitude near 300 kilometres. Each event produced clouds of fragments whose behaviour differed according to altitude, solar activity, and atmospheric drag. It also depended on the construction materials involved in the original spacecraft or intercept vehicle destroyed during the event. Low orbit objects below 600 kilometres may re enter within 25 years, whereas fragments near 1,000 kilometres can persist for centuries, gradually spreading into shells that threaten operational systems in polar and sun synchronous orbits.\nCommercial constellations operated by firms in the United States and Europe, sometimes involving more than 5,000 active satellites from a single network, increase conjunction warnings and demand automated manoeuvring standards. International guidelines issued through the United Nations Committee in Vienna, the Inter Agency Space Debris Coordination Committee, and national regulators in New Delhi and Washington recommend several practices. These include passivation, disposal orbits, collision avoidance, and post mission removal within defined periods after operational life ends. Technologies under testing since 2021 include robotic capture arms in Japan, nets and harpoons in Europe, drag sails, and dedicated removal missions designed to deorbit large rocket bodies before they fragment further.\nNo cleanup technology can succeed economically if launches continue adding derelict mass faster than operators remove it, especially in congested bands between 700 and 900 kilometres used by Earth observation satellites. Preserving access to space therefore requires design discipline, shared tracking data, and enforceable licensing in India and abroad. It also requires a cultural shift from treating orbit as limitless to managing it as finite infrastructure."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-48", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Heat islands in big cities", "text": "Urban heat islands arise when built up districts in Delhi, Ahmedabad, Mumbai, Chennai, and Hyderabad become measurably warmer than surrounding rural areas. The cause lies in concrete, asphalt, metal roofs, waste heat, and reduced vegetation, which alter how surfaces absorb and release energy. Studies by the India Meteorological Department, IIT Delhi, and municipal agencies have recorded nighttime differences of 3.5 to 6 degrees Celsius between dense central neighbourhoods and greener peripheral zones during intense summer conditions in May and June.\nDark roads and rooftops absorb solar radiation throughout the day. Meanwhile, narrow streets between tall buildings restrict ventilation and trap heat released by vehicles, air conditioners, generators, and industrial equipment across commercial districts. Unlike a brief afternoon peak, elevated nighttime temperatures prevent the human body from recovering fully, increasing risks for older residents, outdoor workers, infants, and households in Bengaluru or Lucknow without dependable cooling.\nAhmedabad became a prominent policy example after the severe heatwave of 2010, when temperatures touched 46.8 degrees Celsius. Municipal authorities subsequently prepared India's first Heat Action Plan in 2013 with support from health experts. The plan combined early warnings from agencies in New Delhi and Gandhinagar, public messaging in Gujarati, Hindi, and English, cool shelters, adjusted work advice, and hospital preparedness across wards where vulnerability mapping identified dense settlements and large elderly populations. Similar approaches have since spread to more than 100 cities and districts, although implementation quality varies according to budgets, staffing, data resolution, and coordination between municipal corporations, State Disaster Management Authorities, and local health departments.\nSatellite land surface temperature maps at resolutions near 30 metres now allow planners in Pune and Jaipur to identify hotspots. These hotspots occur around transport depots, industrial estates, informal settlements, and newly developed layouts lacking tree cover. Mitigation requires more than emergency response, because urban design choices made in Surat, Indore, Kochi, and Nagpur can either intensify or moderate heat exposure for the next 50 years of metropolitan growth.\nCool roofs using reflective coatings can reduce indoor temperatures by 2.5 degrees in low income housing. Restoring lakes, planting diverse native trees, protecting wetlands, and using permeable surfaces helps neighbourhoods shed heat through shade and evaporation. Building codes revised after 2016 increasingly address orientation, ventilation, insulation, and external shading, yet enforcement remains uneven where developers prioritise saleable floor area over long term thermal comfort and public health. As Indian cities add millions of residents by 2040, treating heat as core infrastructure rather than seasonal weather will determine whether growth remains liveable."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-49", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Generic medicines and how they work", "text": "Generic medicines are pharmaceutical products marketed after the patent protection on an original brand has expired. They contain the same active ingredient, strength, dosage form, and intended therapeutic use as the reference product approved earlier. In India, the Central Drugs Standard Control Organisation in New Delhi and State Licensing Authorities in Mumbai, Chennai, Hyderabad, and Ahmedabad regulate manufacturing licences, inspections, labelling, and quality standards for companies supplying pharmacies across the country.\nA generic tablet of paracetamol 500 milligrams, for example, must deliver the same active substance as its branded counterpart, although inactive ingredients affecting colour, taste, binding, or shelf life may differ between manufacturers. Bioequivalence studies normally require the rate and extent of absorption in healthy volunteers to fall within an accepted range of 80 to 125 percent relative to the reference medicine under controlled conditions.\nLower prices arise principally because generic manufacturers do not repeat the original discovery programme, extensive clinical development, or promotional expenditure undertaken by innovator companies. Those companies operate in the United States, Europe, Japan, and India over periods exceeding 10 years. Competition among multiple suppliers can reduce the price of widely used treatments for diabetes, hypertension, infection, and pain by 60 to 90 percent. This expands access for households in Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, and West Bengal with limited insurance coverage.\nThe Jan Aushadhi programme, relaunched in 2016 and coordinated from New Delhi, supplies medicines through thousands of dedicated stores. In such stores, a strip priced at 25 rupees in the open market may cost substantially less for chronic patients. Doctors in public hospitals in Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Delhi, and Himachal Pradesh are encouraged to prescribe by generic name so that pharmacists can dispense affordable equivalents without altering the intended therapy.\nQuality concerns nevertheless require serious attention, because substandard production, poor storage above 30 degrees Celsius, falsified products, and weak supply chains can undermine confidence. This remains true even where the underlying science of equivalence is well established internationally. Regulatory reforms after 2020 expanded risk based inspections, digital tracking pilots in Gujarat and Maharashtra, and public testing reports intended to identify manufacturers whose samples repeatedly fail dissolution, assay, or contamination standards at government laboratories. For narrow therapeutic index medicines, including some treatments used after transplantation in Bengaluru or for epilepsy in Lucknow, clinicians may monitor patients closely when switching suppliers despite formal bioequivalence approval by regulators. When manufacturing is reliable and prescribing is transparent, generics allow health systems serving 1.4 billion people to treat more patients with the same budget."}, {"tid": "hcm-b1-50", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Managing watersheds in dry regions", "text": "A watershed is the land area from which rainfall drains towards a common stream, tank, reservoir, or river. This makes it a practical unit for managing soil, vegetation, groundwater, and livelihoods together in dry regions of India. In districts across Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Madhya Pradesh, annual rainfall may remain below 700 millimetres while potential evaporation exceeds 1,800 millimetres. Capturing brief monsoon flows therefore determines whether wells, farms, and drinking water systems survive the long dry season.\nWatershed programmes promoted since the 1990s by ministries in New Delhi, State Rural Development Departments, and organisations in Pune and Hyderabad have combined contour trenches, check dams, farm ponds, pasture protection, and afforestation across treated micro watersheds. The celebrated work at Ralegan Siddhi in Maharashtra and Hivare Bazar in Ahmednagar district showed how collective rules on grazing, groundwater extraction, and cropping could complement physical structures. Those structures were built between 1975 and 2005.\nSuccessful treatment begins on ridge lines, where grasses, native trees, and stone bunds across slopes in Satara, Solapur, Anantapur, and Kurnool slow runoff before it gathers destructive speed. Left unchecked, the water strips fertile topsoil from cultivated fields below. Moving downslope, gully plugs, nala bunds, percolation tanks, and recharge shafts are located according to geology, fracture patterns, drainage lines, and village priorities rather than through uniform engineering templates imposed without local consultation or maintenance arrangements.\nEvaluations covering projects completed between 2009 and 2019 frequently report water table rises of 1.5 to 3 metres, expanded protective irrigation, and reduced seasonal migration. They also report cropping intensity gains near 25 percent where institutions remained active after external funding ended. Where user groups in Rajasthan or Telangana failed to repair breaches, desilt structures, or resolve upstream downstream disputes, benefits declined rapidly despite impressive initial construction statistics recorded by implementing agencies and district administrations.\nClimate change now complicates older assumptions, because rainfall in Marathwada, Bundelkhand, and Rayalaseema increasingly arrives through fewer but more intense events, with storms delivering 80 to 120 millimetres within 24 hours and overwhelming structures designed using historical averages from 1980 or 1990. Modern watershed management therefore integrates weather advisories from Pune, groundwater budgeting in every gram panchayat, and crop choices suited to available water. It also integrates transparent social audits involving women, landless workers, pastoralists, and downstream hamlets across the drainage system. Durable success depends less upon counting structures than upon sustaining the institutions that allocate scarce water fairly after the project vehicles, engineers, and inaugural ceremonies have disappeared from the village landscape."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-01", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Anganwadi centres and child nutrition", "text": "In almost every village and town lane, there is a small centre for young children. It is called an anganwadi, and it opens early in the morning. A local woman runs the centre with the help of an assistant. Children below the age of six come here every day. They play, sing songs and learn simple things with joy. The centre gives them a hot cooked meal at midday. Small children also get take home food for the evening. Pregnant women and new mothers come to the centre as well. They receive extra food to keep them strong and healthy. The worker weighs each child on a scale every month. She keeps a chart to see if the child is growing well. If a child looks weak, she informs the health staff at once. Health workers visit the centre on fixed days of the month. They give drops and injections to protect children from disease. The centre is thus the first school and the first clinic of a child. On the weighing day, mothers sit in a row with their babies in their laps. The worker calls each child by name and notes the weight with care. After food, they rest for a while on clean mats spread on the floor.\n\nThere are more than fourteen lakh such centres in the country. They serve over seven crore young children in total. Crore of women and lakhs of young girls also benefit from them. The worker visits homes to talk to young mothers. She teaches them about clean water, feeding and rest. She tells them when to take the child to a hospital. Young girls who have left school get food support here. They also learn about health and a balanced diet. Many centres now have better buildings with toilets and kitchens. Some have small gardens where vegetables are grown for meals. A phone app helps the worker record the weight of each child. The data goes to officers who watch the progress of each area. On festival days, mothers gather at the centre for talks and songs. The centre binds the whole lane or village together. For a poor family, it is a place of trust and help. The worker gets training from time to time to improve her work. Mothers are taught to wash hands before cooking and feeding the baby. The centre also keeps a small first aid box for minor cuts and burns. The anganwadi worker is known to every child by name. Her work is quiet, daily and full of care. She lays the base for a healthy and bright next generation."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-02", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Ayushman Arogya Mandir health centres", "text": "Every village cluster now has a small health centre near the homes of people. Earlier, a sick person had to travel far to a town hospital. Even for a simple fever, the family lost a full day. The new centres have changed this old and hard routine. More than one hundred and eighty thousand such centres now work in the country. A trained health officer and a nurse serve at each centre. One centre serves about five thousand people in the plains. In hills and tribal areas, it serves a smaller number of people. The centre opens in the morning and treats common diseases. It gives free medicines for most day to day problems. Simple blood and other tests are also done free here. A mother can bring her child for a full health check. Old people get their blood pressure and sugar checked with care. The centre keeps a stock of common medicines for the whole month. A vehicle can be called in no time if a patient turns serious. On some days, the centre holds a camp for eye testing in the village. \n\nThe centres give twelve kinds of health services in all. Care for mothers and newborn babies comes first in the list. Care for teeth, eyes and ears is also given here. Patients with long term diseases get regular follow up at the centre. The staff teaches people how to stay fit and healthy. Yoga classes and health talks are held on fixed days. If a patient needs a big hospital, the centre refers him at once. A digital record of each patient is kept at the centre. Doctors in far off hospitals can see this record when needed. A patient can also talk to a specialist on a video call. This saves the poor family money, time and long travel. The centres also keep a watch on the spread of disease. If fever cases rise in a village, the staff reports it fast. Cleanliness drives are run with the help of the village council. In this way, the small centre has become the guard of village health. People now seek treatment early instead of waiting in pain. Early care saves lives and also saves money for the family. Health, in the true sense, has come closer to the home. The staff also visits the homes of women who have just delivered a baby. They check the health of the mother and the newborn together. Children of the area are given their drops and doses at the centre itself. A clean and calm centre earns the trust of the whole village in time."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-03", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Fair price shops and the public distribution system", "text": "In every village and town area, there is a small ration shop. Families buy their monthly grain from this fair price shop. Each family holds a card that shows the names of its members. The card fixes how much grain the family will get each month. Wheat, rice and sugar are sold at very low rates here. For the last few years, the grain has been given fully free. This free grain helps crores of poor families across the country. The shop opens on fixed dates that are known to all. People stand in a line with their cards and bags. The dealer weighs the grain on a digital scale now. An electronic machine records each sale at the same time. The buyer places a thumb on the machine to confirm the sale. A printed slip shows the weight and the date of purchase. This system has ended many old complaints about less weight. The days and hours of the shop are painted on a board outside. Old and disabled persons are helped first as a mark of respect. If the stock is late, the dealer informs the people in advance. \n\nThere are more than five and a half lakh such shops in the country. Almost all of them now use the electronic machine for sales. Every ration card in the country has been made digital. The cards are linked with the identity records of the family. If a family moves to a new town for work, the card still works. The family can lift its grain from any shop in the country. This freedom means a lot to workers who travel for jobs. No poor family now sleeps hungry in a strange new place. The government sends grain to the shops through its own stores. Officers check the stock and quality of grain from time to time. A complaint number is pasted on the wall of each shop. People can call and report any wrong practice by the dealer. Some shops now also sell pulses, oil and other goods. They are slowly turning into small village stores of trust. The ration shop looks like a very ordinary place. Yet it carries the food security of the whole nation. For the poorest family, it is the surest promise of two meals. Trucks bring the grain to the shop before the month begins. The sacks are stored on wooden planks away from damp walls. The dealer keeps the shop clean so that rats and insects stay away. On the last day, left over stock is counted in front of village elders."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-04", "diff": "easy", "topic": "ULLAS adult literacy programme", "text": "Many adults in our villages never went to school as children. Some had to work in the fields from a small age. Others, mostly girls, were kept at home for house work. They grew up unable to read a letter or sign their name. A national programme called ULLAS now teaches such adults. It is made for all persons above the age of fifteen years. Classes are held in schools, halls and even under big trees. The teacher is often a young volunteer from the same village. Students learn to read simple words and write their names. They learn to count money and read bus boards and signs. Lessons also teach them about health, banks and legal rights. A simple book is given to each learner free of cost. The same book is available in twenty seven languages of the country. A phone app helps learners study at home in free time. Many learners are grandmothers who study beside their grandchildren in the evening. Some are workers who attend class after a full day of labour. The volunteer reads each line aloud and the class repeats it together.\n\nCrores of adults have joined these classes in the last few years. Lakhs of volunteers teach them without taking any fee. At the end of the course, a simple test is held. Those who pass get a certificate from a national institute. One hill state has now become fully literate in this manner. Four other states and territories reached this mark before it. The share of adults who can read has crossed eighty per cent. A mother who learns to read can help her children study. A farmer can read the rate list at the market himself. A worker can check his wages and sign with full knowledge. An old woman can read the letters of her son with joy. Reading also protects people from fraud and cheating. No one can make them put a thumb mark on wrong papers. The programme treats learning as a right of every age. It says that it is never too late to open a book. For the learner, the first written word of his name is a victory. Whole villages join in the pride of their new readers. On the test day, the learners sit in rows with pencils in hand. Some hands shake a little, but the eyes stay firm and hopeful. When the results come, the whole class claps for each name read out. A small function is held and sweets are shared among all. The certificate is framed and hung on the wall of the house."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-05", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Ujjwala clean cooking fuel connections", "text": "For years, women in poor homes cooked food on smoky stoves. They burnt wood, dung cakes and dry leaves for fuel. The smoke filled the small kitchen and hurt their eyes. It also harmed their lungs and the health of small children. Collecting wood took hours of walking every other day. A scheme named Ujjwala was started to end this hardship. It gives a cooking gas connection free of cost to poor women. The connection is given in the name of the woman of the house. She does not have to pay the security deposit for the cylinder. The first stove and the first filled cylinder are also supported. The scheme began from a town in the eastern part of the country. Its first target was met ahead of the fixed time. More rounds of the scheme have since covered more families. Even families that migrate for work can take a connection. They only have to give a simple self declaration for it. The woman is taught how to use the stove with full safety. She learns to check the pipe and to close the valve at night. A small support on the price of refills helps the poorest homes. This support goes straight into the bank account of the woman.\n\nToday, more than ten crore such connections have been given. The total number of gas users in homes has grown many times. A gas stove lights in a moment and cooks food fast. There is no smoke, no ash and no smell in the kitchen. The woman saves the hours she spent in collecting wood. She can use that time for work, rest or her children. Children study in a cleaner home in the evening. The health of mothers has improved in lakhs of homes. Refilling the cylinder is now booked on a phone call. The filled cylinder reaches the home or the nearby agency shop. Women feel proud to own a connection in their own name. It is often the first asset recorded in her name. Neighbours who see the blue flame want the same change. In this way, one connection inspires a whole street. Clean fuel has quietly changed the daily life of poor women. It has given them time, health and a new sense of dignity. The delivery man now reaches lanes where no vehicle went earlier. Women gather and share tips on saving gas while cooking food. They cover the vessel and cook on a low flame to save fuel. Even the taste of food is said to be better on a clean flame."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-06", "diff": "easy", "topic": "PM SVANidhi loans for street vendors", "text": "Every street of our towns has vendors who serve us daily. One sells vegetables from a cart in the morning lane. Another sells hot snacks near the bus stand in the evening. A third mends shoes or sells fruit at the crossing. These vendors work hard but have very little money of their own. When their stock ends, they borrow from money lenders at high rates. A scheme called SVANidhi was started to free them from this trap. It gives small loans to vendors without any security. The first loan is of fifteen thousand rupees for working needs. When the vendor repays it on time, a bigger loan follows. The second loan is of twenty five thousand rupees. A third loan of fifty thousand rupees can also be given. The loan comes from a bank and goes into the vendor account. Vendors who repay on time are treated as trusted customers. The town office first prepares a list of vendors in each area. A vendor is given an identity card after a simple check of his cart. Camps are held where bank staff fill the loan forms on the spot. No vendor has to run from office to office for the loan.\n\nMore than one crore such loans have been given so far. They have reached over seventy five lakh street vendors. For most of them, it is their first loan from a bank. The scheme also teaches vendors to take payment by phone. A small board with a code hangs on every cart now. Customers scan the code and pay in a few seconds. Crores of such payments have been made to vendors already. Vendors get small rewards for using digital payment often. They also get a letter that proves their vendor identity. This letter helps them get a fixed place to sell in the town. The scheme has now been extended for a few more years. New groups of vendors will be covered in this period. A vendor with a bank loan stands taller in the market. He can buy better stock and earn more each day. His children can hope for a life beyond the cart. The humble street corner has become a place of new respect. Small loans, given with trust, have changed lakhs of lives. Vendors also meet in groups and share their problems with officers. They speak about carts, licences, water and clean places to stand. Many towns now mark fixed vending zones with sheds and lights. A vendor who once feared the bank now walks into it with pride."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-07", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Common Service Centres in villages", "text": "In most big villages today, there is a small digital service centre. A young person from the village itself runs the centre. He or she is trained to work on computers and the internet. People come here for many kinds of daily government work. They apply for certificates of birth, caste and income here. They update their identity cards and get new cards printed. Forms for exams, jobs and schemes are filled at the centre. The centre also helps people open and use bank accounts. Money can be sent to family members in far off towns. Bills for power, water and phones are paid at the counter. Old people draw their pension through the centre with ease. They no longer travel to the district town for small work. Train and bus tickets are also booked at these centres. Farmers get the papers of their land records printed here. Students print their admit cards and results from the centre. Farmers check the daily rates of crops on the screen of the centre. They also see the weather report before they plan work in the fields. The centre prints land maps that banks ask for during crop loans. In the evening, children gather to learn typing and computer basics.\n\nThere are now more than five lakh such centres in the country. They reach almost nine out of ten villages of the land. The number has grown many times in the last ten years. Each centre earns from small fees fixed for every service. The person who runs it is a small entrepreneur of the village. Women run a good number of these centres with success. The centre also gives digital training to villagers free of cost. Elders learn to use a smart phone step by step. A farmer can talk to a doctor far away on a video call. Legal advice is also given through video in some centres. During the harvest season, crop insurance papers are filled here. The centre keeps a record of every service it gives. Its work is watched by officers through the same network. For the villager, the centre is the government at walking distance. It has saved time, money and many journeys for crores of people. A single computer in a small room has changed village life deeply. During elections, the centre helps voters check their names in the list. At the time of festivals, it books tickets for workers coming home. The operator keeps the machines clean and updates them every week. A good centre is known by the queue of happy faces outside it."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-08", "diff": "easy", "topic": "Eklavya Model Residential Schools", "text": "In the tribal areas of our country, schools were once far and few. A child had to walk many miles to reach a middle school. Many children, mostly girls, left studies after the primary stage. A chain of residential schools named Eklavya is changing this picture. These schools are made only for tribal boys and girls. A child can study here from class six to class twelve. The school gives free food, free stay and free books to all. Uniforms, shoes and other needs are also given free of cost. Each school is built on a large campus of many acres. About four hundred and eighty children study in one school. There are smart classes, science labs and a big library. A playground and rooms for music and art are also provided. Teachers live on the campus and guide the children at all hours. The pattern of studies follows the central board of the country. The day begins early with exercise on the ground for all children. After classes, children play games like football, hockey and archery. Local tribal games and dances are also taught with full respect. A doctor visits the campus each week to check the health of children.\n\nMore than seven hundred such schools have been approved so far. Over five hundred of them are already open and running. They are set up in blocks where tribal people live in large numbers. A special national body runs these schools for the government. New buildings are coming up fast in remote forest districts. The cost of building one campus runs into many crores. The government also pays a fixed yearly amount for each child. Children learn computers and spoken communication with special care. Sports teams of these schools win prizes at state level meets. Many old students have joined colleges and good jobs. Some have become teachers, nurses and officers in service. Parents who never saw a school now dream big for their children. Girls of these schools speak with confidence before any crowd. The school hostel becomes a second home for the child. Festivals of the tribe are celebrated on the campus with pride. In this way, the schools respect the culture of the children. They open the doors of the modern world at the same time. On the annual day, children present science models and tribal art together. Old students return to the campus and guide the younger ones. They tell them about college life, hostels and entrance tests. Such visits fill the children with hope and clear goals. The school thus grows stronger with each passing batch of students."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-09", "diff": "medium", "topic": "PM-KISAN income support to farmers", "text": "The Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi, popularly called PM-KISAN, transfers income support directly to farmer families. Under the scheme, every eligible family receives Rs 6,000 in a year. The amount is paid in three equal instalments of Rs 2,000 each. The money moves by Direct Benefit Transfer into Aadhaar-seeded bank accounts, without any intermediary. The scheme was launched in February 2019 at Gorakhpur. Its design deliberately avoided the machinery of state-level distribution that had slowed earlier subsidies. A farmer can check the status of an instalment on the scheme portal or a mobile application. Registration is generally completed through common service centres or the portal itself. Corrections of name and account details follow the records of the land revenue department.\n\nThe scale of the programme becomes clear from the instalment released most recently. The twenty-fourth instalment carried more than eighteen thousand crore rupees to over nine crore farmer families. Cumulative transfers under the scheme have crossed Rs 4.64 lakh crore since its launch. The Cabinet has approved the continuation of the scheme into the next decade with a large outlay. Eligibility rests upon landholding records, and certain categories of taxpayers and institutional holders remain excluded. Periodic electronic verification of identity, called eKYC, has been made compulsory for continued payment. This verification is conducted through biometric authentication, one-time passwords or face authentication on a mobile phone. States are responsible for preparing and correcting the lists of beneficiaries in their records. Village-level saturation drives have attempted to enrol eligible families who remained outside the net. Grievances are registered through a helpdesk and through the public grievance portal of the government. Critics observe that the amount is modest against the rising cost of seed and fertiliser. Supporters answer that a predictable, unconditional transfer before each sowing season has its own value. For many small holders, the instalment arrives exactly when the field must be prepared.\n\nIn the village, the payment dates are now part of the farming calendar. Farmers speak of the instalment along with the rains and the sowing season. The money is small, but it comes without a visit to any office. A farmer can draw it from the bank, the post office or a banking agent. Many families use the amount to buy seed and fertiliser at the right time. Others keep a part of it for school fees or for a visit to the doctor. Women hold accounts in their own names in lakhs of families. The direct payment has also cut the role of middlemen in farm support."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-10", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Mission Karmayogi civil services training", "text": "Mission Karmayogi is the government's programme for rebuilding the training of its own employees. Approved in September 2020, it is formally the National Programme for Civil Services Capacity Building. Its stated object is to move personnel management from a rule-based system to a role-based system. Under the older arrangement, an officer's training depended mainly upon the service and the batch. The mission instead begins by defining the competencies that each government role actually requires. Training is then matched to those competencies rather than to seniority or habit. The Capacity Building Commission was created to set standards and harmonise the efforts of training institutions. A special purpose vehicle, Karmayogi Bharat, operates the digital platform of the mission. Tripartite agreements have been signed with twenty-seven states and union territories for joint implementation.\n\nThe platform of the mission is called iGOT Karmayogi, and its growth has been rapid. It reports more than 1.41 crore registered users drawn from ministries, states and public institutions. The catalogue carries over 3,700 courses covering administration, technology, finance and behavioural skills. Course completions on the platform have crossed six crore in aggregate. Courses are offered in twenty-three languages, with the largest collections in Hindi and other regional languages. Every government employee is expected to prepare an individual capacity building plan each year. Departments link these plans to the annual goals that their sections must achieve. Complex subjects are broken into short modules that an officer can complete between files. Assessments and feedback scores are recorded against the learner's profile on the platform. The mission also attempts to change the culture symbolised by the word karmayogi itself. The official is addressed as a servant of outcomes rather than a custodian of procedure. Whether this philosophical shift survives contact with office routine remains an open question. The infrastructure of continuous learning, in any case, has been built at national scale.\n\nThe change is most visible in the training institutes of the states. Academies that once ran a few courses a year now run courses through the year. Young officers take short online modules before they join a new post. Senior officers are also expected to learn, and many do so with care. Courses on data, on law and on public dealing draw the largest numbers. Trainers say the level of class talks has risen since the platform began. A clerk in a district office can now learn from the same teacher as a secretary. This shared base of learning is the quiet gain of the mission."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-11", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Project Cheetah reintroduction", "text": "The cheetah was declared extinct in India in 1952, the last animals having been hunted in the central Indian forests. The project, launched in September 2022, is the attempt to restore the species to the grasslands of the country. It is described by its planners as the world's first intercontinental translocation of a large carnivore. The founding animals arrived in two principal consignments from southern Africa. Eight cheetahs came from Namibia in the first transfer that September. Twelve more arrived from South Africa in February of the following year. A third group of nine animals reached the country from Botswana in February this year. All the animals were released into a national park at Kuno, an area of about 748 square kilometres. Kuno was selected for its grassland habitat, prey base and relative distance from dense human settlement.\n\nThe progress of the project is measured most concretely in the births on Indian soil. The first litter was born the following March to a female named Jwala. A female named Mukhi became the first cheetah born in India to survive to adulthood in the wild population. With five cubs born in a recent litter, the total population reached 53 in March this year. Of these, thirty-three animals are cubs born in India across ten successful litters. Three cheetahs have been moved to a second sanctuary as the next home of the project. The long-term plan imagines a connected population spread across a large landscape of two states. The project has also recorded deaths from infection, conflict and natural causes, which its managers report publicly. Radio collars track the movement of free-ranging animals across the park boundaries. Local communities around Kuno have been engaged as cheetah friends to reduce conflict with livestock owners. The experiment remains scientifically contested, but its demographic direction is no longer in doubt.\n\nFor the villages around the park, the cheetah has become a part of daily talk. School children draw the animal in their art classes and name the cubs. Guides and drivers from nearby towns earn from the tourists who now visit. The forest staff patrol the grasslands on foot through the heat of the day. They note the signs of prey, water and danger in a daily register. When a cheetah moves out of the park, the staff follow its track with care. This close watch is what keeps both the animals and the people safe."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-12", "diff": "medium", "topic": "e-NAM digital agriculture market", "text": "The National Agriculture Market, known as e-NAM, is a digital platform that links the physical mandis of India. It was launched in April 2016 with the modest ambition of giving the farmer a wider market. In the traditional arrangement, a farmer sold produce in the nearest mandi to a small circle of traders. Prices differed sharply between mandis, and the farmer rarely knew the rate prevailing elsewhere. The platform addresses this failure by placing the produce of many mandis before a common pool of buyers. A trader registered in another state can bid for a lot after its quality is tested and recorded. The farmer remains free to reject the highest bid and sell locally in the usual manner. Assaying laboratories in the mandis grade the produce so that distant buyers can trust the description.\n\nA decade of expansion has carried the platform to 1,656 mandis across twenty-three states and four union territories. Nearly two crore farmers have registered on it, along with lakhs of traders and thousands of farmer producer organisations. Cumulative trade through the platform has reached thirteen crore metric tonnes, valued at nearly five lakh crore rupees. Price information for 247 commodities is published on a mobile application in near real time. The central government gives grant assistance of up to Rs 75 lakh to each mandi for infrastructure and software. Electronic weighing scales and assaying equipment are the standard purchases under this assistance. Farmer producer organisations are encouraged to aggregate the produce of their members for online sale. Aggregation gives small holders the lot size that distant buyers prefer to handle. The platform also supports logistics modules that connect transporters with successful bidders. Adoption remains uneven, and much trade still settles outside the digital auction. Even so, the posted prices of e-NAM now discipline negotiations in the physical yard itself.\n\nA farmer who visits the mandi today carries a phone in his pocket. He checks the rates at three or four mandis before he loads his cart. If the home mandi offers a poor rate, he can wait or sell elsewhere. This simple power to compare has changed the tone of the yard. Traders now bid with more care, since the farmer knows the outside price. Young farmers help their fathers read the rates on the app. The habit of checking prices is spreading to goods beyond the farm. In this sense, the platform has taught the village a new way to trade."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-13", "diff": "medium", "topic": "PM Vishwakarma artisan support", "text": "PM Vishwakarma is a central sector scheme addressed to the traditional artisan and craftsperson. It was launched on the occasion of Vishwakarma Jayanti with an outlay of Rs 13,000 crore spread over five years. The scheme recognises 18 traditional trades in its first phase of implementation. The list includes carpenters, blacksmiths, goldsmiths, potters, cobblers, tailors, masons and makers of fishing nets, among others. Its architecture is described through three words: Samman, Samarthya and Samriddhi, meaning respect, capability and prosperity. Every enrolled artisan receives a PM Vishwakarma certificate and an identity card. The certificate is intended to give the beneficiary a formal identity that banks and buyers can recognise. Registration is free and is carried out through common service centres and the scheme portal.\n\nThe capability component begins with basic training of five to seven days. During training, the artisan receives a stipend of Rs 500 for each day of attendance. Advanced training is offered to those who wish to upgrade their skills further. A toolkit incentive of up to Rs 15,000 is provided through e-vouchers for modern tools. The prosperity component rests upon collateral-free enterprise loans at a concessional rate of interest. The first tranche of one lakh rupees is repayable over eighteen months, and a second tranche of two lakh rupees follows successful repayment. Interest subvention keeps the effective cost to the borrower at five per cent per annum. By the middle of September this year, basic training had been completed by more than twenty-four lakh beneficiaries. Toolkits have been delivered to over eighteen lakh artisans, and loans worth more than five thousand crore rupees have been sanctioned. An independent evaluation reported that most surveyed beneficiaries recorded a moderate to significant increase in income. The scheme attempts to hold together a social order of hereditary skill that markets alone were steadily dissolving.\n\nThe change can be seen in the workshop of a village potter. He now owns an electric wheel bought with the help of the toolkit amount. His son, who once planned to leave for the city, works beside him. Orders come through a phone app from buyers in nearby towns. The family income has risen, and so has its standing in the village. Such stories repeat in the lanes of barbers, carpenters and weavers. A trade that was seen as a burden is being seen as a base. That shift in respect may prove to be the deepest gain of all."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-14", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Vibrant Villages Programme for border villages", "text": "The Vibrant Villages Programme reverses the usual description of settlements along the northern border. These villages are officially treated not as the last villages of the country but as its first villages. The first phase of the programme was approved in February 2023 with an outlay of forty-eight hundred crore rupees. It covers 662 villages in forty-six blocks of nineteen districts, spread over four states and one union territory. The programme was launched in April of the same year from Kibithoo in Arunachal Pradesh, among the easternmost inhabited places in India. Each selected village prepares a Village Action Plan through its gram panchayat. The plans converge the schemes of different departments upon the specific deficits of the village. Road connectivity, telecom coverage, electrification, drinking water and livelihood creation form the standard agenda. Tourism is promoted deliberately, so that the border village earns from the visitors it earlier merely watched.\n\nThe second phase of the programme was approved two years later, in April, with an outlay of Rs 6,839 crore. It extends coverage to 1,954 additional villages in fifteen states and two union territories along land borders. This phase was launched in February this year from Nathanpur in Assam. Together, the two phases reach more than twenty-six hundred villages along the frontier. Saturation of household-level facilities is the stated standard for the second phase. Smart classes in schools, telemedicine links and banking access are among the services being converged. Homestays and local festivals are marketed to draw domestic tourists towards the border districts. The strategic reasoning is stated openly in the programme documents. Populated and prosperous border villages anchor the nation's presence in difficult terrain. Out-migration from these villages, long treated as natural, is identified as the central problem to reverse. The programme therefore measures itself not by works completed but by families persuaded to stay.\n\nLife in these villages is shaped by snow, slope and distance. A new road cuts the time to the nearest town from a day to hours. A mobile tower brings the voice of a son in the city into the kitchen. Tourists who stay in village homes leave money with the family itself. Young men train as guides, drivers and cooks for the visitors. Women sell woollen goods, pickles and dried fruit at small stalls. Each such earning is one more reason for a family to stay on. The border village, long seen as an end, is being recast as a beginning."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-15", "diff": "medium", "topic": "National Clean Air Programme", "text": "The National Clean Air Programme was launched in January 2019 as the first national framework for air quality management. It presently covers 130 cities that failed to meet the national air quality standards over consecutive years. Each covered city prepares its own action plan based upon an apportionment of its pollution sources. The plans address road dust, vehicle emissions, construction activity, industrial sources and the burning of waste and biomass. The programme originally targeted a reduction of twenty to thirty per cent in coarse particulate matter within five years. The target was subsequently revised to a reduction of up to 40 per cent, or attainment of the national standard of 60 micrograms per cubic metre, by last year. Performance-linked grants totalling more than sixteen thousand crore rupees have been released to the cities. The grants are channelled through the urban local bodies responsible for implementation.\n\nProgress reporting for the latest year presents a mixed but improving picture across the network. Of the covered cities, more than a hundred recorded a reduction in particulate levels against the base year. Seventy-two cities achieved a reduction exceeding twenty per cent, and twenty-six cities crossed forty per cent. Fourteen cities met the national ambient standard, against only six cities in the base year. The Central Pollution Control Board publishes the data of all covered cities on a public dashboard. Within the programme cities, more than eleven thousand industrial units have shifted to cleaner fuels. City governments have mechanised road sweeping, created green buffers and begun remediating legacy waste dumpsites. Air quality monitoring stations have multiplied, and their readings are published for public scrutiny. Critics point out that winter episodes in the northern plains still breach every standard by wide margins. They also note that several plans depend upon actions by agencies the city government does not control. The programme's defenders answer that a permanent institutional structure now exists where episodic panic once stood. The direction of the data, rather than any single season, is offered as the fair test.\n\nFor the citizen, the change is felt in small daily choices. Schools in the worst-hit cities now plan outdoor sports by the air quality index. Hospitals report the load of breathing cases on poor air days. City buses and metro trains gain riders when the air turns bad. Builders are asked to cover sites and spray water on dusty roads. Each of these steps is minor on its own. Taken together across a hundred cities, they form a new civic habit. Clean air is slowly becoming a claim that citizens make as a right."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-16", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Geographical Indication tags", "text": "A Geographical Indication, or GI, is a sign used on products that belong to a specific place. The qualities, reputation or characteristics of the product must be essentially attributable to that origin. The legal framework is the GI Act of 1999. The GI Registry, which examines and registers applications, is headquartered in the south. Darjeeling Tea holds the distinction of being the first product to receive the tag in India. The register has since grown to more than 800 products across the states and union territories. A large majority of the registrations, over six hundred, have been granted since the year 2014. Handicrafts form the largest category, followed by agricultural goods, foodstuffs and manufactured products. Uttar Pradesh leads the state table with eighty-one registrations, followed by Tamil Nadu and Maharashtra.\n\nRegistration is only the first step in converting a reputation into an income. The producers of a registered product may enrol as authorised users of the indication. The number of authorised users has risen from a few hundred to nearly twenty-nine thousand in recent years. Authorised users can act against traders who sell imitations under the protected name. State governments have begun promoting GI products through exhibitions, e-commerce and airport retail. The government has announced a target of ten thousand GI registrations by the end of this decade. Examination capacity at the Registry is being expanded to handle the expected volume of applications. Awareness camps teach producer groups how to document the history and method of their product. Documentation of traditional knowledge is itself a valued outcome of the application process. Critics warn that a tag without marketing and quality control changes little for the producer. Exporters answer that origin branding is precisely how agricultural premiums are built in world markets. The register, read as a whole, amounts to a map of India's regional specialisations.\n\nBehind each entry on the register stands a group of ordinary makers. A weave may carry the work of five hundred looms in one small town. A spice may come from the farms of a single valley. The tag tells the buyer that the goods are true to that place. It also tells the young maker that the family craft has a name. Many producer groups now print the tag on their packs and labels. Buyers in far cities pay a better price for goods they can trust. The register thus joins the pride of a place with the purse of its people."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-17", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Direct Benefit Transfer and digital public infrastructure", "text": "The architecture that the Government of India describes as digital public infrastructure rests upon three foundations that administrators throughout the country now treat as a single system. The Jan Dhan programme carried bank accounts to households that the banking system had never reached in earlier decades of planning. The Aadhaar programme gave residents a verifiable identity that departments could trust without demanding repeated documentation from citizens. Mobile connectivity joined the account and the identity, allowing the State to transfer money, information and entitlements directly to the intended beneficiary.\n\nDirect Benefit Transfer is the application through which this architecture touches welfare administration most visibly throughout the states. Cumulative transfers through the mechanism exceeded Rs 49.09 lakh crore by January 2026, spread across hundreds of schemes administered by the Centre and the states together. Aadhaar numbers generated in the country have crossed 144 crore, while Jan Dhan accounts have grown from 14.72 crore in 2015 to 57.71 crore by March 2026. Deposits within those accounts have risen from Rs 15,670 crore to Rs 2.94 lakh crore over the same period of expansion. The Unified Payments Interface processed 21.70 billion transactions worth Rs 28.33 lakh crore during January 2026 alone, with 691 banks live upon the platform.\n\nThe government presents these figures as evidence that leakage, duplication and delay have been engineered out of welfare delivery. The DBT Mission portal publishes estimated gains of Rs 5,14,201.92 crore up to March 2025 from the deletion of duplicate and non-existent beneficiaries across programmes. The largest single component of that estimate arises from the public distribution system, where 6.36 crore duplicate ration cards stand deleted. Such figures are government estimates rather than independently audited savings, and economists throughout the universities continue to debate their precise magnitude. The direction of the underlying change, involving verifiable identity and direct payment rails, is nevertheless accepted across the policy spectrum.\n\nThe Indian model has acquired an international constituency that earlier technology exports from the country rarely commanded. The UPI system operates in 8 countries, and India has signed digital public infrastructure cooperation agreements with 24 countries across Asia, Africa and Latin America. Delegations from the World Bank, the International Monetary Fund and the United Nations have studied the stack as a template for population-scale governance. The deeper significance lies in the demonstration that public platforms, built as open infrastructure, can carry private innovation at national scale. Whether this architecture preserves competition and privacy under pressure will determine its standing in the longer history of Indian statecraft."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-18", "diff": "hard", "topic": "PM-JANMAN for Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups", "text": "The Pradhan Mantri Janjati Adivasi Nyaya Maha Abhiyan, known throughout the administration as PM-JANMAN, was launched on 15 November 2023, the day observed nationally as Janjatiya Gaurav Divas. The mission addresses the condition of 75 Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups distributed across 18 States and one Union Territory of the country. These communities, identified by stagnant population, low literacy and pre-agricultural technology, had remained partially outside the coverage of general development programmes for decades. The mission therefore adopts a saturation approach under which every household of the target communities is to receive a defined bundle of basic facilities.\n\nThe approved outlay of Rs 24,104 crore combines a Central share of Rs 15,336 crore with a State share of Rs 8,768 crore over three years. Eleven interventions are delivered through nine line ministries whose programmes ordinarily operate upon separate administrative tracks throughout the districts. The bundle includes safe housing, clean drinking water, road and telecom connectivity, education, health and nutrition, electrification of un-electrified households and sustainable livelihood support. Implementation proceeds habitation by habitation, with household surveys conducted through a dedicated mobile application by district administrations. The design consciously reverses the demand-based model in which remote communities were expected to apply for facilities they scarcely knew existed.\n\nProgress reported up to 30 June 2026 records 4.76 lakh houses sanctioned for the target communities, of which 2.61 lakh stand completed and occupied. Multipurpose centres, student hostels and mobile medical units form the supporting infrastructure that ministries have converged upon the same habitations. The Ministry of Tribal Affairs coordinates the mission, while the NITI Aayog and the Cabinet Secretariat review saturation figures with the states at regular intervals. Critics within the development literature observe that housing completion, though measurable, is the easiest component of the bundle to deliver. Livelihood interventions and quality schooling demand sustained engagement that construction targets cannot by themselves guarantee.\n\nThe mission nevertheless marks a doctrinal shift in the treatment of the most marginalised citizens within Indian welfare administration. Earlier frameworks measured success by expenditure and coverage averaged across districts, a method under which the smallest communities disappeared statistically. PM-JANMAN instead names its beneficiary communities exhaustively and accepts accountability habitation by habitation and household by household. Its three-year window will test whether administrative intensity of this order can be converted into permanent service delivery. The communities themselves, long accustomed to being the last recipients of every programme, are the constituency best placed to judge the experiment."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-19", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Defence exports and indigenous production", "text": "Defence exports from India reached Rs 38,424 crore during the financial year 2025-26, the highest figure since the sector began reporting statistics. The increase of Rs 14,802 crore over the preceding year represents growth of 62.66 per cent within a single twelve-month period. Defence public sector undertakings contributed 54.84 per cent of the total value, while the private sector accounted for the remaining 45.16 per cent. The number of exporting firms and public units rose to 145, and Indian equipment was shipped to more than 80 countries across several continents. For a country whose defence exports stood at Rs 686 crore in 2013-14, the trajectory represents a structural change rather than a statistical fluctuation.\n\nThe export expansion accompanies a parallel record in indigenous production, which reached Rs 1.78 lakh crore during the same financial year. The Department of Defence Production attributes the movement to indigenisation lists that progressively bar the import of specified equipment. Items upon these positive indigenisation lists must be procured from Indian sources after notified dates, creating assured demand for domestic manufacturers. Production ecosystems have consequently deepened throughout the defence corridors of Uttar Pradesh and Tamil Nadu, where anchor investments draw component suppliers into clusters. The government has set targets of Rs 3 lakh crore in defence production and Rs 50,000 crore in exports by the year 2029.\n\nThe composition of exports has shifted from components and spares towards complete platforms and systems of greater technological content. Ammunition, armoured vehicles, patrol vessels, radar systems and artillery guns manufactured by firms such as Bharat Forge, Larsen and Toubro and the public sector Bharat Electronics now figure in overseas orders. The BrahMos missile system, developed jointly with Russia, supplied the most visible demonstration of Indian equipment entering foreign service. Export licensing procedures have been digitised through the Directorate General of Foreign Trade, reducing the clearance time that exporters earlier protested against. Defence attaches posted in Indian missions abroad have been assigned explicit targets for promoting Indian equipment in their host countries.\n\nSustaining this trajectory will require the industry to clear quality, financing and after-sales thresholds that established exporters manage routinely. Foreign buyers evaluate not merely the platform but the spare-parts pipeline and training support that accompany it throughout its service life. Indian exporters must also navigate the export-control expectations of partners whose components are embedded within Indian systems. The strategic return, however, extends beyond commerce into the partnerships that equipment relationships create between governments. A country that buys Indian patrol vessels or artillery enters a decades-long maintenance relationship with Indian industry. The export figures of 2025-26 therefore register simultaneously in the ledgers of trade and of foreign policy."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-20", "diff": "hard", "topic": "National Education Policy 2020 restructuring", "text": "The National Education Policy 2020, approved by the Union Cabinet in July 2020, replaced a policy framework that had governed Indian education since 1986. Its most visible structural intervention abolished the 10+2 arrangement and substituted a 5+3+3+4 design aligned to the developmental stages of the child. The Foundational Stage joins three years of Anganwadi or pre-school provision with Grades 1 and 2 for children between three and eight years. Preparatory, Middle and Secondary stages follow for the age bands of 8 to 11, 11 to 14 and 14 to 18 years respectively. The redesign formally brings early childhood care and education, historically administered as nutrition programming, within the educational framework of the State.\n\nThe policy directs that the medium of instruction until at least Grade 5, and preferably until Grade 8, shall be the home language, mother tongue or regional language of the learner. It abolishes the rigid separations between arts and sciences, between curricular and extra-curricular activity, and between vocational and academic streams that structured the older system. Board examinations are to be redesigned as modular assessments offered twice annually, testing core concepts rather than accumulated memorisation. A national assessment centre, PARAKH, has been established to set standards for student evaluation across boards. The policy targets a Gross Enrolment Ratio of 100 per cent in school education by 2030 and 50 per cent in higher education by 2035.\n\nImplementation responsibilities rest predominantly with the State governments, since education occupies the Concurrent List of the Constitution of India. The Department of School Education and Literacy has pursued adoption through the NIPUN Bharat mission for foundational literacy and numeracy in the early grades. Approximately 2 crore children who are presently out of school are to be brought back into the system through open schooling and bridge provisions. Teacher education is to be restructured into an integrated four-year degree located within multidisciplinary institutions. The policy expects public investment in education to approach 6 per cent of gross domestic product.\n\nThe debate surrounding the policy concentrates upon capacity rather than upon its stated objectives, which command wide assent. Foundational learning at the prescribed standard requires trained early-childhood educators in numbers that the Anganwadi system does not presently possess. Language provisions raise practical questions in classrooms where migrant children carry several home languages into a single section. Universities have moved unevenly towards the multiple entry and exit structures that the policy prescribes for undergraduate programmes. The framework will consequently be judged across a generation of classrooms rather than within any single plan period. Its authors intended such a horizon when they described the document as a policy for the decades ahead."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-21", "diff": "hard", "topic": "DPDP Act and the 2025 Rules", "text": "The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, enacted by Parliament on 11 August 2023, created the first comprehensive statutory framework for personal data in India. The Act remained substantially inoperative until the Digital Personal Data Protection Rules were notified on 14 November 2025 after public consultation. The Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology describes the framework through the acronym SARAL, denoting rules that are Simple, Accessible, Rational and Actionable. Seven principles organise the legislation, including consent and transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation, accuracy, storage limitation, security safeguards and accountability. The consultation history was extensive, drawing 22,600 comments upon the earlier Bill and 6,915 inputs upon the draft Rules.\n\nThe operative vocabulary of the regime designates the individual as the Data Principal and the processing entity as the Data Fiduciary. A fiduciary may process personal data only for a lawful purpose to which the principal has given consent, or for certain legitimate uses that the Act enumerates. Data Principals hold enforceable rights to access their information, to seek correction and erasure, and to nominate another individual to exercise rights in the event of death or incapacity. Children receive heightened protection, with verifiable parental consent required and tracking, behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at them prohibited outright. Significant Data Fiduciaries, notified on the basis of data volume and risk, attract additional obligations including audits and impact assessments.\n\nEnforcement rests with the Data Protection Board of India, a body of four members that functions entirely through digital proceedings. Penalties are calibrated to the gravity of breach, rising to Rs 250 crore for failure to maintain reasonable security safeguards against a data breach. The compliance framework is phased across an eighteen-month window, allowing fiduciaries to rebuild consent architecture, notice systems and grievance mechanisms in sequence. Exemptions for the State on grounds of sovereignty and public order remain the most contested provisions of the legislation among civil liberties scholars. Startups and specified research processing receive relaxations intended to protect innovation from disproportionate compliance burdens.\n\nThe framework enters force within an economy where platform services have accumulated personal data for two decades without a governing statute. Its practical meaning will be determined by the jurisprudence the Board develops and by the compliance culture that large fiduciaries establish throughout their vendor chains. Citizens encounter the regime through consent notices whose quality will decide whether consent operates as a genuine choice or as a ritual click. The government presents the Act as the fourth pillar of the digital architecture that already includes identity, payments and document exchange. Whether privacy becomes the load-bearing pillar or the decorative one is the question the next decade will answer."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-22", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Ethanol blending programme reaches E20", "text": "Petrol sold throughout India now carries an ethanol blend of 20 per cent, a target that the government advanced from 2030 and achieved during 2025-26. The blending proportion stood below 1.5 per cent in 2013-14, when ethanol procurement served mainly the liquor and chemical industries. The progression through approximately 12 per cent in 2022-23 and 19.2 per cent in 2024-25 required simultaneous expansion of feedstock, distillery capacity and oil-company logistics. Production capacity for ethanol has grown from 421 crore litres in 2014 to approximately 2,000 crore litres across the country. The programme rests within the National Policy on Biofuels, administered by the Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas in coordination with the Department of Food and Public Distribution.\n\nThe official accounting of benefits aggregates savings and transfers across the decade since the Ethanol Supply Year 2014-15. The Ministry reports foreign exchange savings exceeding Rs 1.97 lakh crore through the substitution of approximately 316 lakh metric tonnes of crude oil imports. Avoided carbon dioxide emissions are estimated at 952 lakh metric tonnes, while payments transferred to farmers for feedstock exceed Rs 1.66 lakh crore over the period. Sugarcane supplied the original feedstock base, supplemented by surplus rice, damaged foodgrains and maize cultivated across the eastern states. Interest subvention schemes supported distillery expansion in grain-surplus regions where sugarcane cultivation is hydrologically unsuitable. The pricing structure differentiates ethanol by feedstock, paying a premium for grain-based production over the sugarcane rates.\n\nThe programme has attracted scrutiny from economists and environmental researchers across the universities and policy institutes. Sugarcane expansion in water-stressed districts of Maharashtra and Karnataka competes directly with drinking water and food cropping for scarce groundwater reserves. Food-versus-fuel objections intensified when rice stocks held by the Food Corporation of India were diverted towards distilleries during a year of cereal inflation. Vehicle manufacturers have certified compatibility for the E20 blend, though owners of older vehicles report mileage losses that official testing places within modest limits. The Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas has stated that no decision has been taken to mandate blending beyond the present 20 per cent level.\n\nThe strategic logic of the programme is located in the import bill rather than in the fuel tank. India imports the overwhelming majority of its crude oil, and every percentage point of blending displaces tanker cargoes with domestic agricultural produce. The programme thereby converts an energy-security expenditure into rural income across the cane and maize belts. Its second decade will be governed by feedstock diversification towards second-generation ethanol from crop residue and bamboo. Whether the water economics of the first decade can be corrected by that transition remains the unresolved question of the policy."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-23", "diff": "hard", "topic": "Sagarmala port-led development programme", "text": "The Sagarmala programme, administered by the Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways, organises the maritime development of India around a coastline exceeding 7,500 kilometres. Its premise holds that port capacity, rather than shipping demand, has constrained the trade competitiveness of Indian industry. The programme identifies 839 projects with an estimated investment of Rs 5.8 lakh crore for implementation in phases until 2035. Of these, 315 projects stand completed, while the remainder advance through construction and appraisal across the major and non-major ports. Five pillars structure the intervention: port modernisation, port connectivity enhancement, port-led industrialisation, coastal community development, and coastal shipping with inland water transport.\n\nThe cargo statistics trace the cumulative effect of dredging, mechanisation and connectivity works upon the maritime system. Coastal shipping cargo has expanded from 87 million tonnes per annum to 195 million tonnes per annum since the programme commenced operations. Inland waterway cargo has grown from 18.10 million tonnes to 145.50 million tonnes across the same period of sustained investment. Fourteen coastal economic zones were conceptualised to locate manufacturing within economical distance of port infrastructure along the maritime states. The programme estimates an employment potential of one crore positions, divided between 40 lakh direct and 60 lakh indirect opportunities across the logistics chain. More than 60,000 candidates have been trained under skill initiatives supported through dedicated centres.\n\nConnectivity projects carry the largest share of investment because Indian ports historically suffered from congested evacuation corridors behind the berths. Dedicated rail sidings, four-lane road links and pipeline networks are built to move cargo from quay to hinterland without transhipment delays. The Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways coordinates these works with Indian Railways, the National Highways Authority of India and the state maritime boards of the coastal states. Port community development funds finance fisheries infrastructure, skill centres and passenger jetties in the settlements adjoining the project sites, while training centres run by the programme serve the youth of the same settlements. Environmental appraisal of dredging and reclamation remains a recurring point of litigation between developers and coastal communities.\n\nThe programme's horizon of 2035 places its completion beyond several electoral cycles, a duration its planners defend as inherent to maritime infrastructure. Ports compete regionally with Colombo, Singapore and Jebel Ali for transhipment traffic that Indian cargo presently routes through foreign hubs. The Sagarmala framework attempts to recover that traffic through deeper drafts, faster turnaround and industrial cargo anchored at Indian ports themselves. Its success will be measured less by project completion counts than by the share of Indian trade that Indian ports handle directly. In that measure resides the difference between a construction programme and a maritime strategy."}, {"tid": "hcm-b2-24", "diff": "hard", "topic": "National One Health Mission", "text": "The National One Health Mission institutionalises a proposition that epidemiologists have pressed upon governments for decades: human health cannot be secured independently of animal health and environmental condition. The Union Cabinet approved the mission in February 2024 upon the recommendation of the 21st Prime Minister's Science, Technology and Innovation Advisory Council. Sixteen Central and State ministries and departments participate formally, spanning human health, animal husbandry, environment, agriculture, pharmaceuticals, defence, earth sciences, space sciences and disaster management. The mission is steered by the Office of the Principal Scientific Adviser and implemented principally through the Department of Health Research and the Indian Council of Medical Research. Its second assembly convened at Bharat Mandapam in November 2025, reviewing integrated surveillance pilots across the states.\n\nThe operational core of the mission is surveillance that crosses previously separate reporting systems. Integrated monitoring now extends across slaughterhouses, bird sanctuaries, zoos, live-animal markets and wastewater systems within selected districts. A network of 23 laboratories of BSL-3 and BSL-4 standard provides the diagnostic depth that outbreak investigation requires. The National Institute of One Health at Nagpur is the nodal institution for training, reference diagnostics and research coordination. A National Joint Outbreak Response Team, drawing specialists from the ministries, has been constituted to deploy when signals cross sectoral thresholds. Exercises simulating avian influenza and nipah-like events have tested these protocols across several states.\n\nThe scientific rationale draws force from the origin pattern of recent epidemics. The majority of emerging infectious diseases in humans are zoonotic, spilling over from animal reservoirs whose ecology is disturbed by land-use change and wildlife trade. Antimicrobial resistance travels between farms, hospitals and water bodies along pathways that single-sector surveillance cannot observe. Climate change is redrawing the distribution of vector species across the Himalayan foothills and the coastal districts of the peninsula. A mission architecture that assigns these connected phenomena to disconnected departments reproduces the blindness that the One Health framework exists to correct.\n\nThe institutional challenge is commensurate with the scientific one, since ministries guard budgets, data and credit with practised vigilance. The mission attempts to govern through shared platforms and joint protocols rather than through a merged department that Parliament has not created. State governments carry the operational burden because veterinary services, public health laboratories and pollution boards are administered at that level. Success will appear in unremarkable statistics: outbreaks detected in animals before human cases accumulate, and investigations that open with all relevant data already joined. Against the cost of a single pandemic, the mission's proponents note, such insurance is inexpensive. The argument has prevailed in the Cabinet once; its durability across budget cycles will decide the matter."}, {"tid": "hcm-d-01", "diff": "medium", "topic": "How vaccines protect a community", "text": "A vaccine is a preparation that teaches the body to defend itself against a particular disease. It usually contains a weakened or harmless part of the germ that causes the disease. Once it enters the body, the immune system learns to recognise that germ. If the real germ arrives later, the body already knows how to fight it. The illness is then prevented, or it becomes much milder than it would have been. The first vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner, an English doctor, in 1796. He used material from cowpox to protect people against smallpox, a deadly disease of that time. His work showed that protection could be given before the disease struck. The idea soon spread throughout medical practice across the world, and vaccination became a routine part of healthcare in most countries.\nVaccination protects more than the person who receives the injection. When most people in a community are protected, the germ finds few people to infect. Even those who cannot be vaccinated are shielded by the protection around them. This group includes very young babies and people with weakened immune systems. Health workers call this community protection, and it depends on high coverage throughout the population. A large share of the population must be vaccinated for the shield to hold. Without that coverage, the germ can spread quickly through towns and villages. India offers a strong example of what such coverage can achieve. The last case of wild polio was recorded in 2011, after years of nationwide drives. In 2014, the World Health Organisation declared the country free of the disease. During the drives, millions of children received drops at booths, in schools and at their homes.\nBehind every vaccination drive stands a chain of careful work. Vaccines must be kept cold from the factory to the clinic. Health workers carry them in insulated boxes on long journeys to remote villages. In India, the Universal Immunisation Programme provides free vaccines against a dozen diseases. Nurses record each dose on a card, so that no child misses the next one. The card also helps doctors plan the next visit for each child. Parents are asked to bring their children on the dates written on the card. Follow-up visits make sure that the full course is completed on time. When families, health workers and the government act together, the system works well. A simple injection then becomes one of the cheapest and most powerful tools for keeping a whole community healthy, and the experience of one generation strengthens the protection of the next."}, {"tid": "hcm-d-02", "diff": "medium", "topic": "Volcanoes and the land they shape", "text": "A volcano is an opening in the crust of the Earth through which molten rock, ash and gas escape from below the surface. Deep underground, rock melts into a thick liquid called magma. As pressure builds, the magma forces its way upward through cracks in the crust. When it reaches the surface, it is called lava. It flows out in streams that cool and solidify into new rock. Some eruptions are quiet, with lava flowing slowly down the slopes. Others are devastating explosions that throw ash high into the sky. They send clouds of hot gas sweeping across the landscape. The shape of a volcano depends on what comes out of it. Runny lava builds wide, gentle slopes, while thick lava and ash build steep cones that rise steeply above the surrounding plains.\nSome volcanoes have influenced the course of history. In the year 79, Mount Vesuvius in Italy buried the Roman towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum under ash. The ash preserved streets, houses and everyday belongings for later generations to study. In 1883, the eruption of Krakatoa was heard thousands of kilometres away. The dust it threw into the atmosphere cooled the climate for years afterwards. India too carries the marks of ancient eruptions. The Deccan Traps, an extensive stretch of basalt rock across the west of the country, formed from ancient lava flows more than 65 million years ago. Today, scientists continually monitor active volcanoes with instruments that measure ground movement, gas and heat. Mount Etna in Italy, Mount Fuji in Japan and Mauna Loa are watched throughout the year, so that warnings can be given before an eruption begins.\nVolcanoes are not only a danger; they also bring benefits to the land around them. Ash gradually breaks down into rich soil, and farmers in volcanic regions cultivate coffee, tea and vegetables in fields that stay fertile for generations. In some countries, heat from underground is harnessed to produce electricity and to warm households. This clean source of energy is called geothermal power, and its development has grown steadily in recent decades. Yet living near a volcano needs care, and people who settle on its slopes must recognise the warning signs. They must also know the evacuation routes prepared by local authorities for their settlements. A volcano reminds us that the Earth is still changing beneath our feet. The same forces which destroy towns also create entirely new land, renew the soil and shape the landscapes on which human life depends."}]};
